WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.550
Hey, everybody, guess what?

2
00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:02.740
It's that time again.

3
00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.810
It's time to talk about addiction.

4
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.080
Specifically today,

5
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.290
substance abuse in teens and young people.

6
00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.630
It's a huge problem.

7
00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.730
Of course, it's a parent's worst fear.

8
00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:17.350
So how do we deal with this?

9
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.690
How can we identify a kid who is at risk?

10
00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:23.710
And what can be done to prevent our young ones

11
00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.490
from developing dependency issues?

12
00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:27.850
Well, to answer these questions,

13
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.120
I did what you do when you host a podcast.

14
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.460
I turn to the experts,

15
00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.410
people like today's guest, my friend, Jessica Lahey,

16
00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.070
returning for her second appearance on the show.

17
00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.280
Her first being episode 282,

18
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.490
which was almost exactly four years ago.

19
00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:46.780
Jess writes about education, parenting, and child welfare

20
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.550
for The Atlantic, The Washington Post,

21
00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.040
and The New York Times.

22
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.497
She's the author of the New York Times bestselling book

23
00:00:52.497 --> 00:00:54.130
"The Gift Of Failure."

24
00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:57.510
She co-hosts a popular podcast called AmWriting

25
00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.800
alongside another podcast alum, KJ Dell'Antonia,

26
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.790
that was episode 396.

27
00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.170
And hot off the press, her second book,

28
00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.660
and the primary focus of today's conversation,

29
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.527
is entitled "The Addiction Inoculation:

30
00:01:10.527 --> 00:01:14.730
"Raising Healthy Kids in a Culture of Dependence."

31
00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.040
This is the Galley copy.

32
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.760
The final edition looks a lot nicer,

33
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.390
but I think you get the idea.

34
00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:22.680
In any event, as a recovering alcoholic myself,

35
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.600
somebody who has done battle with the demons of addiction

36
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.730
as well as a dada for currently,

37
00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.710
delicately parenting to teens,

38
00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.510
this subject is of particular personal importance

39
00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:37.890
and I think a useful conversation, I suspect,

40
00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.020
will resonate with many of you as well.

41
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.170
So please hit that subscribe button, click like,

42
00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.490
leave your thoughts in the comment section below.

43
00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.050
And without further ado,

44
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:52.050
please enjoy my conversation with Jessica Lahey.

45
00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.340
There she is!

46
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:01.600
Wow.
Yeah, it goes--

47
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.050
Tell me about what's going on

48
00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:03.883
in the background there.

49
00:02:03.883 --> 00:02:05.580
That's unbelievable.

50
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.570
So my dad was a designer and he had a wall like this.

51
00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:14.400
He worked in a old straw hat factory that had been converted

52
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.670
and he had this 20-foot high wall

53
00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.250
and it was just covered with things he'd collected

54
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:20.500
all over the world.

55
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.310
And I have a picture of it

56
00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.520
'cause it was just one of my favorite things.

57
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.634
In fact, that's my dad right there.

58
00:02:26.634 --> 00:02:27.467
Aww.

59
00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:28.690
It's just one of those things that I just,

60
00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:29.523
I always loved it.

61
00:02:29.523 --> 00:02:32.270
And so I knew when I got this office

62
00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:35.110
that this wall was gonna be for that.

63
00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.950
Well, two observations.

64
00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:38.020
First--
Yes, sir.

65
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.380
You definitely win the Zoom background award.

66
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.888
This is the coolest.
Thank you.

67
00:02:42.888 --> 00:02:43.721
Thank you.

68
00:02:43.721 --> 00:02:44.786
(laughs)

69
00:02:44.786 --> 00:02:49.520
And that's important in this moment that we're in, right?

70
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:50.353
Yeah.
Curating.

71
00:02:50.353 --> 00:02:51.186
I just got really bored

72
00:02:51.186 --> 00:02:52.220
with all the book titles behind me.

73
00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.380
I was like, I could just, everybody does that.

74
00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.612
That's what I was expecting with you.

75
00:02:57.612 --> 00:02:58.445
(laughs)

76
00:02:58.445 --> 00:03:00.177
This has a very kind of Peter Beard feel to it.

77
00:03:00.177 --> 00:03:02.600
And I love just sort of the collecting the stuff

78
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:05.130
from around the world and having that be a part of

79
00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:06.510
who you are and the stuff you,

80
00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.200
and I don't carry a lot of stuff with me.

81
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.930
I'm not a big sentimental person.

82
00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.130
So the fact that these are things I still have

83
00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:13.230
is kind of a big deal.

84
00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:14.670
Mm-hmm, cool.

85
00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:15.990
Well, so nice to see you.

86
00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.840
Thank you for doing this.
Thank you.

87
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.001
I wish that we could do it--

88
00:03:19.001 --> 00:03:21.090 line:15%
Thank you for having me.
In person.

89
00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:21.923 line:15%
I know.

90
00:03:21.923 --> 00:03:24.253 line:15%
We did this at your dining room table.

91
00:03:25.521 --> 00:03:27.930 line:15%
Just two microphones and, yeah.

92
00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.233 line:15%
And some juice.

93
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.130
I think it's been, how many?

94
00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.570
It's been a couple years at this point.

95
00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.497
Five years, yeah.

96
00:03:35.497 --> 00:03:38.600
I'm only now getting over my resentment

97
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.450
that you cheated on me with Dax.

98
00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.225
But I'm in a good place with it.

99
00:03:43.225 --> 00:03:44.681
(laughs)

100
00:03:44.681 --> 00:03:46.229
Well, it was so sad is because

101
00:03:46.229 --> 00:03:49.180
those were like the last things I scheduled.

102
00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:51.160
Those are some of the first things that got canceled

103
00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:52.880
when the pandemic hit.
Right.

104
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.410
My next flight was supposed to go to Los Angeles

105
00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.750
to do Armchair Expert and hopefully see you

106
00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.060
and do a couple of other things.

107
00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.230
And that was one of the first things that got canceled.

108
00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.330
Yeah, yeah, and here we are,

109
00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.985
one year into this whole thing.

110
00:04:06.985 --> 00:04:08.230
I know, I know.

111
00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.540
But you've got this new book coming out.

112
00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:12.510
I'm super excited--
Thank you.

113
00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:13.780
To talk to you about it.

114
00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:18.780
It's such an important subject matter and conversation

115
00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:22.000
that needs to be had.

116
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:22.950
This is a book that

117
00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:26.640
I feel like should've been written decades ago.

118
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.860
I'm so glad that you've written it.

119
00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.140
And it's really potent and powerful

120
00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.020
and I think it's gonna help a lot of parents.

121
00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:34.853
It's funny.

122
00:04:34.853 --> 00:04:37.780
Before this book even became an idea in my head,

123
00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:40.620
this was stuff that I wanted to be able to talk to you about

124
00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.453
the first time 'round.

125
00:04:41.453 --> 00:04:44.670
And we talked for whatever it was, two hours and somethin',

126
00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.770
and only scratched the surface on the substance abuse stuff.

127
00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:52.260
But that second book thing is really scary.

128
00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.860
When you have a book, especially a book that's done well,

129
00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.220
I didn't want to just write like the obvious next thing

130
00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:03.640
or like "Gift of Failure Part Two" or whatever, the sequel.

131
00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:05.669
And it took a long time to figure out

132
00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:06.502
what I was gonna write about.

133
00:05:06.502 --> 00:05:08.330
And in the meantime, life was happening

134
00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:12.393
and lifelined me up with this topic.

135
00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.470
I love "Gift of Failure."

136
00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:19.680
I will always love "Gift of Failure,"

137
00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.860
but this is the book that I was meant to write.

138
00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:26.300
This is the book that life has brought me to this place.

139
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.610
And all the crappy stuff

140
00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.830
that I had to go through to get here,

141
00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.680
this is why it's worth it.

142
00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:36.250
It's interesting that you would comment

143
00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:39.543
that you weren't sure what you wanted to write about next.

144
00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.760
'Cause it's so obvious that this is the book

145
00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.473
that you were born to write, to me.

146
00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.174
It's funny.

147
00:05:49.174 --> 00:05:50.640
My agent is so supportive.

148
00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.500
And I would send her ideas and she'd be like,

149
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:54.260
hmm-mm, no, I love you, but no.

150
00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:55.715
This isn't it.

151
00:05:55.715 --> 00:05:56.737
And then I'd send her something else.

152
00:05:56.737 --> 00:05:58.840
And I was sort of circling the topic.

153
00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.840
And keep in mind, so I got sober

154
00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.470
when I sold "Gift of Failure."

155
00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:04.960
There was this big auction.

156
00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.300
It was like 14 editors.

157
00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.740
We had a big auction for it.

158
00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:13.150
And right after that is when I got sober,

159
00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:14.540
because I knew I couldn't write the book

160
00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.490
and continue to do what I was doing with my life.

161
00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:22.400
And so you're not really supposed to do much new stuff

162
00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:23.600
for about a year.

163
00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:25.720
And so I was dealing with "The Gift of Failure" stuff

164
00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:27.230
and trying to write that book.

165
00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:30.360
And then a year after "Gift of Failure" came out,

166
00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:31.810
and I had about a year sober,

167
00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.370
I went to go speak in a drug and,

168
00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:38.170
an adolescent drug and alcohol rehab, rehab for adolescents.

169
00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:41.470
And I went to go do like a service thing

170
00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:43.170
where you go and you do the speaking thing.

171
00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:44.920
And then I sort of realized at the end,

172
00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:46.500
I looked around and I'm like, wait a second,

173
00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.320
if you guys are here 24/7,

174
00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.550
there has to be some sort of education program.

175
00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.090
And I had just left my full-time teaching job.

176
00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:57.533
So next thing I knew, I was working,

177
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.830
I was getting drug tested and I was working

178
00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:02.710
in a drug and alcohol rehab for adolescents

179
00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:03.543
as their writing teacher.

180
00:07:03.543 --> 00:07:05.070
And I did that for five years.

181
00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:07.730
And it was the best teaching job ever.

182
00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:09.600
And I don't think I could've written this book

183
00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.483
without having done that work too.

184
00:07:12.422 --> 00:07:14.680
So it was a combination that everything had to align

185
00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:16.330
in order for this book to happen.

186
00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:20.690
They ultimately phased out the adolescent component

187
00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.950
of that facility, right?

188
00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:23.783
Yeah.

189
00:07:23.783 --> 00:07:26.080
If you have a kid now in Vermont

190
00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:31.080
and you want inpatient treatment, it doesn't exist.

191
00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:32.620
Although--
Right.

192
00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:35.600
I heard the best news just the other day.

193
00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.830
Lead singer of Phish, founding member of Phish,

194
00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:42.498
and I'm not a huge Phish fan even

195
00:07:42.498 --> 00:07:43.838
though I live in Vermont--
Trent, Trent, Trent--

196
00:07:43.838 --> 00:07:44.980
Yes, thank you.
Trent Astanasio.

197
00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:46.490
How do you say his last name?

198
00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:48.650
I do not know, but he's opening.

199
00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:50.610
I just saw in our local paper the other day

200
00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:53.496
that he's gonna open a rehab in Vermont.

201
00:07:53.496 --> 00:07:54.329
Oh, wow.

202
00:07:54.329 --> 00:07:55.770
He had gone to University of Vermont.

203
00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:57.530
I live just south of Burlington.

204
00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:00.790
And my husband is affiliated with the University of Vermont

205
00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:02.260
so I could not be more excited

206
00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.350
because my secret hope someday

207
00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:09.680
is to open a recovery high school for adolescents

208
00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:10.840
in the Burlington area.

209
00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:14.100
So I'm thrilled.
Wow, that would be cool.

210
00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:18.610
That's weirdly ironic because the whole Phish thing

211
00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:21.328
is about just smoking tons of dope, right?

212
00:08:21.328 --> 00:08:22.161
(laughs)

213
00:08:22.161 --> 00:08:25.960
Yeah, I'm really excited to see what he does.

214
00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:27.730
It just hit the news.

215
00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:30.220
Just hit our local news a couple days ago.

216
00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:33.920
I've been thinking a lot lately about the current state

217
00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:37.940
of the greater treatment industrial complex

218
00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:40.390
because I just watched this movie called "Body Brokers."

219
00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:41.790
Have you seen this film yet?

220
00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:42.710
No, I haven't yet.

221
00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:44.210
And it deals with--

222
00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:45.610
I've been underwater, yeah.

223
00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:46.546
I wanna see it, though.

224
00:08:46.546 --> 00:08:47.740
I mean, it's a narrative feature,

225
00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:51.540
but the writer/director is a guy who was in,

226
00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.800
in and out of, I think, a few treatment centers,

227
00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.213
and it deals with the insurance fraud component of it

228
00:08:58.213 --> 00:09:01.110
and the kind of abuses that are occurring,

229
00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:04.440
which from, and this is relevant

230
00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:05.670
to what we're gonna talk about,

231
00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:08.100
creates confusion and trepidation

232
00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:09.430
when faced with the prospect

233
00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:12.550
of whether I should put my kid in a treatment center.

234
00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:14.960
Is this only gonna be, and you talk about this in the book,

235
00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:16.800
is this only gonna be a breeding ground

236
00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.800
for unhealthy behavior because of the kind of community

237
00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:24.853
that you're suddenly introducing your child to.

238
00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.530
It's so hard to know what,

239
00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:32.190
I mean, right now, so many people are making money

240
00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:34.170
off of treating people, which is,

241
00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:36.810
you have to be able to make money somehow.

242
00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.522
And yeah, and then there are, especially with kids,

243
00:09:39.522 --> 00:09:40.830
there's so many parents just desperate

244
00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:41.880
for anyone to help them.

245
00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.170
And even if you can find a quality place to send your kid,

246
00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.763
it's just so hard to get a kid in.

247
00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:50.870
It's just a really bad spot right now.

248
00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:53.810
The thing for me that I think it's important to remember

249
00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:56.963
is that the new book, "The Addiction Inoculation"

250
00:09:58.193 --> 00:09:59.950
is just about the prevention side.

251
00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:03.790
I think one of the problems

252
00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:06.510
is that there are so many people in recovery

253
00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:09.410
who want to help other people who need to get better

254
00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:10.550
and wanna get better.

255
00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:13.610
And so the draw is for people in recovery

256
00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:15.930
to go become counselors themselves.

257
00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:16.800
And I think that's great.

258
00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:17.810
I think that's fantastic.

259
00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:19.760
But just having gone through recovery

260
00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:22.160
does not make me an expert in treatment.

261
00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.440
What I am an expert in now is prevention.

262
00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:27.390
And I'm not an expert in treatment.

263
00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:29.790
So when it comes to that stuff,

264
00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:32.210
I will defer to the experts.

265
00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:34.330
I have only my own experience.

266
00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:37.870
I know I have an academic understanding of treatment

267
00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:41.990
and that sort of stuff, but my wheelhouse is prevention.

268
00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:42.823
Right.

269
00:10:42.823 --> 00:10:46.640
And the book certainly uses that as a focus,

270
00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:51.030
but interlineated throughout are a couple narratives.

271
00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:56.030
Most potent in my mind, we have this girl Georgia

272
00:10:56.380 --> 00:11:00.410
and this boy Brian and you kind of tell their stories

273
00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:02.057
throughout the book.

274
00:11:02.057 --> 00:11:04.760
And what you kind of gleaned from that

275
00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:09.390
is how messy and tricky and non-linear this whole thing is,

276
00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:12.330
like, once the child is addicted

277
00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.113
or the young person is addicted,

278
00:11:16.946 --> 00:11:20.100
and you admit to your own kind of inadequacies

279
00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:21.640
even as being a sober person

280
00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:25.740
with how challenging it becomes to try to guide that person

281
00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:29.330
or figure out how to get them towards the solution.

282
00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:30.960
Well, and the other big picture there,

283
00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:34.770
is how unclear the research is.

284
00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:37.020
At least even for the things that we think we know.

285
00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:41.390
I mean, the entire chapter about Brian is about peers,

286
00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:42.690
kids and their peer groups.

287
00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:44.900
And the research on that,

288
00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:46.720
if you were to ask someone for the short answer,

289
00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.540
is what is the research on peer cohorts?

290
00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:52.230
It would say, if your kid hangs out with kids who do drugs,

291
00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:54.000
they will be more likely to do drugs.

292
00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.823
And that just sounded a little too simple for me.

293
00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:03.670
Number one, I love my job, and I'm a big research dork,

294
00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:05.210
and I get to read and read and read

295
00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:06.800
and translate all that stuff.

296
00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:08.770
I'm also married to a scientist.

297
00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:10.600
I'm married to a statistician

298
00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:12.210
and a physician and an ethicist.

299
00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:15.960
And so anytime I say, this just doesn't sound

300
00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:16.793
clear-cut to me.

301
00:12:16.793 --> 00:12:17.720
Let's dig through the research

302
00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:19.243
and let's find out what the good studies are,

303
00:12:19.243 --> 00:12:21.260
what the bad studies are and blah, blah, blah.

304
00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:23.970
So it turns out that not only,

305
00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:26.970
there are so many camps in the recovery world

306
00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:28.470
and in the substance abuse world

307
00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:32.380
and I wanted to try to go into this free of a camp

308
00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:34.993
and try to look at all the research and figure out,

309
00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:39.640
if Brian's story is that he had drug issues,

310
00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:41.140
then the last thing I should have done

311
00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:42.903
was let my son be his friend.

312
00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:45.630
But it wasn't that simple.

313
00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:50.450
So what do we do with a world that isn't very simple

314
00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:53.840
and very rarely cut and dry or black and white,

315
00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:54.790
that kind of thing.

316
00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:58.050
And also, the big story for me with this book is

317
00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:04.130
my passion is to learn stuff, learn from it,

318
00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:05.570
and become a better person.

319
00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:09.900
And so the last thing I wanted to do is make parents feel

320
00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:13.343
any kind of shame or bad or regret.

321
00:13:13.343 --> 00:13:16.960
The goal with this book is to be as empowering as possible.

322
00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:19.160
Yeah, you succeeded in that regard.

323
00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:20.210
But yeah, it's hard.

324
00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:22.380
It's so complicated and tricky.

325
00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:25.910
And I'm going to resist every impulse in my body

326
00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:29.661
to not make this a personal counseling session.

327
00:13:29.661 --> 00:13:30.970
(laughs)

328
00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:34.360
I've got two teen daughters and--

329
00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:35.910
My kids' in there.

330
00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:38.060
Yeah, I know, yeah.

331
00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:40.787
My daughters do not wanna be part of the podcast, though,

332
00:13:40.787 --> 00:13:42.070
and I respect that.

333
00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:44.250
So we're not gonna do that specifically,

334
00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.390
but there's so many kind of universalities

335
00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:47.680
that we can extract from this.

336
00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:51.750
But because we didn't really talk about sobriety

337
00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:53.600
and addiction in our last podcast,

338
00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:55.430
which was focused on "The Gift of Failure"

339
00:13:55.430 --> 00:14:00.360
and just learning and, young people and learning,

340
00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:03.550
why don't we begin with your story,

341
00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:04.810
which is how you open the book,

342
00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:08.350
'cause I think it beautifully contextualizes all of this.

343
00:14:08.350 --> 00:14:09.183
Thank you.

344
00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:12.890
Oh, it's funny, asking.

345
00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:15.560
I did not expect this book to be a memoir, by the way.

346
00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:17.683
It just became that.

347
00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:24.220
So I was raised in a family with an alcoholic parent

348
00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:29.400
and lots of other alcoholics and drug addicts

349
00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.820
sort of just sprinkled throughout the family tree,

350
00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:33.700
all over the place.

351
00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:35.540
And I don't think there are that many people out there

352
00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:37.500
that don't have at least one out there somewhere.

353
00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:39.950
But I happen to have, I think, more than average.

354
00:14:41.824 --> 00:14:46.500
And what's funny is I think it's taken me a really long time

355
00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:49.420
to untangle all of what it meant to be raised

356
00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:52.380
by someone with a substance abuse problem.

357
00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:56.370
And despite the fact that I really do think

358
00:14:56.370 --> 00:14:58.110
I had a fairly idyllic childhood,

359
00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:00.780
I also knew that the one thing I didn't want to be

360
00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:02.700
was a person who drank too much.

361
00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:06.040
And so I really went all the way over

362
00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:08.200
to the abstinent end of the spectrum.

363
00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:11.140
I was such a dorky teenager.

364
00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:13.660
I was like the designated driver.

365
00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:16.500
I became a peer drug and alcohol counselor in college.

366
00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:19.163
I was like the uncoolest person in college.

367
00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:25.350
I talk in the book that scene about me standing there

368
00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:28.490
in like beer soaked living room in a frat house

369
00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:32.393
lecturing about how alcohol is broken down to,

370
00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:35.730
I was horrifying.

371
00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:37.580
And I also was a resident assistant,

372
00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:41.580
so I was like the narc on the floor in a dorm that was very,

373
00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:43.300
had a very big drug culture.

374
00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:47.190
So I just didn't really drink

375
00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:50.240
or do any drugs or anything like that through college.

376
00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:53.060
And then I really didn't have a problem

377
00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:55.800
with drugs or alcohol until I was in my 40s.

378
00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:57.960
And it just snuck up on me.

379
00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:00.610
And I get that my story is a little different

380
00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:01.710
from a lot of people.

381
00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:03.990
There wasn't that, like, I had that first drink

382
00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:07.580
and I suddenly knew what was missing from my life.

383
00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:09.830
For me, it was of slow build.

384
00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:11.910
And I knew for a really long time

385
00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:14.760
that it was building and building and going nowhere good.

386
00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:17.810
My dad actually was the one who called me

387
00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:21.110
out on my drinking, which was a really big deal.

388
00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:24.660
And I'm so grateful to him, so grateful to him.

389
00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:26.430
Well, I think it's an interesting story.

390
00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:28.840
I mean, first of all, when I think of you in college

391
00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:32.412
being the narc and all of that and being the goody-goody,

392
00:16:32.412 --> 00:16:35.970
I have to suspect on some level that was a fear response

393
00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:37.300
to your family history.

394
00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:41.410
Like, I am gonna will my way into not being that person.

395
00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:44.420
And alcoholism just doesn't really work that way.

396
00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:49.420
And when opportunity met whatever situation you were in,

397
00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:51.330
it took hold.

398
00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:56.330
And because it's not a super dramatic bottom,

399
00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:02.960
it's a story less told in the kind of annals of alcoholism,

400
00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:04.880
but I think it's incredibly common.

401
00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:09.880
Like, the extent to which you went to hide your drinking

402
00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:14.750
and all of that stuff that's indelibly alcoholic

403
00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:18.010
is relatable to anybody who's in recovery.

404
00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:20.660
And yet the fact that when you got sober,

405
00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:25.437
your concern was that your friends would be surprised

406
00:17:25.437 --> 00:17:28.130
or or thinking, well, you don't really have a problem

407
00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:30.490
because you were so good at masking this for so long.

408
00:17:30.490 --> 00:17:31.323
Oh, it wasn't my fear.

409
00:17:31.323 --> 00:17:32.370
It what happened.

410
00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:36.240
I mean, I went to my first meeting

411
00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:38.100
and had to sort of come out to my friends

412
00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:41.390
and I had to talk them into it.

413
00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:46.390
I mean, one friend, I really had to talk her into it.

414
00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:48.420
And it was a weird thing.

415
00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:50.230
I was so good at hiding it.

416
00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:51.317
I was so good at hiding it.

417
00:17:51.317 --> 00:17:54.310
And in fact, when my husband first read

418
00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:57.610
the Galley of the new book, of "The Addiction Inoculation,"

419
00:17:57.610 --> 00:18:00.350
I don't let people read stuff until it's sort of done, done.

420
00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:03.630
And there was so much news to him, stuff in there.

421
00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:05.860
And it was hard for him to digest,

422
00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:07.110
reading that first chapter.

423
00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:09.130
And then I had to send it to my parents.

424
00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:10.860
And that's a whole other.

425
00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:14.180
So many people say, I'm just so glad you're honest.

426
00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:17.090
And I'm perfectly fine being honest

427
00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:18.450
and forthright and out there,

428
00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:20.880
but I also don't wanna hurt the people around me

429
00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:22.110
that I love.
Sure.

430
00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:24.010
And those are their stories to tell.

431
00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:26.673
But as you know, the more we talk about it,

432
00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:31.390
I think, the easier other people find it to talk about.

433
00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:32.928
Yeah, I mean, tell me about it.

434
00:18:32.928 --> 00:18:35.140
When my parents read "Finding Ultra,"

435
00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:37.048
that was very terrifying.

436
00:18:37.048 --> 00:18:38.070
(laughs)

437
00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:40.233
They had lots of opinions, I can tell you.

438
00:18:41.898 --> 00:18:43.700
So I understand that.

439
00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:45.270
But it takes what it takes.

440
00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:48.430
And you hit your bottom and you reckoned with the demon

441
00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:49.730
and you came out the other side.

442
00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:50.810
So you've got like, what,

443
00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:52.935
maybe eight years now at this point?

444
00:18:52.935 --> 00:18:54.890
Or coming up on eight?

445
00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:56.110
Almost eight years, yeah.

446
00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:58.580
Yeah, good for you, that's cool.

447
00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:01.081
My sobriety date is my mother's birthday.

448
00:19:01.081 --> 00:19:01.992
Wow.

449
00:19:01.992 --> 00:19:05.030
Because I got so hammered at her birthday party.

450
00:19:05.030 --> 00:19:09.826
That was the night, yeah, I shit at the fan, yeah.

451
00:19:09.826 --> 00:19:11.075
(laughs)

452
00:19:11.075 --> 00:19:12.430
And then having to--
I don't remember it.

453
00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:14.000
I don't remember it.

454
00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:14.833
Right.

455
00:19:14.833 --> 00:19:16.210
I hear it was really bad,

456
00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:19.310
but I'm kinda grateful I don't remember.

457
00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:20.387
I don't know.

458
00:19:20.387 --> 00:19:21.380
I don't know if I'm grateful I don't remember it

459
00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:23.120
or the, whatever.

460
00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:25.802
I like that it was your dad, though, that kind of--

461
00:19:25.802 --> 00:19:26.874
Yeah, I do too.

462
00:19:26.874 --> 00:19:27.720
Knocked on the door and brought you in,

463
00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:28.660
which is pretty cool.
I do too.

464
00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:29.900
And then having to contend

465
00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:32.520
with "The Gift of Failure" coming out

466
00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:35.830
and all the hullabaloo around that in early sobriety,

467
00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:39.300
I suspect, would've been relatively overwhelming.

468
00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:41.823
Actually it was even worse than that.

469
00:19:41.823 --> 00:19:43.190
I don't know if we had this conversation,

470
00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:45.790
but the day after I hand it in

471
00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:48.670
the first draft of my manuscript for "The Gift of Failure,"

472
00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:50.600
I went on a, I had been training,

473
00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:51.880
a friend of mine has a horse farm

474
00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:54.340
and I had been training a horse with her,

475
00:19:54.340 --> 00:19:55.660
and I was just out.

476
00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:56.540
My husband went with me.

477
00:19:56.540 --> 00:19:57.770
He never rides with me.

478
00:19:57.770 --> 00:19:59.830
He doesn't really know how to ride.

479
00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:02.680
We were just out on a trail thing and the horse threw me

480
00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:03.870
and I fell on my head.

481
00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:06.220
And I lost my memory, I lost my ability

482
00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:07.750
to read and write for a while.

483
00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:11.750
And then while I was still in that post-concussive syndrome

484
00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:15.570
with depression and seeing, my vision problems

485
00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:19.410
and reading problems, I was teaching at,

486
00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:20.330
I mean, it was just horrible.

487
00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:23.660
And then my editor came back to me

488
00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:25.300
on the first draft of "Gift of Failure,"

489
00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:26.540
did I tell you this story?

490
00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:27.656
I don't think, though.

491
00:20:27.656 --> 00:20:29.760
That it was quote, unquote, that,

492
00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:31.590
oh, yeah, because I didn't reveal the story

493
00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:33.520
'till after we talked 'cause it was so humiliating.

494
00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:35.120
I never talked about it.

495
00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:36.590
That the first draft of "The Gift of Failure"

496
00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:38.580
was, quote, unpublishable.

497
00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:42.350
And the words ghost came out, that maybe I needed a ghost,

498
00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:45.260
which for me was a ghostwriter which is so humiliating

499
00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:47.580
because I, at the time, was writing a column

500
00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:48.680
for The New York Times,

501
00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:50.670
I write for The Atlantic for years.

502
00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:55.670
And so instead, I said, I tell you what,

503
00:20:56.624 --> 00:20:59.660
can I have these probationary chapters?

504
00:20:59.660 --> 00:21:01.010
Just let me have two chapters to,

505
00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:04.820
you lay it on me, tell me how bad my book is

506
00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:07.250
and what I did wrong and what I can do better.

507
00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:08.083
And she did.

508
00:21:08.083 --> 00:21:09.900
And those two chapters turned into the rest of the book.

509
00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:13.910
So all of that, I was just newly sober,

510
00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:17.320
I had a post-concussion syndrome with depression,

511
00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:19.850
it was just (retches), it was horrible.

512
00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:20.920
It was really, really bad.

513
00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:21.881
But--

514
00:21:21.881 --> 00:21:23.581
Yeah, being humble to your core.

515
00:21:24.629 --> 00:21:26.420
The host of AmWriting.
I mean, I learned a lot.

516
00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:27.750
The New York Times writer

517
00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:29.830
being told they need a ghostwriter, wow.

518
00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:31.960
But imagine if I had been drinking

519
00:21:31.960 --> 00:21:34.413
when I was in that post-concussive syndrome.

520
00:21:35.605 --> 00:21:37.490
There've been a few times that I've been so grateful

521
00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:38.540
that I'm not drinking anymore.

522
00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:39.373
That was one.

523
00:21:39.373 --> 00:21:41.223
The pandemic has been the other one.

524
00:21:42.372 --> 00:21:43.920
I'm just, ugh,

525
00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:45.600
I can't even imagine what that would've been like

526
00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:46.800
if I was still drinking.

527
00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:50.090
Well, speaking of the pandemic

528
00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:54.566
and kind of transitioning into young people,

529
00:21:54.566 --> 00:21:57.460
what is your kind of sense or take

530
00:21:57.460 --> 00:22:02.460
on how this past year has impacted young people

531
00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:05.810
perhaps differently than older people?

532
00:22:05.810 --> 00:22:10.810
And then how is that related to addiction

533
00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:12.363
and substance abuse?

534
00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:16.350
So one of the big things that the pandemic has done

535
00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:18.810
for all of us, not just kids, but all of us,

536
00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:20.733
is take away our sense of control.

537
00:22:22.864 --> 00:22:27.020
And for kids then, as out of control as I feel as a parent,

538
00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:29.860
imagine how out of control my kid feels,

539
00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:31.923
can barely leave the house.

540
00:22:33.530 --> 00:22:37.270
Kids have so little autonomy in their lives to begin with.

541
00:22:37.270 --> 00:22:39.170
And now all of a sudden, not only

542
00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:41.130
college kids are having to stay home,

543
00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:43.810
kids are having to do school from home,

544
00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:46.780
some kids have very, very few spaces to call their own

545
00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:48.120
and have any autonomy over,

546
00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:50.983
and suddenly all of that is closing in and closing in.

547
00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:55.600
My kid, I have a 17-year-old and he's going,

548
00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:57.880
I'm trying to imagine what it would've been like for me

549
00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:01.043
at 16 and 17 to barely leave the house for,

550
00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:03.893
no dating, no leaving the house.

551
00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:07.270
So taking away kids' control

552
00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:08.630
does a couple of different things.

553
00:23:08.630 --> 00:23:11.450
Obviously, we're seeing really high levels

554
00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:13.053
of depression and anxiety.

555
00:23:14.570 --> 00:23:16.270
I work with a bunch of clinicians.

556
00:23:17.950 --> 00:23:19.080
When the pandemic first hit,

557
00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:22.300
a bunch of us sort of parenting educators and writers

558
00:23:22.300 --> 00:23:23.860
got together and created this thing called

559
00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:25.770
the Parenting in Place Master Class.

560
00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:29.309
And we do these sessions and,

561
00:23:29.309 --> 00:23:32.190
I'm doing a session actually with a mutual friend of ours,

562
00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:33.290
Julie Lythcott-Haimes.

563
00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:36.280
She and I have a session coming up on older kids

564
00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:38.920
and getting them out of the nest and off to college.

565
00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:43.500
And the parents there and the clinicians there

566
00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:46.170
are talking about just incredible of depression,

567
00:23:46.170 --> 00:23:50.554
lots of anxiety, tons of suicidal ideation.

568
00:23:50.554 --> 00:23:53.150
I have friends there, in this group,

569
00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:55.090
who are not just clinicians,

570
00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:57.930
but like work in schools with kids all the time

571
00:23:57.930 --> 00:24:00.920
and they're like, I don't know what to do

572
00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:04.930
with all of this sadness and despair and helplessness.

573
00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:07.870
And that's what taking control away does to us.

574
00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:10.130
It creates this learned helplessness,

575
00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:14.190
which is, there's some great research from Martin Seligman

576
00:24:14.190 --> 00:24:15.460
at the University of Pennsylvania

577
00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:20.460
about the fact that when exposed to long-term helplessness,

578
00:24:20.670 --> 00:24:25.670
frustration, pain, our sort of default response as humans

579
00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:28.610
is to go helpless and just sort of

580
00:24:28.610 --> 00:24:29.950
curl up in the fetal position.

581
00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:34.520
And the way we undo that, the way we interrupt that circuit

582
00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:36.770
is by giving control back.

583
00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:41.510
And all of these kids have not only had the normal amount

584
00:24:41.510 --> 00:24:44.070
of sort of parents in their stuff all the time

585
00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:46.800
suddenly just their ability to do just about anything

586
00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:48.300
and have control over just about everything

587
00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:49.220
has been taken away

588
00:24:49.220 --> 00:24:51.710
and learned helplessness has gone through the roof.

589
00:24:51.710 --> 00:24:52.950
Some people are talking about it

590
00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:54.600
in terms of, like, regressing,

591
00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:57.670
which isn't entirely appropriate, but close.

592
00:24:57.670 --> 00:25:00.530
I mean, lots of kids are sort of reading books

593
00:25:00.530 --> 00:25:02.440
they used to read when they were 12,

594
00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:03.610
but haven't looked at in a long time

595
00:25:03.610 --> 00:25:06.600
as a way of giving themselves comfort and stuff like that.

596
00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:08.180
My kid went through a period

597
00:25:08.180 --> 00:25:10.340
where he was watching movies he'd loved

598
00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:13.483
as like an elementary, like the animated stuff

599
00:25:13.483 --> 00:25:16.080
that he loved as an elementary kid and, yeah.

600
00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:20.363
So I think that depression, that anxiety,

601
00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:26.290
those feelings of hopelessness, all of those feelings are,

602
00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:30.450
really put kids at risk for substance abuse, number one,

603
00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:33.270
but obviously mental health issues at the same time.

604
00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:35.510
And it's incredibly hard to get help right now.

605
00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:37.940
It's incredibly hard to get help right now

606
00:25:37.940 --> 00:25:39.780
from a mental health perspective.

607
00:25:39.780 --> 00:25:41.233
Everyone's overbooked.

608
00:25:42.583 --> 00:25:45.250
A kid has to do stuff by Zoom, which is not the same thing

609
00:25:45.250 --> 00:25:46.950
as being in the room with a therapist.

610
00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:47.950
It's hard.

611
00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:49.470
It's hard for everybody.

612
00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:52.950
Yeah, I mean, we're experiencing that in our house.

613
00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:57.140
Anxiety, depression, the loss of agency,

614
00:25:57.140 --> 00:25:59.440
the powerlessness, the frustration

615
00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:01.210
that gets baked into that.

616
00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:06.170
And all of those contribute to this mental soup

617
00:26:06.170 --> 00:26:11.170
that primes a young person to be susceptible to substances

618
00:26:11.350 --> 00:26:12.600
in a pretty profound way.

619
00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:16.010
Like, we have one, here I am talking about my family,

620
00:26:16.010 --> 00:26:18.910
but I have one daughter who's very extroverted--

621
00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:21.106
Cloaking is much your area.

622
00:26:21.106 --> 00:26:23.020
Yeah, one very extroverted child

623
00:26:23.020 --> 00:26:24.910
and one very introverted child.

624
00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:28.310
And noticing how they are navigating this

625
00:26:28.310 --> 00:26:31.552
in very different ways has been fascinating as well.

626
00:26:31.552 --> 00:26:33.071
Yeah, I have the same situation.

627
00:26:33.071 --> 00:26:34.410
One is internalizing everything

628
00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:37.420
and it's very difficult to have,

629
00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:41.180
to get this child to communicate about it,

630
00:26:41.180 --> 00:26:43.300
whereas the other one is just a hurricane.

631
00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:45.170
Like, you never know what you're gonna get.

632
00:26:45.170 --> 00:26:48.930
And it's changing from hour to hour.

633
00:26:48.930 --> 00:26:51.080
Yeah, and actually my introvert

634
00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:55.000
has actually liked a lot of the aspects of being home more

635
00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:56.190
and being in his cave.

636
00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:58.533
He went nocturnal for a while too, I think.

637
00:26:59.433 --> 00:27:01.990
And having all of those relationships be online

638
00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:03.200
and not having to go to school,

639
00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:05.090
that was working really well for a while.

640
00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:07.240
But even he, I think, is starting to hit a point

641
00:27:07.240 --> 00:27:09.980
where he's like, okay, it's been a year now.

642
00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:10.813
I'm 17.

643
00:27:13.491 --> 00:27:16.590
And it really has affected kids differently.

644
00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:18.263
Some kids are thriving, honestly.

645
00:27:19.192 --> 00:27:22.130
I talk to parents all the time whose kids are thriving

646
00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:24.520
because they've had independent projects.

647
00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:26.560
So homeschool is working for them.

648
00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:29.970
Hybrid is what my kids are doing and that's working well.

649
00:27:29.970 --> 00:27:32.620
But in general, it's been really hard to watch kids

650
00:27:32.620 --> 00:27:35.920
have to struggle through this often by themselves.

651
00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:38.960
And what is your sense as an educator

652
00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:41.890
in terms of how learning is happening

653
00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:44.463
when now it's all digital and on Zoom?

654
00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:49.650
Yeah, so the secret sauce of teaching is engagement,

655
00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:53.150
relevance, connection, interpersonal connection,

656
00:27:53.150 --> 00:27:55.160
connecting the material you're learning

657
00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:56.580
to something you care about.

658
00:27:56.580 --> 00:27:59.060
We know that learning does not happen

659
00:27:59.060 --> 00:28:01.470
when there's not an emotion tied to it.

660
00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:05.660
So emotion is what allows really deep learning to happen.

661
00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:08.690
And when you feel disconnected,

662
00:28:08.690 --> 00:28:10.680
not just from the people you're learning from,

663
00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:13.400
but from, okay, well, sure,

664
00:28:13.400 --> 00:28:15.590
I am understanding that this is geometry,

665
00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:18.410
but how does that relate to anything beyond the classroom?

666
00:28:18.410 --> 00:28:21.480
So the really best teachers not only create

667
00:28:21.480 --> 00:28:23.550
an emotional connection with their students,

668
00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:26.200
they help connect the material

669
00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:27.830
to something the kid cares about.

670
00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:29.090
They create relevance.

671
00:28:29.090 --> 00:28:30.810
So they have a sense for,

672
00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:32.490
oh, this kid really likes astronomy.

673
00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:34.270
Let me explain to him how geometry

674
00:28:34.270 --> 00:28:35.460
and astronomy work together.

675
00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:37.850
And oh, this kid over here, she likes to sew.

676
00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:39.840
So let me explain how seam allowances,

677
00:28:39.840 --> 00:28:42.950
how we use fractions to work with seam that kind of thing.

678
00:28:42.950 --> 00:28:44.480
So that relevance is important.

679
00:28:44.480 --> 00:28:47.200
But then really what it comes down to with engagement

680
00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:52.200
is that emotional interest attachment stimulation

681
00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:54.870
for whatever it is we're teaching

682
00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:56.020
or the people that are teaching it.

683
00:28:56.020 --> 00:28:57.963
And when this disconnect has happened,

684
00:28:59.290 --> 00:29:03.020
at the end of the first season of the pandemic,

685
00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:05.840
at least the kids already knew their teachers.

686
00:29:05.840 --> 00:29:07.170
They'd been in their classrooms

687
00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:09.060
with their teachers that year.

688
00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:10.730
Then looking at the fall, which,

689
00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:12.560
and by the way, I just have to say this

690
00:29:12.560 --> 00:29:13.793
for teachers out there.

691
00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:18.350
Online teaching, virtual teaching, distance learning

692
00:29:18.350 --> 00:29:20.730
that is a skill that you have to learn.

693
00:29:20.730 --> 00:29:22.910
It's not like something that just,

694
00:29:22.910 --> 00:29:26.650
classroom teaching does not just translate to online

695
00:29:26.650 --> 00:29:31.050
especially because the best kinds of teaching

696
00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:34.040
don't work with me as a talking head,

697
00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:36.280
just sort of (blabbers), that's lecturing

698
00:29:36.280 --> 00:29:38.110
and lecturing is actually a really

699
00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:40.110
poor vehicle for learning.

700
00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:43.130
So the teachers that were doing sort of the best work

701
00:29:43.130 --> 00:29:45.000
had to try to figure out how to adapt

702
00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:47.830
whether it's project-based learning or independent inquiry

703
00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:51.530
or, all of that stuff is really hard to do

704
00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:53.750
in this weird talking head format.

705
00:29:53.750 --> 00:29:56.570
And you've probably seen all the articles.

706
00:29:56.570 --> 00:29:59.910
Here's why virtual learning or here's why Zoom calls

707
00:29:59.910 --> 00:30:01.870
are so emotionally exhausting for us.

708
00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:05.010
Because we're looking for social cues that aren't there.

709
00:30:05.010 --> 00:30:06.720
I do a lot of speaking engagements

710
00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:10.960
where I'm looking at either my own face or a blank screen.

711
00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:13.930
And I'm like, these jokes landing?

712
00:30:13.930 --> 00:30:15.243
Right.
I don't know.

713
00:30:16.720 --> 00:30:21.070
Yeah, and when everyone's just a box on a screen,

714
00:30:21.070 --> 00:30:24.960
how is the teacher supposed to really get into

715
00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:26.510
what's gonna motivate that child?

716
00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:28.780
It just becomes infinitely more difficult.

717
00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:32.890
And kids are so astute at figuring out how to do

718
00:30:32.890 --> 00:30:34.250
and runs around the rules.

719
00:30:34.250 --> 00:30:36.120
Like, they'll log on and check in

720
00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:38.063
and then they turn the camera off,

721
00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:40.170
who knows what's going on.

722
00:30:40.170 --> 00:30:43.400
I mean, I've seen it all and it's not--

723
00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:44.530
Well, but think about some of the kids

724
00:30:44.530 --> 00:30:48.990
for whom having the camera off as a legit concern.

725
00:30:48.990 --> 00:30:52.400
Like, let's say I'm a kid who,

726
00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:54.410
the women in our family have to wear headscarves

727
00:30:54.410 --> 00:30:57.700
in the presence of men that are not of the same family.

728
00:30:57.700 --> 00:31:01.330
So that now means if I'm online in my kitchen,

729
00:31:01.330 --> 00:31:02.700
because that's the only common space

730
00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:04.390
we have in our apartment,

731
00:31:04.390 --> 00:31:07.560
and that now means my mom has to be in her headscarf.

732
00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:09.870
Or what if I'm a kid who lives in poverty?

733
00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:12.940
What if I'm a kid who lives in a shelter?

734
00:31:12.940 --> 00:31:14.210
I don't wanna have the camera on.

735
00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:17.490
I don't want people to see what's going on in my house.

736
00:31:17.490 --> 00:31:20.270
There are very real reasons why some kids

737
00:31:20.270 --> 00:31:22.810
can't engage in the same way.

738
00:31:22.810 --> 00:31:25.830
And PS, some teachers are still penalizing kids

739
00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:28.680
for not having their cameras on or that kind of thing.

740
00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:32.330
So I think teachers, all of a sudden, it's like, hold on,

741
00:31:32.330 --> 00:31:35.050
I'm now a guest in your home.

742
00:31:35.050 --> 00:31:36.740
It's not like you're coming to my classroom.

743
00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:38.600
I'm now a guest in your home.

744
00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:41.650
What new considerations do I need to consider for that?

745
00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:43.627
I've never even done that before.

746
00:31:43.627 --> 00:31:46.540
Right.
It's a whole new thing that,

747
00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:48.970
I'm just amazed that it's gone off

748
00:31:48.970 --> 00:31:50.543
as well as it has, frankly.

749
00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:55.430
Well, it'll be interesting maybe a decade from now

750
00:31:55.430 --> 00:32:00.430
to really do some studies on the long-term impact of this.

751
00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:02.300
It's gonna take a while, yeah.

752
00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:04.930
And I say the same thing for substance abuse this year, too.

753
00:32:04.930 --> 00:32:08.460
I mean, we have groups doing surveys every year

754
00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:12.530
of sort of like the Monitoring the Future survey.

755
00:32:12.530 --> 00:32:17.530
They look at kids' attitudes around drugs and alcohol

756
00:32:18.290 --> 00:32:20.090
and their habits and stuff like that.

757
00:32:20.090 --> 00:32:22.850
But I really think it is gonna take a couple of years

758
00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:24.320
for us to see the full impact

759
00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:26.653
and get a clear picture of what's happening.

760
