Like, we tend to think of those things,

1042
00:45:13.130 --> 00:45:17.530
like, oh, like an acrimonious divorce

1043
00:45:17.530 --> 00:45:22.220
or physical or tremendous emotional abuse in the household,

1044
00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:27.057
but it can be triggered by something less sort of severe

1045
00:45:29.510 --> 00:45:31.160
like some mild bullying.

1046
00:45:31.160 --> 00:45:35.040
But that child's experience of that is more traumatic

1047
00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:37.230
than appears on the surface.

1048
00:45:37.230 --> 00:45:42.230
So then does it not just become an issue of communication,

1049
00:45:44.010 --> 00:45:47.120
like how to figure out how to crack the code

1050
00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:49.970
with your child so that you can open that channel

1051
00:45:49.970 --> 00:45:54.400
and really be able to understand what's going on

1052
00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:56.054
with that person.

1053
00:45:56.054 --> 00:45:59.740
Well, and the people who are in the addiction is a,

1054
00:45:59.740 --> 00:46:01.060
or substance use disorder

1055
00:46:01.060 --> 00:46:02.850
is what we're supposed to be saying,

1056
00:46:02.850 --> 00:46:07.850
that substance use disorder is related directly to trauma.

1057
00:46:07.890 --> 00:46:10.400
And that's Gabor Mate of the world.

1058
00:46:10.400 --> 00:46:12.960
And he's written so beautifully about that.

1059
00:46:12.960 --> 00:46:17.010
That is a legit, talk about camps and substance abuse

1060
00:46:17.010 --> 00:46:18.010
and substance use disorder.

1061
00:46:18.010 --> 00:46:19.440
So there's the trauma camp.

1062
00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:23.110
There's the idea that substance abuse

1063
00:46:23.110 --> 00:46:24.890
is a developmental disorder.

1064
00:46:24.890 --> 00:46:28.660
Because adolescents, kids are just uniquely wired

1065
00:46:28.660 --> 00:46:31.290
to want that risk and want that novelty

1066
00:46:31.290 --> 00:46:33.850
and also driven by the lower brain.

1067
00:46:33.850 --> 00:46:38.850
And people don't often become substance users/abusers

1068
00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:40.680
later on in life.

1069
00:46:40.680 --> 00:46:43.750
If it's gonna happen, it tends to happen during adolescence.

1070
00:46:43.750 --> 00:46:46.930
And then there's the brain science camp as well.

1071
00:46:46.930 --> 00:46:48.850
So speaking of the camps,

1072
00:46:48.850 --> 00:46:50.210
the trauma one is a very big one.

1073
00:46:50.210 --> 00:46:53.440
And actually the analogy that I hate but it's so apt

1074
00:46:53.440 --> 00:46:57.760
is that genetics is the bullet in the gun

1075
00:46:57.760 --> 00:46:59.260
and that trauma is the trigger.

1076
00:46:59.260 --> 00:47:01.230
That that bullet could sit there forever

1077
00:47:01.230 --> 00:47:04.040
and there could be two kids have the same trauma

1078
00:47:04.040 --> 00:47:08.880
and one just is better able to deal with it

1079
00:47:08.880 --> 00:47:13.430
and another kid just, have that trauma that happens to them

1080
00:47:13.430 --> 00:47:17.280
and they're off to the races and running with addiction.

1081
00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:19.460
So yeah.

1082
00:47:19.460 --> 00:47:22.914
In thinking about this whole construct

1083
00:47:22.914 --> 00:47:25.610
in the context of addiction

1084
00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:28.967
and then thinking about "The Gift of Failure,"

1085
00:47:28.967 --> 00:47:30.260
I mean "The Gift of Failure" was all about

1086
00:47:30.260 --> 00:47:34.363
like allowing kids to fail and kind of providing them

1087
00:47:34.363 --> 00:47:35.890
like a little bit greater agency

1088
00:47:35.890 --> 00:47:39.460
than the typical controlling parent.

1089
00:47:39.460 --> 00:47:41.660
It's about loosening the reins in some respect.

1090
00:47:41.660 --> 00:47:43.744
You see my problem then, don't you?

1091
00:47:43.744 --> 00:47:45.760
I know, so you have this inherent conflict now

1092
00:47:45.760 --> 00:47:48.820
because this book is all about how and when

1093
00:47:48.820 --> 00:47:49.970
to intervene, right?

1094
00:47:49.970 --> 00:47:54.280
So how do you square this idea of allowing

1095
00:47:54.280 --> 00:47:57.870
and then sort of being involved

1096
00:47:57.870 --> 00:48:01.057
and sort of letting kids fail versus,

1097
00:48:04.310 --> 00:48:06.350
which kind of, would be permissive

1098
00:48:06.350 --> 00:48:11.350
to an exploratory phase maybe with substances or maybe not.

1099
00:48:11.450 --> 00:48:14.430
So when is it appropriate to kind of

1100
00:48:14.430 --> 00:48:18.670
be in that allowing space or to shut things down?

1101
00:48:18.670 --> 00:48:19.503
Yeah.

1102
00:48:19.503 --> 00:48:21.100
Okay, so we gotta back up then,

1103
00:48:21.100 --> 00:48:23.833
because the research is really clear.

1104
00:48:25.110 --> 00:48:26.983
Well, it's not really clear.

1105
00:48:28.060 --> 00:48:29.490
You have to think about confounders

1106
00:48:29.490 --> 00:48:30.810
when you're talking about this research.

1107
00:48:30.810 --> 00:48:32.623
But if you look at the research,

1108
00:48:33.640 --> 00:48:37.120
the data show that if you are the kind of parent

1109
00:48:37.120 --> 00:48:41.780
that is consistently messaging total abstinence until 21,

1110
00:48:41.780 --> 00:48:44.280
until it is legal to drink or use pot

1111
00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:45.563
or whatever the thing is,

1112
00:48:46.440 --> 00:48:48.240
then your child is less likely

1113
00:48:48.240 --> 00:48:50.490
to have substance use disorder during their lifetime.

1114
00:48:50.490 --> 00:48:54.170
Now, as someone who always comes at statistics and data

1115
00:48:54.170 --> 00:48:55.840
with a question mark in my head, I say,

1116
00:48:55.840 --> 00:48:58.300
well, except it would be the parents

1117
00:48:58.300 --> 00:49:01.480
that have the total abstinence agenda

1118
00:49:01.480 --> 00:49:03.450
whose kids would have less access

1119
00:49:03.450 --> 00:49:04.920
to blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

1120
00:49:04.920 --> 00:49:09.120
But either way, the reason I bring that up is that it,

1121
00:49:09.120 --> 00:49:11.233
the parents who are like, okay, well,

1122
00:49:12.200 --> 00:49:13.540
I just want them to be safe.

1123
00:49:13.540 --> 00:49:16.120
So as long as they're doing it in the basement

1124
00:49:16.120 --> 00:49:17.830
and they're not driving, it's okay.

1125
00:49:17.830 --> 00:49:20.160
Or if I take the keys, or if they're,

1126
00:49:20.160 --> 00:49:21.450
as long as they're here in the home,

1127
00:49:21.450 --> 00:49:23.410
or I wanna give them a sip

1128
00:49:23.410 --> 00:49:25.930
so that they can grow up to be like those Europeans

1129
00:49:25.930 --> 00:49:27.830
that have the really sort of moderate,

1130
00:49:28.990 --> 00:49:32.870
they're not overusing their drinking and just having enough

1131
00:49:32.870 --> 00:49:34.550
and it's a part of the lifetime,

1132
00:49:34.550 --> 00:49:37.200
their life and meals and stuff like that.

1133
00:49:37.200 --> 00:49:40.560
The problem is, is that that sort of European romantic myth

1134
00:49:40.560 --> 00:49:44.710
of raising a moderate drinker is it's not true.

1135
00:49:44.710 --> 00:49:47.620
Not only from the standpoint of that doesn't work for kids,

1136
00:49:47.620 --> 00:49:50.090
a kid whose parents have a permissive attitude

1137
00:49:50.090 --> 00:49:52.780
around drinking and doing drugs at home

1138
00:49:52.780 --> 00:49:55.810
while drinking and doing drugs before 21,

1139
00:49:55.810 --> 00:49:59.010
those kids are more likely to have a substance use disorder

1140
00:49:59.010 --> 00:49:59.990
during their lifetime.

1141
00:49:59.990 --> 00:50:03.200
Also, hello, Europe has the highest rates

1142
00:50:03.200 --> 00:50:05.253
of alcoholism in the world.

1143
00:50:07.200 --> 00:50:09.130
They're even starting to have to deal with that.

1144
00:50:09.130 --> 00:50:11.860
France has gone back and changed its guidelines

1145
00:50:11.860 --> 00:50:13.590
around how much is healthy drinking

1146
00:50:13.590 --> 00:50:15.360
because they realize, yeah, yeah, yeah,

1147
00:50:15.360 --> 00:50:16.900
this has always been a part of our culture.

1148
00:50:16.900 --> 00:50:19.270
But oh my gosh, we have a problem.

1149
00:50:19.270 --> 00:50:20.830
I didn't realize that, wow.

1150
00:50:20.830 --> 00:50:25.323
Yeah, so it's really important that we not buy into that,

1151
00:50:27.140 --> 00:50:28.840
but I can raise moderate drinkers

1152
00:50:28.840 --> 00:50:31.700
by having this sort of sip of wine with dinner.

1153
00:50:31.700 --> 00:50:33.380
And I have to make it really clear,

1154
00:50:33.380 --> 00:50:35.050
talking about my own kids.

1155
00:50:35.050 --> 00:50:37.510
My younger kid is livid, right?

1156
00:50:37.510 --> 00:50:39.500
Because he's like, this is so unfair,

1157
00:50:39.500 --> 00:50:42.740
because my older brother, you didn't know this stuff then.

1158
00:50:42.740 --> 00:50:46.600
So he got to have his first taste of wine

1159
00:50:46.600 --> 00:50:48.660
beyond your finger when he was an infant.

1160
00:50:48.660 --> 00:50:51.740
Someone had sent us this grotesquely amazing

1161
00:50:51.740 --> 00:50:53.010
but expensive bottle of wine,

1162
00:50:53.010 --> 00:50:54.290
and so I put some on my finger

1163
00:50:54.290 --> 00:50:55.450
and put it on my infant's tongue.

1164
00:50:55.450 --> 00:50:58.640
I wanted his first taste of wine to be a Chateau d'Yquem.

1165
00:50:58.640 --> 00:51:00.870
Then--
(laughs) How dare you?

1166
00:51:00.870 --> 00:51:01.703
I know.

1167
00:51:01.703 --> 00:51:04.540
And then there was this like,

1168
00:51:04.540 --> 00:51:05.920
yeah, we know you're gonna drink,

1169
00:51:05.920 --> 00:51:07.130
but be careful kinda thing.

1170
00:51:07.130 --> 00:51:09.460
And now my 17-year-old is like, this sucks,

1171
00:51:09.460 --> 00:51:11.560
'cause you now are like total abstinence,

1172
00:51:11.560 --> 00:51:14.520
absolutely not, it's illegal, we don't do that before 21.

1173
00:51:14.520 --> 00:51:18.990
And all I can do is say, look, I'm only modeling for you

1174
00:51:18.990 --> 00:51:20.270
what I wanna see in you.

1175
00:51:20.270 --> 00:51:22.270
Like, I want you to say,

1176
00:51:22.270 --> 00:51:25.110
huh, I thought I had the right data,

1177
00:51:25.110 --> 00:51:26.620
I thought I was doing the right thing

1178
00:51:26.620 --> 00:51:28.340
and I realize now I'm not

1179
00:51:28.340 --> 00:51:29.970
and so I'm going to change what I'm doing

1180
00:51:29.970 --> 00:51:31.780
to do the right thing.

1181
00:51:31.780 --> 00:51:32.850
Sure.
Hello?

1182
00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:34.953
Total abstinence in my house until age 21.

1183
00:51:36.129 --> 00:51:36.962
And that's fine.

1184
00:51:36.962 --> 00:51:40.210
But do you not run the risk, hold on,

1185
00:51:40.210 --> 00:51:43.550
like, if you draw that hard line in the sand,

1186
00:51:43.550 --> 00:51:47.330
are you not risking the behavior going underground?

1187
00:51:47.330 --> 00:51:49.470
Like, you're gonna cut off the communication,

1188
00:51:49.470 --> 00:51:50.890
you're setting up the scenario

1189
00:51:50.890 --> 00:51:53.890
which your kid is gonna lie to you and hide stuff from you

1190
00:51:53.890 --> 00:51:56.670
and do what's he's gonna do or she's gonna do

1191
00:51:56.670 --> 00:51:59.263
and then you're not pivy to what's going on.

1192
00:52:00.660 --> 00:52:03.519
Except, so the other thing we know--

1193
00:52:03.519 --> 00:52:04.790
For people that are only listening,

1194
00:52:04.790 --> 00:52:06.990
you just have the biggest smile on your face.

1195
00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:08.065
(laughs)

1196
00:52:08.065 --> 00:52:09.225
No, it's just that--
Go ahead.

1197
00:52:09.225 --> 00:52:11.000
This is what killed me.

1198
00:52:11.000 --> 00:52:13.510
The writing of this book was really hard for me.

1199
00:52:13.510 --> 00:52:16.570
Because I do not read my children's emails,

1200
00:52:16.570 --> 00:52:17.660
I do not read their text.

1201
00:52:17.660 --> 00:52:19.580
I have never gone on the high school portal

1202
00:52:19.580 --> 00:52:21.130
and looked at my kids' grades.

1203
00:52:21.130 --> 00:52:23.280
I don't listen to their phone calls.

1204
00:52:23.280 --> 00:52:25.776
I trust my children.

1205
00:52:25.776 --> 00:52:29.220
And until I have reason not to trust my children,

1206
00:52:29.220 --> 00:52:32.190
I don't search their rooms, I don't read their stuff.

1207
00:52:32.190 --> 00:52:34.160
That's very important to me.

1208
00:52:34.160 --> 00:52:36.680
Because the research also is clear on this,

1209
00:52:36.680 --> 00:52:38.830
is that kids who are more controlled by their parents

1210
00:52:38.830 --> 00:52:40.140
lie to their parents more.

1211
00:52:40.140 --> 00:52:42.080
It's just the reality.

1212
00:52:42.080 --> 00:52:44.560
So if I want my kid to be the kind of kid

1213
00:52:44.560 --> 00:52:46.530
who can trust me and talk to me about things,

1214
00:52:46.530 --> 00:52:49.690
I have to respect his privacy

1215
00:52:49.690 --> 00:52:53.220
and I have to put forward the idea

1216
00:52:53.220 --> 00:52:54.980
that I trust and respect him, okay?

1217
00:52:54.980 --> 00:52:56.880
So there's that.

1218
00:52:56.880 --> 00:52:59.450
There's also real concerted efforts

1219
00:52:59.450 --> 00:53:03.490
to make these conversations really common

1220
00:53:03.490 --> 00:53:08.460
and an understanding that you don't have that one sex talk,

1221
00:53:08.460 --> 00:53:10.980
you don't have one drug and alcohol talk.

1222
00:53:10.980 --> 00:53:13.820
And my kid's in, just last semester,

1223
00:53:13.820 --> 00:53:17.860
he was in a biology class and the teacher asked the kids,

1224
00:53:17.860 --> 00:53:20.140
how often do your parents talk to you about substance abuse?

1225
00:53:20.140 --> 00:53:21.500
And Finn was like, oh my God,

1226
00:53:21.500 --> 00:53:24.890
when does my parent not talk to me about substance abuse.

1227
00:53:24.890 --> 00:53:26.990
So we're having a ton of conversations about it

1228
00:53:26.990 --> 00:53:27.823
all the time.

1229
00:53:27.823 --> 00:53:29.060
We're very open about those things.

1230
00:53:29.060 --> 00:53:31.150
And in order to get that place

1231
00:53:31.150 --> 00:53:34.240
where I feel like they can trust me to talk to me,

1232
00:53:34.240 --> 00:53:39.070
I have to be there to listen to all the other stuff

1233
00:53:39.070 --> 00:53:42.130
that interest them that isn't necessarily the stuff

1234
00:53:42.130 --> 00:53:43.440
I wanna hear.

1235
00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:45.690
I have to respect them

1236
00:53:45.690 --> 00:53:48.160
and we have to have open communication.

1237
00:53:48.160 --> 00:53:50.180
And I just have to hope

1238
00:53:50.180 --> 00:53:52.810
that the balance I'm trying to strike

1239
00:53:52.810 --> 00:53:55.520
between that trust and that respect,

1240
00:53:55.520 --> 00:53:58.690
obviously always subject to change at anytime

1241
00:53:58.690 --> 00:54:01.210
if I was getting scared about my kid

1242
00:54:01.210 --> 00:54:04.040
or there was a plain view doctrine situation

1243
00:54:04.040 --> 00:54:05.293
going on in my home.

1244
00:54:07.420 --> 00:54:12.420
But I can't force my kid to see the world

1245
00:54:13.690 --> 00:54:16.770
exactly the way I see it, unless I'm showing him

1246
00:54:16.770 --> 00:54:18.420
that I respect him to make the decisions.

1247
00:54:18.420 --> 00:54:21.380
And it's a really fine line I have to cross.

1248
00:54:21.380 --> 00:54:23.390
And as I said, it's open to change at any time

1249
00:54:23.390 --> 00:54:26.080
if I'm worried that my kid is having a problem,

1250
00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:27.380
that could turn on a dime.

1251
00:54:28.650 --> 00:54:30.660
Yeah, I mean, one of the things

1252
00:54:30.660 --> 00:54:32.430
that I'm really proud of as a parent

1253
00:54:32.430 --> 00:54:34.500
is that very thing of just,

1254
00:54:34.500 --> 00:54:36.270
I'm talking about this all the time

1255
00:54:36.270 --> 00:54:39.250
and I'm sharing my own experiences.

1256
00:54:39.250 --> 00:54:41.990
Like, my kids have heard all these stories

1257
00:54:41.990 --> 00:54:42.900
of things that I've done

1258
00:54:42.900 --> 00:54:45.150
and I've brought them to AA meetings

1259
00:54:45.150 --> 00:54:49.750
and they have experience with what that world is all about.

1260
00:54:49.750 --> 00:54:53.983
And most of my friends are sober people.

1261
00:54:55.130 --> 00:54:57.460
So my kids are exposed to them.

1262
00:54:57.460 --> 00:54:59.550
And actually my friends are able to have

1263
00:54:59.550 --> 00:55:01.880
really amazing conversations with my kids.

1264
00:55:01.880 --> 00:55:05.300
Because those conversations are devoid

1265
00:55:05.300 --> 00:55:07.580
of the emotional charge that it would have

1266
00:55:07.580 --> 00:55:09.713
if it's me talking, right?

1267
00:55:10.609 --> 00:55:11.442
Right.

1268
00:55:11.442 --> 00:55:12.275
And as a result of that,

1269
00:55:12.275 --> 00:55:15.648
we have amazing open channels of communication.

1270
00:55:15.648 --> 00:55:20.350
Like, my eldest daughter tells me all kinds of crazy stuff

1271
00:55:20.350 --> 00:55:24.180
and so for me then it's about, like,

1272
00:55:24.180 --> 00:55:27.180
I can feel my body tensing up and I wanna react

1273
00:55:27.180 --> 00:55:30.010
or I have this impulse to judge

1274
00:55:30.010 --> 00:55:32.720
or to make some definitive statement.

1275
00:55:32.720 --> 00:55:37.259
And it takes all that I have to just like listen and receive

1276
00:55:37.259 --> 00:55:40.590
and to kind of absorb that

1277
00:55:40.590 --> 00:55:43.580
in a loving and compassionate way.

1278
00:55:43.580 --> 00:55:47.250
And I found that the more I'm able to be in that space,

1279
00:55:47.250 --> 00:55:49.860
the better our relationship is.

1280
00:55:49.860 --> 00:55:52.340
And I'm taking out an insurance policy

1281
00:55:52.340 --> 00:55:54.810
on maintaining that open channel of communication.

1282
00:55:54.810 --> 00:55:58.070
But it has its challenging moments.

1283
00:55:58.070 --> 00:55:59.300
And I will also say that--
Of course.

1284
00:55:59.300 --> 00:56:04.300
It has not been an iron clad inoculation against problems.

1285
00:56:06.910 --> 00:56:08.250
Oh, absolutely not.

1286
00:56:08.250 --> 00:56:09.910
Right, absolutely not.

1287
00:56:09.910 --> 00:56:11.900
None of these things are.

1288
00:56:11.900 --> 00:56:16.583
And I think so much of,

1289
00:56:17.530 --> 00:56:20.610
so much of what I hear from kids,

1290
00:56:20.610 --> 00:56:22.730
and I get to talk to a lot of kids and it's so much fun,

1291
00:56:22.730 --> 00:56:24.790
and one of things I do when I'm out at a school

1292
00:56:24.790 --> 00:56:27.620
talking to kids is I give them all my email address,

1293
00:56:27.620 --> 00:56:29.500
and I say, okay, look,

1294
00:56:29.500 --> 00:56:31.300
I'm gonna be talking to your parents tonight.

1295
00:56:31.300 --> 00:56:34.360
I want you to email me with, before that,

1296
00:56:34.360 --> 00:56:36.910
with the things you want me to tell your parents.

1297
00:56:36.910 --> 00:56:39.030
And the top three things,

1298
00:56:39.030 --> 00:56:40.840
I mean usually I get three things

1299
00:56:40.840 --> 00:56:41.730
over and over and over again.

1300
00:56:41.730 --> 00:56:44.980
One is, I'm not my brother, I'm not my sister,

1301
00:56:44.980 --> 00:56:46.920
I'm not you when you were my age.

1302
00:56:46.920 --> 00:56:49.350
That sort of like, I don't feel seen, heard, known

1303
00:56:49.350 --> 00:56:50.610
for who I am.

1304
00:56:50.610 --> 00:56:52.710
I feel like you're raising some imaginary kid

1305
00:56:52.710 --> 00:56:54.980
you wish I was and not me.

1306
00:56:54.980 --> 00:56:57.690
So that's really stunning to me.

1307
00:56:57.690 --> 00:57:00.030
I also get a lot about like,

1308
00:57:00.030 --> 00:57:02.280
if you are going to tell me I have to do something,

1309
00:57:02.280 --> 00:57:03.490
then you should have to do it too.

1310
00:57:03.490 --> 00:57:06.716
It's usually related to having to put down the phone.

1311
00:57:06.716 --> 00:57:07.549
Like, if I have to put down the phone,

1312
00:57:07.549 --> 00:57:08.850
why doesn't my parent?

1313
00:57:08.850 --> 00:57:11.220
But one of the other big things they tell me is,

1314
00:57:11.220 --> 00:57:13.640
I want to talk to my parents.

1315
00:57:13.640 --> 00:57:15.360
I really, really do.

1316
00:57:15.360 --> 00:57:18.010
It's just that I don't wanna talk about the stuff

1317
00:57:18.010 --> 00:57:19.850
they wanna talk about all the time,

1318
00:57:19.850 --> 00:57:22.080
which is like I don't wanna constantly

1319
00:57:22.080 --> 00:57:23.130
be talking about school.

1320
00:57:23.130 --> 00:57:24.900
Can't we talk about something else?

1321
00:57:24.900 --> 00:57:27.540
And so I think the reason that,

1322
00:57:27.540 --> 00:57:29.010
one of the chapters in the book

1323
00:57:29.010 --> 00:57:31.240
is about getting this conversation started

1324
00:57:31.240 --> 00:57:32.300
and how hard that can be.

1325
00:57:32.300 --> 00:57:35.030
And I talked to lots of therapists

1326
00:57:35.030 --> 00:57:38.580
who have to try to get kids to talk about stuff

1327
00:57:38.580 --> 00:57:39.790
when they don't wanna talk.

1328
00:57:39.790 --> 00:57:42.330
And a lot of 'em, there's some cool stuff.

1329
00:57:42.330 --> 00:57:45.020
Like, if you're in the same room with your kid

1330
00:57:45.020 --> 00:57:46.900
and they don't want to say words,

1331
00:57:46.900 --> 00:57:49.710
tell them to text it to you across the room.

1332
00:57:49.710 --> 00:57:52.240
That may feel weird and horrible and fake,

1333
00:57:52.240 --> 00:57:54.860
but it's actually, can be a really authentic way

1334
00:57:54.860 --> 00:57:56.220
of communicating.

1335
00:57:56.220 --> 00:57:57.360
But I mean, the reason,

1336
00:57:57.360 --> 00:57:59.350
in the chapter that I'm talking about,

1337
00:57:59.350 --> 00:58:01.730
I talk about the crazy lengths I go to

1338
00:58:01.730 --> 00:58:04.710
to get my kids to talk to me and tell me stuff.

1339
00:58:04.710 --> 00:58:07.480
But a little bit of that is me showing them

1340
00:58:07.480 --> 00:58:09.970
that it's really, that I want it that much,

1341
00:58:09.970 --> 00:58:13.560
that I want, that I am so willing to meet them

1342
00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:17.450
on their level in a place that they wanna talk about stuff,

1343
00:58:17.450 --> 00:58:21.440
that I'm willing to go to these stupid lengths of making,

1344
00:58:21.440 --> 00:58:24.276
recreating the show "Hot Ones"--

1345
00:58:24.276 --> 00:58:25.561
I was just gonna bring that up.

1346
00:58:25.561 --> 00:58:26.394
In my dining room.

1347
00:58:26.394 --> 00:58:28.440
That's one of my favorite parts of the whole book,

1348
00:58:28.440 --> 00:58:30.839
is the "Hot Ones" dinner--
It was so much fun.

1349
00:58:30.839 --> 00:58:31.720
And the questions that you came up with.

1350
00:58:31.720 --> 00:58:34.080
So talk a little bit about that.

1351
00:58:34.080 --> 00:58:36.610
Yeah, so we've loved the show "Hot Ones"

1352
00:58:36.610 --> 00:58:37.640
since the very beginning.

1353
00:58:37.640 --> 00:58:40.490
Sean Evans, I think, is a talented interviewer.

1354
00:58:40.490 --> 00:58:42.410
Such a talented interviewer.

1355
00:58:42.410 --> 00:58:43.810
And his shtick.

1356
00:58:43.810 --> 00:58:45.920
Partially 'cause he's an incredible researcher,

1357
00:58:45.920 --> 00:58:50.910
but also the shtick is that you're eating a hot wing and,

1358
00:58:50.910 --> 00:58:52.160
10 different hot wings of,

1359
00:58:52.160 --> 00:58:55.770
10 XL of hotter and hotter hot wings,

1360
00:58:55.770 --> 00:58:58.740
and that throws you off of your game a little bit,

1361
00:58:58.740 --> 00:59:00.720
get your defenses down that kind of thing.

1362
00:59:00.720 --> 00:59:02.590
And it's fun.

1363
00:59:02.590 --> 00:59:04.410
You go into that thinking it's gonna be fun.

1364
00:59:04.410 --> 00:59:05.870
It's a little scary, but it's fun.

1365
00:59:05.870 --> 00:59:10.870
So my husband, I said, let's do hot ones at home over,

1366
00:59:11.010 --> 00:59:14.230
at our dinner table, but we've got to come up with questions

1367
00:59:14.230 --> 00:59:15.063
for the kids.

1368
00:59:15.063 --> 00:59:16.100
And they can't be stupid questions.

1369
00:59:16.100 --> 00:59:18.070
They can't be prying questions.

1370
00:59:18.070 --> 00:59:20.500
They can't be questions that will embarrass them.

1371
00:59:20.500 --> 00:59:22.330
What are the 10 questions we get?

1372
00:59:22.330 --> 00:59:24.770
Like, what do we really wanna know?

1373
00:59:24.770 --> 00:59:28.197
And so we wrote 10 questions for each kid,

1374
00:59:28.197 --> 00:59:29.320
one to go with each wing.

1375
00:59:29.320 --> 00:59:33.360
And then I went and got unseasoned wings and vegan wings

1376
00:59:33.360 --> 00:59:35.830
and bought the full range

1377
00:59:35.830 --> 00:59:39.480
of that season's hot sauces, I think, and did each one.

1378
00:59:39.480 --> 00:59:41.490
And the kids didn't know, it was a total surprise,

1379
00:59:41.490 --> 00:59:42.830
and they came down.

1380
00:59:42.830 --> 00:59:43.840
And they were all in.

1381
00:59:43.840 --> 00:59:44.880
It was really fun.

1382
00:59:44.880 --> 00:59:48.040
They sort of smiled as soon as they understood

1383
00:59:48.040 --> 00:59:49.340
what was happening.

1384
00:59:49.340 --> 00:59:50.460
And we did the whole thing.

1385
00:59:50.460 --> 00:59:52.180
And we were willing to be self-effacing,

1386
00:59:52.180 --> 00:59:53.570
like, yep, we're this dorky.

1387
00:59:53.570 --> 00:59:54.510
We really do want you.

1388
00:59:54.510 --> 00:59:57.760
And we acknowledged the fact that it was funny

1389
00:59:57.760 --> 00:59:59.360
and we had a great evening.

1390
00:59:59.360 --> 01:00:01.430
We did end up drinking ice cream, though,

1391
01:00:01.430 --> 01:00:03.575
because the hottest hot wing

1392
01:00:03.575 --> 01:00:04.600
(laughs)

1393
01:00:04.600 --> 01:00:08.030
required pretty much just solid vanilla ice cream.

1394
01:00:08.030 --> 01:00:10.433
It was just so overpoweringly hot.

1395
01:00:10.433 --> 01:00:12.536
Like, two million Scoville or whatever it is.

1396
01:00:12.536 --> 01:00:13.490
It was crazy.

1397
01:00:13.490 --> 01:00:17.530
The one that they make, the last dab, it was intense.

1398
01:00:17.530 --> 01:00:19.050
It was really crazy.

1399
01:00:19.050 --> 01:00:20.160
But it worked.

1400
01:00:20.160 --> 01:00:21.900
I'm looking at the questions that you asked.

1401
01:00:21.900 --> 01:00:25.250
It's like, what about you reminds you

1402
01:00:25.250 --> 01:00:27.130
of one or more of your parents?

1403
01:00:27.130 --> 01:00:30.990
Like, provocative questions that are open-ended.

1404
01:00:30.990 --> 01:00:33.650
And when your mouth is on fire, I would imagine that was,

1405
01:00:33.650 --> 01:00:35.680
that would've been pretty hilarious.

1406
01:00:35.680 --> 01:00:38.110
It was also fun because some, one of the questions,

1407
01:00:38.110 --> 01:00:39.981
like, what about you reminds you

1408
01:00:39.981 --> 01:00:41.060
of one or more of your grandparents too?

1409
01:00:41.060 --> 01:00:43.040
And it was really interesting

1410
01:00:43.040 --> 01:00:45.370
that the answers really surprised me.

1411
01:00:45.370 --> 01:00:47.960
And we had whole discussions about the answers.

1412
01:00:47.960 --> 01:00:49.960
Like, oh, I thought you would've said this,

1413
01:00:49.960 --> 01:00:52.740
or that really surprises me.

1414
01:00:52.740 --> 01:00:54.360
I think you're more like your grandfather

1415
01:00:54.360 --> 01:00:56.050
than your grandmother that kind of thing.

1416
01:00:56.050 --> 01:00:57.670
So it wasn't just about the questions,

1417
01:00:57.670 --> 01:00:59.860
but it was about the conversation that happened

1418
01:00:59.860 --> 01:01:02.223
around the questions too, which was fun.

1419
01:01:04.030 --> 01:01:07.700
What are some of the common mistakes that parents make,

1420
01:01:07.700 --> 01:01:10.470
like well-intentioned parents who are trying to

1421
01:01:10.470 --> 01:01:12.900
institute these preventative measures

1422
01:01:12.900 --> 01:01:16.250
around substance abuse with their kids?

1423
01:01:16.250 --> 01:01:19.060
I think a lot of parents have information.

1424
01:01:19.060 --> 01:01:21.200
For example, lots of parents know

1425
01:01:21.200 --> 01:01:25.420
that the first place kids often encounter opiates

1426
01:01:25.420 --> 01:01:26.790
are in the medicine cabinet, right,

1427
01:01:26.790 --> 01:01:28.810
the leftover pills in the medicine cabinet.

1428
01:01:28.810 --> 01:01:31.020
And I think a lot of our hopes about,

1429
01:01:31.020 --> 01:01:33.960
oh, but my kid wouldn't, or that kind of stuff

1430
01:01:33.960 --> 01:01:37.620
keeps us from putting those opiates under lock and key

1431
01:01:37.620 --> 01:01:40.690
or even having the conversation about opiates

1432
01:01:40.690 --> 01:01:41.780
in the medicine cabinet.

1433
01:01:41.780 --> 01:01:45.440
Well, the vast majority of parents surveyed,

1434
01:01:45.440 --> 01:01:48.900
in this one survey, knew that opiates often come first

1435
01:01:48.900 --> 01:01:50.720
from the family medicine cabinet.

1436
01:01:50.720 --> 01:01:53.240
Only 10% of them were talking to their own kids

1437
01:01:53.240 --> 01:01:55.420
about the dangers of taking opiates

1438
01:01:55.420 --> 01:01:56.510
out of the medicine cabinet.

1439
01:01:56.510 --> 01:01:59.288
So it's also just scary.

1440
01:01:59.288 --> 01:02:02.230
I think one of the reasons that writing a book

1441
01:02:02.230 --> 01:02:05.650
about substance abuse delegates you to, like,

1442
01:02:05.650 --> 01:02:07.990
the bottom shelf down near the floor

1443
01:02:07.990 --> 01:02:10.290
at the back of the bookshelf, at the back of the bookstore,

1444
01:02:10.290 --> 01:02:12.660
is that this is a really scary topic.

1445
01:02:12.660 --> 01:02:16.280
So the only way to make it a not scary topic

1446
01:02:16.280 --> 01:02:18.590
is by talking about it a lot.

1447
01:02:18.590 --> 01:02:23.160
I mean, I think we talk about it a lot in our house

1448
01:02:23.160 --> 01:02:25.110
mainly because I'm proud of my recovery,

1449
01:02:25.110 --> 01:02:28.570
I'm proud of my parent who is now in recovery,

1450
01:02:28.570 --> 01:02:33.570
but they also remember when it wasn't nice.

1451
01:02:33.660 --> 01:02:36.800
And those conversations, especially with my kids,

1452
01:02:36.800 --> 01:02:38.720
I have to have conversations with my kids

1453
01:02:38.720 --> 01:02:41.760
that are more about whether you drink

1454
01:02:41.760 --> 01:02:43.800
or have your first drug or whatever

1455
01:02:43.800 --> 01:02:46.320
but what it looks like and feels like

1456
01:02:46.320 --> 01:02:49.230
when it goes from use into abuse.

1457
01:02:49.230 --> 01:02:51.880
What does that feel like?

1458
01:02:51.880 --> 01:02:53.940
Like, where is that happening?

1459
01:02:53.940 --> 01:02:56.340
So there are some different conversations

1460
01:02:56.340 --> 01:02:58.290
that I think parents are really scared of.

1461
01:02:58.290 --> 01:02:59.123
Rightly so.

1462
01:02:59.123 --> 01:03:02.857
'Cause if you start with your first conversation

1463
01:03:02.857 --> 01:03:07.530
as about taking drugs, that's really scary.

1464
01:03:07.530 --> 01:03:09.850
But if you're starting really young,

1465
01:03:09.850 --> 01:03:12.120
with a really little kid and you're talking about

1466
01:03:12.120 --> 01:03:15.020
the fact that mommy's name is on that bottle of pills

1467
01:03:15.020 --> 01:03:17.940
on the counter because it's for mommy.

1468
01:03:17.940 --> 01:03:19.080
We don't take medicines

1469
01:03:19.080 --> 01:03:20.570
that aren't prescribed for other people.

1470
01:03:20.570 --> 01:03:23.650
Or talking to them about general health and stuff like that.

1471
01:03:23.650 --> 01:03:25.130
If we start really, really young

1472
01:03:25.130 --> 01:03:27.320
and that becomes a normalized part of the conversation,

1473
01:03:27.320 --> 01:03:28.680
it's not nearly as scary.

1474
01:03:28.680 --> 01:03:30.780
And I think avoiding things that are scary

1475
01:03:30.780 --> 01:03:32.150
is something that we're all guilty of.

1476
01:03:32.150 --> 01:03:34.734
I hate having the sex conversations.

1477
01:03:34.734 --> 01:03:35.760
I hate it.

1478
01:03:35.760 --> 01:03:36.593
I hate it.

1479
01:03:36.593 --> 01:03:37.550
(laughs)

1480
01:03:37.550 --> 01:03:38.470
I think you're really good

1481
01:03:38.470 --> 01:03:42.910
at it, though.
It's horrible.

1482
01:03:42.910 --> 01:03:45.050
I think I say this in the book, I can't remember,

1483
01:03:45.050 --> 01:03:46.740
but my husband, I have two boys,

1484
01:03:46.740 --> 01:03:51.060
my husband tends to have more of the sex conversations.

1485
01:03:51.060 --> 01:03:53.130
The preferred place to do it.

1486
01:03:53.130 --> 01:03:55.220
We used to live right near the Dartmouth Skiway,

1487
01:03:55.220 --> 01:03:57.240
like we could walk to it from our house,

1488
01:03:57.240 --> 01:04:00.440
so chairlifts are great because you're both there

1489
01:04:00.440 --> 01:04:02.020
but you're not looking at each other.

1490
01:04:02.020 --> 01:04:03.370
You're looking straight ahead.

1491
01:04:03.370 --> 01:04:06.210
So you don't have to look in the eye of the person,

1492
01:04:06.210 --> 01:04:09.340
which is why driving and chairlifts and place like that

1493
01:04:09.340 --> 01:04:10.210
work really well.

1494
01:04:10.210 --> 01:04:12.580
Chairlifts, great place for these conversations.

1495
01:04:12.580 --> 01:04:13.790
Yeah, well, as--

1496
01:04:13.790 --> 01:04:16.335
'Cause you're trapped for a given amount of time.

1497
01:04:16.335 --> 01:04:17.168
(laughs)

1498
01:04:17.168 --> 01:04:20.100
I mean, you have to be kind of like a Jedi

1499
01:04:20.100 --> 01:04:25.100
in order to get a glimpse into the interior life

1500
01:04:25.610 --> 01:04:26.500
of a teenager.

1501
01:04:26.500 --> 01:04:29.610
You can't will that conversation into happening, right?

1502
01:04:29.610 --> 01:04:31.120
You can't come at it directly.

1503
01:04:31.120 --> 01:04:36.120
Like, I've just learned that I have to cast my fishing rod

1504
01:04:36.330 --> 01:04:39.610
in so many different directions,

1505
01:04:39.610 --> 01:04:41.830
trying different things, coming at it sideways,

1506
01:04:41.830 --> 01:04:45.820
trying to figure out what my child wants to talk about.

1507
01:04:45.820 --> 01:04:49.490
And then it's only in the process of doing that

1508
01:04:49.490 --> 01:04:52.060
and the child becoming comfortable

1509
01:04:52.060 --> 01:04:53.590
that something will eke out

1510
01:04:53.590 --> 01:04:56.420
or some something that they wouldn't have said

1511
01:04:56.420 --> 01:04:59.980
if you ask them directly will become clear.

1512
01:04:59.980 --> 01:05:03.410
And then to grab onto those moments and just hold onto them

1513
01:05:03.410 --> 01:05:06.670
because they don't come that frequently.

1514
01:05:06.670 --> 01:05:09.500
And the thing from, as a teacher, I can tell you,

1515
01:05:09.500 --> 01:05:11.570
is it's not just about the what you say,

1516
01:05:11.570 --> 01:05:13.740
it's often sometimes about the when you say it.

1517
01:05:13.740 --> 01:05:16.940
And when I was teaching middle school

1518
01:05:16.940 --> 01:05:18.950
and was watching kids just sort of screw up

1519
01:05:18.950 --> 01:05:20.700
all around me all the time,

1520
01:05:20.700 --> 01:05:22.440
you don't pounce on a kid, right,

1521
01:05:22.440 --> 01:05:23.640
when they make a big mistake,

1522
01:05:23.640 --> 01:05:25.050
and he's feeling bad about it,

1523
01:05:25.050 --> 01:05:27.010
and talk about how to do better next time.

1524
01:05:27.010 --> 01:05:29.530
Sometimes you have to wait for just the right moment.

1525
01:05:29.530 --> 01:05:32.196
Sometimes they need to breathe for a minute.

1526
01:05:32.196 --> 01:05:36.290
And sometimes, especially with substance use,

1527
01:05:36.290 --> 01:05:38.570
you know this, that it's never ever,

1528
01:05:38.570 --> 01:05:40.730
it's hardly ever that first person

1529
01:05:40.730 --> 01:05:41.930
that comes to you and says,

1530
01:05:41.930 --> 01:05:44.200
I think you've got a problem going on here.

1531
01:05:44.200 --> 01:05:45.770
That's gonna make you think, oh my God,

1532
01:05:45.770 --> 01:05:47.230
I've got a problem going on here.

1533
01:05:47.230 --> 01:05:49.940
Sometimes it's person 100, but it's like a puzzle.

1534
01:05:49.940 --> 01:05:53.130
If person one through 99 doesn't say something,

1535
01:05:53.130 --> 01:05:55.510
then person 100 won't click.

1536
01:05:55.510 --> 01:05:58.340
And sometimes it'll be person 42

1537
01:05:58.340 --> 01:06:00.120
and sometimes it'll be person 68.

1538
01:06:00.120 --> 01:06:02.290
And you can't know that ahead of time.

1539
01:06:02.290 --> 01:06:06.620
So there's partially just a whole timing thing

1540
01:06:06.620 --> 01:06:08.360
that's out of your control

1541
01:06:08.360 --> 01:06:10.620
in terms of when your kids are receptive.

1542
01:06:10.620 --> 01:06:13.110
But making sure they're sleeping well

1543
01:06:13.110 --> 01:06:14.700
and making sure they're eating well

1544
01:06:14.700 --> 01:06:17.950
and making sure that your relationship is in a place

1545
01:06:17.950 --> 01:06:21.090
where they trust you to have these kind of conversations

1546
01:06:21.090 --> 01:06:24.750
that's like the big base that you start from.

1547
01:06:24.750 --> 01:06:27.530
Right, of course, with that, though,

1548
01:06:27.530 --> 01:06:29.181
one of the things that we contend with

1549
01:06:29.181 --> 01:06:33.090
is they don't wanna do any of the stuff that we like,

1550
01:06:33.090 --> 01:06:34.500
hey, you gotta get a good night's sleep.

1551
01:06:34.500 --> 01:06:35.540
Like, maybe--
Right.

1552
01:06:35.540 --> 01:06:37.633
Eat a salad instead of this.

1553
01:06:39.410 --> 01:06:41.300
And they wanna have their own experience.

1554
01:06:41.300 --> 01:06:44.673
And it doesn't matter how much we model healthy behavior,

1555
01:06:46.090 --> 01:06:49.480
they want to figure out who they are

1556
01:06:49.480 --> 01:06:51.420
through differentiation.

1557
01:06:51.420 --> 01:06:53.964
And on some level, you have to allow that, right?

1558
01:06:53.964 --> 01:06:54.797
Like, you have to allow that.

1559
01:06:54.797 --> 01:06:56.660
Because it's like inception,

1560
01:06:56.660 --> 01:06:58.970
and you talk a lot about this in both books.

1561
01:06:58.970 --> 01:07:02.820
Like, you have to orient the child into an experience

1562
01:07:02.820 --> 01:07:07.820
where the idea that you would like them to adopt become,

1563
01:07:07.880 --> 01:07:10.490
they have to take ownership of that on some way,

1564
01:07:10.490 --> 01:07:12.130
like it's their own idea.

1565
01:07:12.130 --> 01:07:14.770
Otherwise, it doesn't stick.

1566
01:07:14.770 --> 01:07:17.810
The inception lens on this discussion.

1567
01:07:17.810 --> 01:07:19.280
That's a place I'd never gone.

1568
01:07:19.280 --> 01:07:20.730
I like it, I like it.

1569
01:07:20.730 --> 01:07:22.560
No, I think you're completely right.

1570
01:07:22.560 --> 01:07:24.950
But I think also that

1571
01:07:26.700 --> 01:07:31.700
there's a certain amount of learning by the,

1572
01:07:35.709 --> 01:07:38.630
in writing, we call it planning and pantsing.

1573
01:07:38.630 --> 01:07:42.080
So there's planners, people who plan an entire book;

1574
01:07:42.080 --> 01:07:43.260
and then there's the pantsers,

1575
01:07:43.260 --> 01:07:45.450
who just sorta jump in and start going.

1576
01:07:45.450 --> 01:07:48.660
And I think a lot of us have to do a lot of pantsing,

1577
01:07:48.660 --> 01:07:51.070
but at the same time we're learning as we go along.

1578
01:07:51.070 --> 01:07:53.300
And this stuff that we're learning as we go along

1579
01:07:53.300 --> 01:07:56.590
sort of informs the next iteration of what we try.

1580
01:07:56.590 --> 01:07:59.440
And that modeling is what we're trying to do for kids too,

1581
01:07:59.440 --> 01:08:01.880
which is learn from the mistakes that we make

1582
01:08:01.880 --> 01:08:03.710
so that they don't have to make those mistakes

1583
01:08:03.710 --> 01:08:05.330
or so that we can do better next time.

1584
01:08:05.330 --> 01:08:10.330
And the thing about like the sleeping and the eating,

1585
01:08:11.000 --> 01:08:13.380
one of the things I talk a lot about in both books

1586
01:08:13.380 --> 01:08:14.890
are logical consequences,

1587
01:08:14.890 --> 01:08:16.880
like making sure that they do get to feel

1588
01:08:16.880 --> 01:08:19.210
the logical consequences of their actions.

1589
01:08:19.210 --> 01:08:20.850
And if your kid is not getting enough sleep,

1590
01:08:20.850 --> 01:08:22.580
and, by the way, kids are getting a lot more sleep

1591
01:08:22.580 --> 01:08:23.673
during the pandemic.

1592
01:08:24.920 --> 01:08:27.480
The research on that survey seemed to show

1593
01:08:27.480 --> 01:08:29.930
that they're getting a fair amount,

1594
01:08:29.930 --> 01:08:31.490
actually quite a bit more sleep.

1595
01:08:31.490 --> 01:08:32.760
They used to just laugh at me,

1596
01:08:32.760 --> 01:08:34.840
like when I would go out and speak at schools and I'd say,

1597
01:08:34.840 --> 01:08:36.500
here's how much sleep you're supposed to be getting,

1598
01:08:36.500 --> 01:08:38.337
and they're like, that's really funny.

1599
01:08:38.337 --> 01:08:39.480
(laughs)

1600
01:08:39.480 --> 01:08:41.430
But now they're actually getting more sleep.

1601
01:08:41.430 --> 01:08:44.750
My kid did go through a period of being nocturnal, though.

1602
01:08:44.750 --> 01:08:45.670
That was really interesting.

1603
01:08:45.670 --> 01:08:50.010
But I had to also let him suffer a little bit when that,

1604
01:08:50.010 --> 01:08:50.850
I couldn't rescue him,

1605
01:08:50.850 --> 01:08:52.480
couldn't take stuff to school for him.

1606
01:08:52.480 --> 01:08:55.040
I let him sleep through his alarm actually, twice,

1607
01:08:55.040 --> 01:08:59.120
because he just wasn't dealing with this stuff.

1608
01:08:59.120 --> 01:09:01.920
But here's a really interesting thing about during the,

1609
01:09:01.920 --> 01:09:04.470
right now anyway, is that schools are having to be

1610
01:09:04.470 --> 01:09:05.810
a lot more flexible.

1611
01:09:05.810 --> 01:09:09.050
So this is a really great time for you to give,

1612
01:09:09.050 --> 01:09:10.710
let your kid feel the consequences

1613
01:09:10.710 --> 01:09:12.540
of some of these mistakes.

1614
01:09:12.540 --> 01:09:15.520
Because a lot of schools are saying,

1615
01:09:15.520 --> 01:09:18.190
we just can't hold kids to the same

1616
01:09:18.190 --> 01:09:20.100
exact rigorous standards we were before this.

1617
01:09:20.100 --> 01:09:21.980
So there's been a lot of opportunities

1618
01:09:21.980 --> 01:09:24.520
to let kids take the reins a little bit more

1619
01:09:24.520 --> 01:09:26.660
during the pandemic, which has been good.

1620
01:09:26.660 --> 01:09:28.300
Yeah, because the stakes are lower.

1621
01:09:28.300 --> 01:09:31.470
I mean, we've been dealing with the nocturnal thing

1622
01:09:31.470 --> 01:09:36.030
and our MO with it is like, okay, well, how do you feel?

1623
01:09:36.030 --> 01:09:38.240
Are you thinking clearly, like how's your mood?

1624
01:09:38.240 --> 01:09:39.810
How are your anxiety levels?

1625
01:09:39.810 --> 01:09:41.490
Oh, that's interesting.

1626
01:09:41.490 --> 01:09:43.970
Maybe you might wanna rethink this.

1627
01:09:43.970 --> 01:09:48.420
But not saying you have to go to bed at this time or else.

1628
01:09:48.420 --> 01:09:51.960
And then by dent of kind of being,

1629
01:09:51.960 --> 01:09:55.850
having kid gloves around it, they come into their own.

1630
01:09:55.850 --> 01:09:57.850
They have that inception moment where they're like,

1631
01:09:57.850 --> 01:09:59.400
yeah, this doesn't work for me.

1632
01:10:00.240 --> 01:10:01.930
But that's hard as a parent.

1633
01:10:01.930 --> 01:10:04.370
Right, and think about what this pandemic has been like

1634
01:10:04.370 --> 01:10:07.140
for kids who are trying to individuate and pull away

1635
01:10:07.140 --> 01:10:10.480
and have more and more and more autonomy and independence

1636
01:10:10.480 --> 01:10:13.230
at a time when it's being taken away from them

1637
01:10:13.230 --> 01:10:14.063
at every turn.

1638
01:10:14.063 --> 01:10:16.376
It's the only act of rebellion.

1639
01:10:16.376 --> 01:10:17.730
I'm gonna stay up all night.

1640
01:10:17.730 --> 01:10:20.230
Well that or, I've been begging parents.

1641
01:10:20.230 --> 01:10:22.250
Man, if you make your kids clean their room

1642
01:10:22.250 --> 01:10:23.110
every single morning

1643
01:10:23.110 --> 01:10:25.250
because you have some misplaced assumption

1644
01:10:25.250 --> 01:10:29.430
that an organized kid becomes an organized adult

1645
01:10:29.430 --> 01:10:32.340
or that a messy kid can't possibly be an organized adult,

1646
01:10:32.340 --> 01:10:33.450
just let go, please.

1647
01:10:33.450 --> 01:10:36.630
Because the only place kids tend to have

1648
01:10:36.630 --> 01:10:39.150
any autonomy whatsoever is in their rooms.

1649
01:10:39.150 --> 01:10:40.640
Just let them have their rooms

1650
01:10:40.640 --> 01:10:43.640
the way they wanna have their rooms right now.

1651
01:10:43.640 --> 01:10:46.980
In fact, my kids, at a certain point,

1652
01:10:46.980 --> 01:10:49.690
they just needed a change so they switched rooms.

1653
01:10:49.690 --> 01:10:52.750
I have a picture, at one point,

1654
01:10:52.750 --> 01:10:55.080
we painted my son's room entirely blue,

1655
01:10:55.080 --> 01:10:57.800
the color of the blue on a globe, like the oceans.

1656
01:10:57.800 --> 01:11:00.370
And then he took an overhead projector from my classroom

1657
01:11:00.370 --> 01:11:04.210
and used a sharpie and made an entire world in his room.

1658
01:11:04.210 --> 01:11:05.920
This would be a really great chance

1659
01:11:05.920 --> 01:11:08.870
to let them just do whatever they want with their rooms

1660
01:11:08.870 --> 01:11:10.810
so that they can have some control somewhere.

1661
01:11:10.810 --> 01:11:12.900
Because if they can't find it,

1662
01:11:12.900 --> 01:11:14.270
if don't give it to them somewhere,

1663
01:11:14.270 --> 01:11:15.370
they're gonna take it.

1664
01:11:15.370 --> 01:11:18.543
And sometimes they take it by deception.

1665
01:11:20.200 --> 01:11:23.130
One of the things that you get good at,

1666
01:11:23.130 --> 01:11:26.282
if you've spent a lot of time

1667
01:11:26.282 --> 01:11:31.282
in the secret societies of recovery

1668
01:11:32.280 --> 01:11:37.280
is you become very attuned to how people behave

1669
01:11:38.590 --> 01:11:41.813
when they're using, like you can spot the signs

1670
01:11:41.813 --> 01:11:43.730
a million miles away

1671
01:11:43.730 --> 01:11:45.790
and you become like a palm reader

1672
01:11:45.790 --> 01:11:47.100
when you encounter somebody

1673
01:11:47.100 --> 01:11:50.050
and you can tell kind of immediately where somebody is at

1674
01:11:50.050 --> 01:11:51.740
and you can just kind of read signals

1675
01:11:51.740 --> 01:11:53.060
in a way that a normal person

1676
01:11:53.060 --> 01:11:55.400
who doesn't have that experience can't.

1677
01:11:55.400 --> 01:11:59.280
And this is something I've been talking a lot about

1678
01:11:59.280 --> 01:12:02.320
with my wife, who is not in recovery

1679
01:12:02.320 --> 01:12:04.500
and doesn't have that much experience

1680
01:12:04.500 --> 01:12:08.430
with people in recovery other than myself.

1681
01:12:08.430 --> 01:12:11.800
And I can say to her, like if we encounter something,

1682
01:12:11.800 --> 01:12:14.060
oh, this is what's going on with that person.

1683
01:12:14.060 --> 01:12:14.893
She'd be like, really?

1684
01:12:14.893 --> 01:12:15.726
I didn't see that.

1685
01:12:15.726 --> 01:12:18.660
And I bring this up because I think it's instructive

1686
01:12:18.660 --> 01:12:22.340
in terms of helping parents or just anybody

1687
01:12:22.340 --> 01:12:24.760
identify the warning signs,

1688
01:12:24.760 --> 01:12:26.850
whether it's a child or an adult

1689
01:12:26.850 --> 01:12:29.310
who might be headed in the wrong direction.

1690
01:12:29.310 --> 01:12:34.040
So maybe talk a little bit about some of those cues

1691
01:12:34.040 --> 01:12:37.480
so that we can all be kind of more on top of things

1692
01:12:37.480 --> 01:12:40.410
before they get too out of control.

1693
01:12:40.410 --> 01:12:45.410
I mean, so the cues that I tend to look for as a parent

1694
01:12:45.720 --> 01:12:47.420
and as a teacher is a change.

1695
01:12:47.420 --> 01:12:52.420
Like, if you have a kid who is baseline fairly introverted

1696
01:12:52.730 --> 01:12:57.730
and likes their cave time and that person is

1697
01:12:58.400 --> 01:13:00.780
doing all of those things now, I'm not,

1698
01:13:00.780 --> 01:13:03.610
I'm more worried about any kid that has any sort of change.

1699
01:13:03.610 --> 01:13:05.360
As a teacher, I look for a change in grades.

1700
01:13:05.360 --> 01:13:08.140
As as a parent, I look for a change in attitude

1701
01:13:08.140 --> 01:13:10.190
and you can just sort of feel that your kid is off.

1702
01:13:10.190 --> 01:13:12.040
And so anytime a kid changes like that,

1703
01:13:12.040 --> 01:13:14.110
I'm always gonna have some sort of questioning

1704
01:13:14.110 --> 01:13:15.780
about what's going on during adolescence.

1705
01:13:15.780 --> 01:13:18.280
That could be tough 'cause it's a moving target.

1706
01:13:18.280 --> 01:13:21.820
Kids are changing like crazy during adolescence

1707
01:13:21.820 --> 01:13:24.550
But change is the big thing that I worry about.

1708
01:13:24.550 --> 01:13:27.310
If they're sleeping more, sleeping a lot less,

1709
01:13:27.310 --> 01:13:29.250
if they're eating more, eating a lot less,

1710
01:13:29.250 --> 01:13:31.840
if they're depressed, whereas they're normally up

1711
01:13:31.840 --> 01:13:33.670
or if they're up when they're normally down,

1712
01:13:33.670 --> 01:13:36.160
that sort of changes the thing I look for the most.

1713
01:13:36.160 --> 01:13:40.430
But one thing I wanted to sort of mention is that

1714
01:13:41.810 --> 01:13:46.810
when we talk about the risk for kids

1715
01:13:47.620 --> 01:13:49.823
and we're talking about sort of where we're,

1716
01:13:51.311 --> 01:13:54.370
where we as parents are in our heads,

1717
01:13:54.370 --> 01:13:56.590
you said the thing about how recovery

1718
01:13:56.590 --> 01:13:58.420
allows you to see things more clearly,

1719
01:13:58.420 --> 01:13:59.780
I also wanna make it really clear

1720
01:13:59.780 --> 01:14:01.560
that one of the things we talk about in recovery

1721
01:14:01.560 --> 01:14:03.760
is that once you've had some recovery,

1722
01:14:03.760 --> 01:14:05.100
once you've gone to a couple of meetings,

1723
01:14:05.100 --> 01:14:06.610
even if you're just faking it

1724
01:14:06.610 --> 01:14:08.360
and still using the whole time,

1725
01:14:08.360 --> 01:14:10.640
knowing that information you learn there

1726
01:14:10.640 --> 01:14:12.423
makes it a lot less fun to use.

1727
01:14:13.309 --> 01:14:14.937
And so that--

1728
01:14:14.937 --> 01:14:15.791
To put it mildly.

1729
01:14:15.791 --> 01:14:16.700
(laughs)

1730
01:14:16.700 --> 01:14:19.460
So that information is a lot of what I'm talking about

1731
01:14:19.460 --> 01:14:20.293
in the book.

1732
01:14:20.293 --> 01:14:22.650
And there's some really cool things I found out about.

1733
01:14:22.650 --> 01:14:25.070
I mean, the reason the word inoculation is in the title

1734
01:14:25.070 --> 01:14:27.780
is because of this thing called inoculation theory

1735
01:14:27.780 --> 01:14:31.350
that's really, really useful and helpful for helping kids

1736
01:14:31.350 --> 01:14:35.050
feel like they have the emotional wherewithal

1737
01:14:35.050 --> 01:14:39.210
and the capability to have good refusal skills

1738
01:14:39.210 --> 01:14:41.130
and to refuse when someone says,

1739
01:14:41.130 --> 01:14:42.810
everybody does it, or it's no big deal.

1740
01:14:42.810 --> 01:14:47.490
And then I learned about the fact

1741
01:14:47.490 --> 01:14:49.160
that most kids tend to overestimate

1742
01:14:49.160 --> 01:14:52.050
how much other kids want to drink and use drugs.

1743
01:14:52.050 --> 01:14:53.840
And so if you come at them with data,

1744
01:14:53.840 --> 01:14:55.610
if you come at them with knowledge,

1745
01:14:55.610 --> 01:14:57.700
not only does it make it less fun to use

1746
01:14:57.700 --> 01:15:00.360
because you're, if I'm coming at you with information

1747
01:15:00.360 --> 01:15:02.280
about what is happening in your brain

1748
01:15:02.280 --> 01:15:03.840
and what this does to your brain,

1749
01:15:03.840 --> 01:15:07.370
and, PS, when you say you're an eighth grader

1750
01:15:07.370 --> 01:15:09.810
and you're saying that everyone's trying drinking

1751
01:15:09.810 --> 01:15:11.090
and it's no big deal,

1752
01:15:11.090 --> 01:15:14.950
and I happen to know that only 24% of kids try

1753
01:15:14.950 --> 01:15:18.250
even a sip more, a sip or more of alcohol by eighth grade,

1754
01:15:18.250 --> 01:15:20.870
then I can say, sweetie, it's not everyone.

1755
01:15:20.870 --> 01:15:22.730
Actually, it's only 24%.

1756
01:15:22.730 --> 01:15:26.400
So if you've got you and 10 friends,

1757
01:15:26.400 --> 01:15:28.620
there's really only a couple of kids that are doing that.

1758
01:15:28.620 --> 01:15:32.210
So that information not only is,

1759
01:15:32.210 --> 01:15:34.780
gives kids the why they want,

1760
01:15:34.780 --> 01:15:37.110
like, because I said so does not work with kids, right?

1761
01:15:37.110 --> 01:15:39.850
I'm sure you're familiar with that at this point.

1762
01:15:39.850 --> 01:15:42.010
But here's the why does work.

1763
01:15:42.010 --> 01:15:46.810
And it not only works because it helps kids feel empowered

1764
01:15:46.810 --> 01:15:49.790
and in control, it's also a big buzzkill.

1765
01:15:49.790 --> 01:15:51.820
Just like going to some meetings is a big buzzkill.

1766
01:15:51.820 --> 01:15:53.670
And it's never as fun to be high

1767
01:15:53.670 --> 01:15:55.640
when you actually know that it's killing cells

1768
01:15:55.640 --> 01:15:58.250
in your hippocampus or it's doing what the other things are

1769
01:15:58.250 --> 01:15:59.490
that it does.

1770
01:15:59.490 --> 01:16:02.410
That's why I'm a big fan of talking to kids about the why.

1771
01:16:02.410 --> 01:16:05.750
Yeah, the peer group thing is so huge, though.

1772
01:16:05.750 --> 01:16:09.160
I know tons of kids and they'll say,

1773
01:16:09.160 --> 01:16:12.100
if I don't go along to get along,

1774
01:16:12.100 --> 01:16:13.590
then I'm not gonna have any friends,

1775
01:16:13.590 --> 01:16:15.470
or I'm gonna be socially ostracized,

1776
01:16:15.470 --> 01:16:17.870
or I'll be the only one who's not doing that.

1777
01:16:17.870 --> 01:16:19.620
And they're just not willing

1778
01:16:19.620 --> 01:16:23.010
to live on the perimeter of their peer group

1779
01:16:23.010 --> 01:16:27.100
for the sake of sobriety when that why

1780
01:16:27.100 --> 01:16:28.300
doesn't really compute.

1781
01:16:28.300 --> 01:16:31.410
Because when you're young, you feel bulletproof anyway.

1782
01:16:31.410 --> 01:16:32.243
So who cares?

1783
01:16:32.243 --> 01:16:33.530
Yup, yeah.

1784
01:16:33.530 --> 01:16:35.660
And what's interesting is that overestimation

1785
01:16:35.660 --> 01:16:36.730
I was just talking about.

1786
01:16:36.730 --> 01:16:38.330
Like, if you ask a kid

1787
01:16:38.330 --> 01:16:39.800
how much they think their friends drink,

1788
01:16:39.800 --> 01:16:41.420
they'll estimate more.

1789
01:16:41.420 --> 01:16:44.410
What's really interesting is, from a gender perspective,

1790
01:16:44.410 --> 01:16:46.680
a lot of boys will up their consumption

1791
01:16:46.680 --> 01:16:49.030
in order to match what that perceived norm is

1792
01:16:49.030 --> 01:16:52.760
even though the truth is lower than the perceived norm,

1793
01:16:52.760 --> 01:16:55.430
whereas girls actually will tend to withdraw.

1794
01:16:55.430 --> 01:16:58.550
And so if all of their friends are drinking

1795
01:16:58.550 --> 01:16:59.930
and wanna do that sort of stuff

1796
01:16:59.930 --> 01:17:02.610
or they perceive that's the case, they'll pull away.

1797
01:17:02.610 --> 01:17:06.240
So there's very different sort of reactions.

1798
01:17:06.240 --> 01:17:09.550
But knowing that, I think, is so important.

1799
01:17:09.550 --> 01:17:13.950
Because if we're modeling for them

1800
01:17:13.950 --> 01:17:15.480
what good relationships look like,

1801
01:17:15.480 --> 01:17:18.010
if we're modeling for them, the thing I value,

1802
01:17:18.010 --> 01:17:21.040
in fact, I was just talking about this earlier today.

1803
01:17:21.040 --> 01:17:23.030
My friends and I were talking about,

1804
01:17:23.030 --> 01:17:25.070
a couple of years ago, we were getting together somewhere

1805
01:17:25.070 --> 01:17:26.300
and a friend of mine called ahead

1806
01:17:26.300 --> 01:17:27.830
to the place we were going to find out

1807
01:17:27.830 --> 01:17:30.170
if there'd be non-alcoholic options for me there.

1808
01:17:30.170 --> 01:17:32.483
And I said in front of my kid,

1809
01:17:33.640 --> 01:17:35.410
that showed me that she loved me.

1810
01:17:35.410 --> 01:17:37.230
And it was at a time when he was having,

1811
01:17:37.230 --> 01:17:40.960
in the middle of a relationship, where he was not feeling

1812
01:17:40.960 --> 01:17:42.450
like he was being treated very well.

1813
01:17:42.450 --> 01:17:44.230
And so we were able to have this conversation

1814
01:17:44.230 --> 01:17:47.560
about what it is we get from relationships.

1815
01:17:47.560 --> 01:17:49.700
That's what I love about being friends with this person.

1816
01:17:49.700 --> 01:17:51.820
And what is it you like being friends with that person?

1817
01:17:51.820 --> 01:17:53.400
'Cause you seem to not feel very good

1818
01:17:53.400 --> 01:17:56.010
when you come home from his house and that kind of thing.

1819
01:17:56.010 --> 01:17:57.374
Right, right, right.

1820
01:17:57.374 --> 01:18:01.590
So the problem is all of this requires us to say,

1821
01:18:01.590 --> 01:18:03.110
what's happening in my relationships,

1822
01:18:03.110 --> 01:18:04.790
what's happening with my drinking,

1823
01:18:04.790 --> 01:18:06.410
what's happening with my drug use,

1824
01:18:06.410 --> 01:18:09.820
what's happening with the way I handle when I make mistakes

1825
01:18:09.820 --> 01:18:11.330
that kind of stuff.

1826
01:18:11.330 --> 01:18:13.560
The way we model that for kids is,

1827
01:18:13.560 --> 01:18:16.980
they tend to watch us more than they tend to listen to us.

1828
01:18:16.980 --> 01:18:17.813
I know.

1829
01:18:17.813 --> 01:18:19.920
Annoyingly so, unfortunately.
I know.

1830
01:18:19.920 --> 01:18:23.690
But you do have to walk the talk in a certain regard.

1831
01:18:23.690 --> 01:18:27.370
You can't expect your kid to model healthy behavior

1832
01:18:27.370 --> 01:18:30.630
or to do what you say if you're not doing that

1833
01:18:30.630 --> 01:18:32.170
in your own household.

1834
01:18:32.170 --> 01:18:33.370
Right, and that doesn't mean

1835
01:18:33.370 --> 01:18:34.640
that you have to be abstinent.

1836
01:18:34.640 --> 01:18:36.410
Because what I'm talking about

1837
01:18:36.410 --> 01:18:40.300
is I have a husband who drinks like a normal person

1838
01:18:40.300 --> 01:18:43.030
and they see both of that from us,

1839
01:18:43.030 --> 01:18:46.260
but what really worries me is this culture of sort of,

1840
01:18:46.260 --> 01:18:48.300
especially around women and drinking,

1841
01:18:48.300 --> 01:18:52.600
the whole I have to have the wine at the end of the day,

1842
01:18:52.600 --> 01:18:54.580
this is mommy juice, here's my wine glass

1843
01:18:54.580 --> 01:18:55.650
with sippy cup on top.

1844
01:18:55.650 --> 01:18:59.040
I saw these cups at a bookstore,

1845
01:18:59.040 --> 01:19:00.120
not gonna name the bookstore

1846
01:19:00.120 --> 01:19:01.800
'cause I happen to love the bookstore,

1847
01:19:01.800 --> 01:19:04.050
and they say, it said on the glass

1848
01:19:04.050 --> 01:19:06.520
I teach, therefore I wine.

1849
01:19:06.520 --> 01:19:11.520
And the idea that we're messaging to kids

1850
01:19:12.190 --> 01:19:15.170
that we use this drug, the drugs and alcohol,

1851
01:19:15.170 --> 01:19:17.460
in order to not feel stuff, to unwind,

1852
01:19:17.460 --> 01:19:18.740
to have to deal with our stress,

1853
01:19:18.740 --> 01:19:21.290
that there's no other way to do that,

1854
01:19:21.290 --> 01:19:22.123
that's what worries me.

1855
01:19:22.123 --> 01:19:26.140
And that's what I try to talk a lot about with my kids.

1856
01:19:26.140 --> 01:19:27.580
Tina Payne Bryson, who works,

1857
01:19:27.580 --> 01:19:29.560
has written a bunch of books with Dan Siegel,

1858
01:19:29.560 --> 01:19:30.667
she's got this great saying,

1859
01:19:30.667 --> 01:19:32.280
"You have to name it to tame it," right?

1860
01:19:32.280 --> 01:19:34.880
So with kids, helping them name their emotions

1861
01:19:34.880 --> 01:19:36.270
and helping them name what they're feeling,

1862
01:19:36.270 --> 01:19:37.960
what they're going through is the first step

1863
01:19:37.960 --> 01:19:40.200
to helping them talk about what's bugging them

1864
01:19:40.200 --> 01:19:42.710
so they don't feel like they have to have a beer

1865
01:19:42.710 --> 01:19:47.710
to quiet that voice, so that they don't have to resort

1866
01:19:47.760 --> 01:19:50.313
to medicating themselves to not, you know.

1867
01:19:51.490 --> 01:19:53.220
The drink I missed the most, frankly,

1868
01:19:53.220 --> 01:19:56.420
is the one before I go to a party

1869
01:19:56.420 --> 01:19:58.170
so that I can feel like I'm worthy

1870
01:19:58.170 --> 01:19:59.310
of walking through that door,

1871
01:19:59.310 --> 01:20:00.940
so I don't have imposter syndrome,

1872
01:20:00.940 --> 01:20:04.210
so I feel like a better version of myself.

1873
01:20:04.210 --> 01:20:09.210
And I think helping kids be the kind of person

1874
01:20:09.910 --> 01:20:12.690
who doesn't have to medicate themselves

1875
01:20:12.690 --> 01:20:16.330
to feel like they're enough, that's my goal with teaching,

1876
01:20:16.330 --> 01:20:17.600
that's my goal with parenting.

1877
01:20:17.600 --> 01:20:18.433
Yeah.

1878
01:20:18.433 --> 01:20:20.540
Well, there's certainly an epidemic

1879
01:20:20.540 --> 01:20:21.990
of low self-esteem, right?

1880
01:20:21.990 --> 01:20:23.230
And that's the heavy lifting.

1881
01:20:23.230 --> 01:20:24.917
Like, how can you get a child

1882
01:20:24.917 --> 01:20:28.470
to feel comfortable in their own skin

1883
01:20:28.470 --> 01:20:32.060
and inhabit their truth and stand their ground

1884
01:20:32.060 --> 01:20:35.640
and look people in the eye and feel good about who they are.

1885
01:20:35.640 --> 01:20:40.320
And so many young people struggle with that tremendously,

1886
01:20:40.320 --> 01:20:43.570
and the level of discomfort that that produces.

1887
01:20:43.570 --> 01:20:46.080
It's no mistake or it's no mystery

1888
01:20:46.080 --> 01:20:51.033
why substance abuse seems like a good option

1889
01:20:51.033 --> 01:20:54.440
because it ameliorates that immediately

1890
01:20:54.440 --> 01:20:55.843
and it's very dependable in that regard.

1891
01:20:55.843 --> 01:20:59.030
It's gonna do its job every single time.

1892
01:20:59.030 --> 01:21:00.040
In the short term, yeah.

1893
01:21:00.040 --> 01:21:02.070
Yeah, in the short term.

1894
01:21:02.070 --> 01:21:03.095
(laughs)

1895
01:21:03.095 --> 01:21:04.136
In the short term.

1896
01:21:04.136 --> 01:21:05.910
I mean, I think that that's the thing, is it works.

1897
01:21:05.910 --> 01:21:09.000
And someone asked me a couple of days ago about

1898
01:21:09.000 --> 01:21:10.980
the whole idea of personality

1899
01:21:10.980 --> 01:21:13.060
and which drug you take and that sort of thing.

1900
01:21:13.060 --> 01:21:15.120
And I said, what's interesting to me

1901
01:21:15.120 --> 01:21:18.520
is for many people they'll have that lock and key moment.

1902
01:21:18.520 --> 01:21:20.270
For me, it was booze.

1903
01:21:20.270 --> 01:21:23.330
And for another people person, Nic Sheff,

1904
01:21:23.330 --> 01:21:25.960
in the book "High" that he and David just put out

1905
01:21:25.960 --> 01:21:27.460
a couple of years ago.

1906
01:21:27.460 --> 01:21:29.670
Nic talks about that moment with crystal meth

1907
01:21:29.670 --> 01:21:31.430
where he was like, yep, I liked pot,

1908
01:21:31.430 --> 01:21:34.187
I liked drinking out, but crystal meth was like, oh,

1909
01:21:35.680 --> 01:21:37.389
that was the thing for me.

1910
01:21:37.389 --> 01:21:38.222
Right.

1911
01:21:38.222 --> 01:21:40.730
And kids often talk about it as

1912
01:21:40.730 --> 01:21:44.360
in terms of everyone else knew how to maneuver through life

1913
01:21:44.360 --> 01:21:46.500
and I just, it was like everyone else had the rules but me.

1914
01:21:46.500 --> 01:21:48.090
I mean, these are things we hear a lot.

1915
01:21:48.090 --> 01:21:52.760
But for the kids, it's like drugs and alcohol gave me

1916
01:21:52.760 --> 01:21:56.580
the ability to be me but better.

1917
01:21:56.580 --> 01:22:01.570
And through everything I do with work and with my writing,

1918
01:22:01.570 --> 01:22:04.440
I want kids to be that better them

1919
01:22:04.440 --> 01:22:06.640
so that they don't have to take something

1920
01:22:06.640 --> 01:22:08.317
to be them but better.

1921
01:22:08.317 --> 01:22:09.150
Yeah, yeah.

1922
01:22:09.150 --> 01:22:12.920
I mean, that was certainly my experience with drinking.

1923
01:22:12.920 --> 01:22:17.050
But I think what's kind of crucial in this,

1924
01:22:17.050 --> 01:22:19.070
and you do talk about this in the book as well,

1925
01:22:19.070 --> 01:22:23.530
is, as parents, to be honest about these things, right?

1926
01:22:23.530 --> 01:22:27.660
Not to just simply vilify drugs and alcohol,

1927
01:22:27.660 --> 01:22:31.530
say they're horrible without acknowledging the truth

1928
01:22:31.530 --> 01:22:33.813
of why people use them.

1929
01:22:33.813 --> 01:22:35.540
Like, I think the more you can kind of

1930
01:22:35.540 --> 01:22:37.180
be transparent about that,

1931
01:22:37.180 --> 01:22:38.990
like, this is how it made me feel,

1932
01:22:38.990 --> 01:22:41.330
or this is why people do it,

1933
01:22:41.330 --> 01:22:43.483
that engenders trust in the child

1934
01:22:43.483 --> 01:22:46.110
so that they feel like they can come to you

1935
01:22:46.110 --> 01:22:47.420
with some of this information.

1936
01:22:47.420 --> 01:22:50.510
But when you just make it very binary,

1937
01:22:50.510 --> 01:22:53.920
then, as you say in the book, like that shuts down,

1938
01:22:53.920 --> 01:22:57.190
like that erodes the trust because the child feels like

1939
01:22:57.190 --> 01:22:58.750
you're being disingenuous about it.

1940
01:22:58.750 --> 01:22:59.583
Yeah.

1941
01:22:59.583 --> 01:23:01.070
I mean, I'm very clear with them.

1942
01:23:01.070 --> 01:23:03.240
I drank to deal with my anxiety.

1943
01:23:03.240 --> 01:23:06.962
My husband, he smoked a lot of pot.

1944
01:23:06.962 --> 01:23:08.477
(laughs)

1945
01:23:08.477 --> 01:23:11.210
After college, when he didn't,

1946
01:23:11.210 --> 01:23:13.330
he couldn't get a job in the field he wanted,

1947
01:23:13.330 --> 01:23:15.020
he didn't know what he was gonna do.

1948
01:23:15.020 --> 01:23:18.340
He'd sort of half-assed it through college.

1949
01:23:18.340 --> 01:23:21.410
And he just was lost and scared and bored

1950
01:23:21.410 --> 01:23:24.080
and lived with some people who grew pot in the basement.

1951
01:23:24.080 --> 01:23:27.140
So at the end, he's very clear with the kids.

1952
01:23:27.140 --> 01:23:29.410
He's like, look, I had to go off

1953
01:23:29.410 --> 01:23:30.800
to graduate school after that

1954
01:23:30.800 --> 01:23:33.280
and I could feel that I'd messed up my memory,

1955
01:23:33.280 --> 01:23:36.763
that my short-term memory was nowhere where it needed to be.

1956
01:23:38.020 --> 01:23:39.770
And I did that to myself.

1957
01:23:39.770 --> 01:23:43.030
Before my brain was done growing and maturing,

1958
01:23:43.030 --> 01:23:46.500
I obliterated parts of my hippocampus

1959
01:23:46.500 --> 01:23:48.990
because I was smoking so much pot.

1960
01:23:48.990 --> 01:23:50.240
We have those conversations with them.

1961
01:23:50.240 --> 01:23:52.630
And I talk in the book actually about the opposite thing,

1962
01:23:52.630 --> 01:23:54.750
being really a problem too,

1963
01:23:54.750 --> 01:23:58.260
is one guy, who should've known better in the book,

1964
01:23:58.260 --> 01:23:59.710
who I don't name him actually

1965
01:23:59.710 --> 01:24:01.100
'cause he was embarrassed by this,

1966
01:24:01.100 --> 01:24:05.050
was he made it sound,

1967
01:24:05.050 --> 01:24:07.500
in a way he was sort of bonding with his son

1968
01:24:07.500 --> 01:24:10.610
and talking about the good times in college and stuff

1969
01:24:10.610 --> 01:24:12.760
and made it sound a little too fun.

1970
01:24:12.760 --> 01:24:15.866
And his son said later on when he was in his 20s,

1971
01:24:15.866 --> 01:24:17.830
in retrospect, I don't think you should've made it

1972
01:24:17.830 --> 01:24:19.573
sound like such a good time.

1973
01:24:21.370 --> 01:24:25.030
Yeah, that reminds me of the section in the book

1974
01:24:25.030 --> 01:24:27.690
where you talk about going to the,

1975
01:24:27.690 --> 01:24:30.430
there's the college party where the beer pong is happening.

1976
01:24:30.430 --> 01:24:31.800
It's like parents weekend

1977
01:24:31.800 --> 01:24:34.220
and the parents are trying to look cool with the kids

1978
01:24:34.220 --> 01:24:36.710
and participating all of this.

1979
01:24:36.710 --> 01:24:40.180
And that kind of like objectivity to realize

1980
01:24:40.180 --> 01:24:43.900
like there's something wildly dysfunctional about this,

1981
01:24:43.900 --> 01:24:46.530
but also completely understandable.

1982
01:24:46.530 --> 01:24:47.510
Yeah, yeah.

1983
01:24:47.510 --> 01:24:51.410
Well, it was fascinating, because the kids at this college

1984
01:24:51.410 --> 01:24:53.840
decided to host a dinner for their parents.

1985
01:24:53.840 --> 01:24:55.640
So they wanna cook for their parents,

1986
01:24:55.640 --> 01:24:58.140
they're excited, they're proud, they want their parent.

1987
01:24:58.140 --> 01:25:02.807
And the parents are about to be in a room with their kids

1988
01:25:02.807 --> 01:25:07.400
and their kids' friends and parents of their kids' friends.

1989
01:25:07.400 --> 01:25:10.420
So there's a lot of pressure to not be the total dork,

1990
01:25:10.420 --> 01:25:13.950
yet a lot of us are complete total dorks.

1991
01:25:13.950 --> 01:25:16.140
And so the kids were all like,

1992
01:25:16.140 --> 01:25:18.230
I know, we'll set up the ski shot.

1993
01:25:18.230 --> 01:25:20.770
Here in New England, what they do is they have the ski

1994
01:25:20.770 --> 01:25:23.900
and then they have shot glasses attached to the ski

1995
01:25:23.900 --> 01:25:25.110
so everyone has to drink,

1996
01:25:25.110 --> 01:25:27.520
like 10 people have to do a shot at the same time.

1997
01:25:27.520 --> 01:25:28.930
And you could see the kids

1998
01:25:28.930 --> 01:25:31.150
trying to convince their parents to drink.

1999
01:25:31.150 --> 01:25:32.470
And the parents were,

2000
01:25:32.470 --> 01:25:33.897
some of 'em were really uncomfortable with it

2001
01:25:33.897 --> 01:25:35.330
and some of them were way in.

2002
01:25:35.330 --> 01:25:37.130
One dad had a shirt on that said

2003
01:25:37.130 --> 01:25:39.010
thirsty with a question mark

2004
01:25:39.010 --> 01:25:42.470
and he was clearly sort of reliving his heyday.

2005
01:25:42.470 --> 01:25:45.550
And we all just wanted our kids to think we were cool.

2006
01:25:45.550 --> 01:25:48.990
And so I was seeing this weird situation

2007
01:25:48.990 --> 01:25:52.390
where parents were being pressured by their children

2008
01:25:52.390 --> 01:25:54.340
and by the perception

2009
01:25:54.340 --> 01:25:56.680
among their children's friends' parents

2010
01:25:56.680 --> 01:25:58.570
to, just have a shot, mom.

2011
01:25:58.570 --> 01:26:01.040
It was really fascinating to me.

2012
01:26:01.040 --> 01:26:02.990
But what was really fascinating,

2013
01:26:02.990 --> 01:26:06.350
we're just listening in on a bunch of those conversations

2014
01:26:06.350 --> 01:26:10.300
about sort of how parents were feeling about,

2015
01:26:10.300 --> 01:26:14.210
I had my younger son there, who at the time was I think 16,

2016
01:26:14.210 --> 01:26:15.990
and he was watching this going on.

2017
01:26:15.990 --> 01:26:19.100
So I was having all these feelings about

2018
01:26:19.100 --> 01:26:20.970
him watching his brother

2019
01:26:20.970 --> 01:26:23.930
and him watching his brother's friends

2020
01:26:23.930 --> 01:26:25.830
and it was really a bizarre situation.

2021
01:26:27.050 --> 01:26:30.110
Right, well, maybe that brings up

2022
01:26:30.110 --> 01:26:31.700
something that we could talk about

2023
01:26:31.700 --> 01:26:33.627
around the differences between

2024
01:26:34.920 --> 01:26:37.010
how this works with college-aged kids

2025
01:26:37.010 --> 01:26:40.352
versus elementary school, middle school, high school

2026
01:26:40.352 --> 01:26:45.340
because there's kind of different inoculatory protocols

2027
01:26:45.340 --> 01:26:48.150
for each of these various age groups.

2028
01:26:48.150 --> 01:26:49.070
Yeah.

2029
01:26:49.070 --> 01:26:51.720
So I go, in the book, I wanna make it really clear.

2030
01:26:51.720 --> 01:26:53.070
Both "Gift of Failure" and this book,

2031
01:26:53.070 --> 01:26:55.310
like broad-based research findings

2032
01:26:55.310 --> 01:26:57.940
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, lots of stories, lots of fun.

2033
01:26:57.940 --> 01:27:00.690
But there's also like here are scripts

2034
01:27:00.690 --> 01:27:01.860
for things you can say

2035
01:27:01.860 --> 01:27:04.210
and here's like this very granular level

2036
01:27:04.210 --> 01:27:06.150
things you can do with your kids at every age group.

2037
01:27:06.150 --> 01:27:08.740
And it starts with preschool

2038
01:27:08.740 --> 01:27:10.510
and goes all the way up into college.

2039
01:27:10.510 --> 01:27:13.910
And the cool thing, I think, even going up into college

2040
01:27:13.910 --> 01:27:16.510
is that we still, when you talk to kids,

2041
01:27:16.510 --> 01:27:20.170
when you do surveys with kids, college-aged kids,

2042
01:27:20.170 --> 01:27:21.570
about where they get their information,

2043
01:27:21.570 --> 01:27:23.340
they're still getting a ton of their information

2044
01:27:23.340 --> 01:27:25.280
from their parents, which is great, right,

2045
01:27:25.280 --> 01:27:27.800
which means that we still have some influence

2046
01:27:27.800 --> 01:27:32.000
over our kids' perceptions, and even into college.

2047
01:27:32.000 --> 01:27:34.380
So there are age-appropriate,

2048
01:27:34.380 --> 01:27:36.280
developmentally-appropriate things you can do

2049
01:27:36.280 --> 01:27:39.790
at every age group to talk about prevention.

2050
01:27:39.790 --> 01:27:41.540
But the college thing really stunned me

2051
01:27:41.540 --> 01:27:45.240
in terms of the amount of alcohol that's present on campus

2052
01:27:45.240 --> 01:27:48.590
and how it's treated, varies by state,

2053
01:27:48.590 --> 01:27:50.933
varies by the culture.

2054
01:27:51.950 --> 01:27:53.810
The college stuff was really fascinating.

2055
01:27:53.810 --> 01:27:56.450
And all the research on Princeton,

2056
01:27:56.450 --> 01:28:01.450
about the pluralistic ignorance stuff at Princeton.

2057
01:28:02.050 --> 01:28:05.250
They took kegs away, because I had no idea

2058
01:28:05.250 --> 01:28:07.350
that Princeton was one of the heaviest drinking colleges

2059
01:28:07.350 --> 01:28:08.813
in the country.
Right, I didn't know that,

2060
01:28:10.245 --> 01:28:12.560
I got drunk at Princeton--
I didn't know.

2061
01:28:12.560 --> 01:28:14.857
So I've experienced that culture there.

2062
01:28:14.857 --> 01:28:16.380
It's been a long time.

2063
01:28:16.380 --> 01:28:19.460
But I didn't realize that it's like other than,

2064
01:28:19.460 --> 01:28:21.794
what was it, like the Indianapolis 500 or--

2065
01:28:21.794 --> 01:28:22.680
The Indy 500.

2066
01:28:22.680 --> 01:28:24.780
That there's more drinking at Princeton

2067
01:28:24.780 --> 01:28:26.260
than anywhere else in the country.

2068
01:28:26.260 --> 01:28:29.080
And it's during alumni weekend in particular.

2069
01:28:29.080 --> 01:28:31.380
And it's sort of like a cross between

2070
01:28:32.730 --> 01:28:34.420
the people coming back

2071
01:28:34.420 --> 01:28:36.500
and wanting to relive their heyday sort of thing.

2072
01:28:36.500 --> 01:28:38.000
But Princeton took advantage,

2073
01:28:38.000 --> 01:28:39.480
some researchers took advantage of the fact

2074
01:28:39.480 --> 01:28:40.500
that Princeton was like, okay,

2075
01:28:40.500 --> 01:28:41.780
well, we'll do something about this.

2076
01:28:41.780 --> 01:28:43.010
We'll get rid of kegs.

2077
01:28:43.010 --> 01:28:45.130
We'll say kegs are not allowed on campus anymore,

2078
01:28:45.130 --> 01:28:47.880
which is such a token, weird thing to do.

2079
01:28:47.880 --> 01:28:51.780
But they took advantage of that to sort of survey

2080
01:28:51.780 --> 01:28:54.000
how much the kids cared

2081
01:28:54.000 --> 01:28:56.030
about whether or not kegs were allowed on campus,

2082
01:28:56.030 --> 01:28:57.687
how much they felt other people cared about it.

2083
01:28:57.687 --> 01:28:59.950
And the research, what they found out

2084
01:28:59.950 --> 01:29:01.140
was that kids were like,

2085
01:29:01.140 --> 01:29:02.900
well, I actually don't care one way or the other,

2086
01:29:02.900 --> 01:29:06.100
but I think everyone else cares a lot.

2087
01:29:06.100 --> 01:29:07.700
And that was the same answer they got

2088
01:29:07.700 --> 01:29:08.770
from just about everyone,

2089
01:29:08.770 --> 01:29:11.090
which is I don't really care that much one way or the other,

2090
01:29:11.090 --> 01:29:13.470
but I think everybody else cares a lot.

2091
01:29:13.470 --> 01:29:16.510
The research coming out of the college communities also

2092
01:29:16.510 --> 01:29:19.810
with the advent of like wellness dorms,

2093
01:29:19.810 --> 01:29:22.400
I mean, these are exploding all over campuses.

2094
01:29:22.400 --> 01:29:24.697
And Ron Lieber has this new book called

2095
01:29:24.697 --> 01:29:27.180
"The Price You Pay for College," which is fantastic.

2096
01:29:27.180 --> 01:29:29.653
He does a lot there about college amenities.

2097
01:29:30.580 --> 01:29:33.360
Big, big ones are wellness dorms

2098
01:29:33.360 --> 01:29:37.270
because they appeal to kids who may not wanna drink

2099
01:29:37.270 --> 01:29:40.590
and who really do wanna get studying done,

2100
01:29:40.590 --> 01:29:42.960
and to parents who are looking for amenities

2101
01:29:42.960 --> 01:29:44.610
that they would really like to pay for.

2102
01:29:44.610 --> 01:29:48.880
And amenities like wellness dorms, where you get rewarded,

2103
01:29:48.880 --> 01:29:51.350
you have to sign contracts that you won't come back drunk

2104
01:29:51.350 --> 01:29:53.700
and that you won't have alcohol in the dorm.

2105
01:29:53.700 --> 01:29:55.720
You get rewards like some places,

2106
01:29:55.720 --> 01:29:57.090
this one place was giving

2107
01:29:57.090 --> 01:29:59.690
the more points you had for wellness categories

2108
01:29:59.690 --> 01:30:01.070
and working out and stuff,

2109
01:30:01.070 --> 01:30:03.650
you could earn towards like Fitbits and things like that.

2110
01:30:03.650 --> 01:30:05.155
Oh, wow.

2111
01:30:05.155 --> 01:30:06.110
That's a cool idea.

2112
01:30:06.110 --> 01:30:08.300
Yeah, and a lot of colleges now have

2113
01:30:08.300 --> 01:30:09.830
sober dorms altogether

2114
01:30:09.830 --> 01:30:13.670
so that if you're a part of the recovery community,

2115
01:30:13.670 --> 01:30:17.383
you can have a sober dorm, which is just fantastic.

2116
01:30:18.260 --> 01:30:19.360
All kinds of resources.

2117
01:30:19.360 --> 01:30:22.610
And I think the more people say,

2118
01:30:22.610 --> 01:30:24.790
yes, that's something I want,

2119
01:30:24.790 --> 01:30:27.440
the more those things will be available

2120
01:30:27.440 --> 01:30:29.410
to college-aged kids.

2121
01:30:29.410 --> 01:30:31.500
I'm just envisioning the conversation

2122
01:30:31.500 --> 01:30:32.820
between the parent and the kid.

2123
01:30:32.820 --> 01:30:35.071
Honey, I've got a great idea.

2124
01:30:35.071 --> 01:30:35.904
I know.

2125
01:30:35.904 --> 01:30:36.737
They've got this wellness dorm.

2126
01:30:36.737 --> 01:30:37.900
It's the sober dorm.

2127
01:30:37.900 --> 01:30:40.114
I really think this is right for you.

2128
01:30:40.114 --> 01:30:40.947
(laughs)

2129
01:30:40.947 --> 01:30:41.780
Here's how you do it.

2130
01:30:41.780 --> 01:30:42.670
Here's how you do it.

2131
01:30:42.670 --> 01:30:44.403
You have that have the best food.

2132
01:30:45.570 --> 01:30:48.840
Like, if your focus is on wellness

2133
01:30:48.840 --> 01:30:50.210
and you make it really clear

2134
01:30:50.210 --> 01:30:52.010
that that's where the freshest fruits and vegetables

2135
01:30:52.010 --> 01:30:54.040
are gonna be, without saying that the fruits and vegetables

2136
01:30:54.040 --> 01:30:57.530
aren't fresh elsewhere, you give them some perks

2137
01:30:57.530 --> 01:30:58.580
to wanna be a part of that.

2138
01:30:58.580 --> 01:31:02.510
And actually some colleges will give them privileges

2139
01:31:02.510 --> 01:31:05.803
that you wouldn't normally get living in other dorms.

2140
01:31:06.710 --> 01:31:09.660
Yeah, I could see a lot of kids like, are you crazy?

2141
01:31:09.660 --> 01:31:11.250
There's no way.

2142
01:31:11.250 --> 01:31:12.590
See, that's the thing, though,

2143
01:31:12.590 --> 01:31:15.640
is that that's your perception of what,

2144
01:31:15.640 --> 01:31:17.560
and what's fascinating is, for me,

2145
01:31:17.560 --> 01:31:19.160
I would've been all in on that.

2146
01:31:19.160 --> 01:31:22.970
And a friend of mine, two of friends' kids

2147
01:31:22.970 --> 01:31:25.200
chose themselves to live in wellness dorms,

2148
01:31:25.200 --> 01:31:29.463
just because they didn't wanna have to give up,

2149
01:31:30.350 --> 01:31:32.120
they didn't wanna be in a sort of situation

2150
01:31:32.120 --> 01:31:35.320
where there was gonna be a lot of booze around all the time.

2151
01:31:35.320 --> 01:31:37.520
Or they wanted it to be quiet.

2152
01:31:37.520 --> 01:31:39.830
And wellness dorms tend to be quieter

2153
01:31:39.830 --> 01:31:42.393
than dorms where there's a lot of drinking.

2154
01:31:43.790 --> 01:31:46.110
I spent a lot of time thinking about

2155
01:31:46.110 --> 01:31:48.720
what I could've learned in college

2156
01:31:48.720 --> 01:31:50.610
had I been sober in college.

2157
01:31:50.610 --> 01:31:54.810
And I have so much admiration and respect for young people

2158
01:31:54.810 --> 01:31:57.698
that get sober at a very young age.

2159
01:31:57.698 --> 01:32:01.883
And the common refrain with that person is,

2160
01:32:02.870 --> 01:32:07.407
like, I'm gonna miss out on all of these experiences,

2161
01:32:07.407 --> 01:32:10.610
and I screwed my life up so early and it's all done.

2162
01:32:10.610 --> 01:32:12.430
And I'm like, you gotta be kidding me.

2163
01:32:12.430 --> 01:32:17.430
Like, if I had been able to get sober at like 18 or 20,

2164
01:32:17.610 --> 01:32:20.270
like your world just opens up to you

2165
01:32:20.270 --> 01:32:23.033
in such a exponential fashion.

2166
01:32:24.155 --> 01:32:25.220
That's the cool thing about Georgia.

2167
01:32:25.220 --> 01:32:26.053
Yeah.
Yeah.

2168
01:32:26.053 --> 01:32:28.300
Georgia and Brian, the two people whose stories

2169
01:32:28.300 --> 01:32:31.200
I really focus on in the book, I mean, they did it.

2170
01:32:31.200 --> 01:32:33.560
They've really pulled their lives together

2171
01:32:33.560 --> 01:32:34.680
at a fairly young age.

2172
01:32:34.680 --> 01:32:37.130
And it's been amazing to watch them.

2173
01:32:37.130 --> 01:32:39.730
I was at a recovery meeting near you actually

2174
01:32:39.730 --> 01:32:42.420
and met someone who was 18.

2175
01:32:42.420 --> 01:32:46.620
It was the Malibu meeting near the high school

2176
01:32:46.620 --> 01:32:48.136
and we couldn't find it--

2177
01:32:48.136 --> 01:32:48.969
Sure.

2178
01:32:48.969 --> 01:32:50.490
'Cause we kept going up to the high school

2179
01:32:50.490 --> 01:32:51.560
and it's not at the high school,

2180
01:32:51.560 --> 01:32:53.490
but that's where the address said it was supposed to be.

2181
01:32:53.490 --> 01:32:55.150
And so this guy, I met this guy

2182
01:32:55.150 --> 01:32:56.680
who was also looking for the same meeting

2183
01:32:56.680 --> 01:32:58.310
and he got sober really young too.

2184
01:32:58.310 --> 01:33:00.347
And I kept looking at him.

2185
01:33:00.347 --> 01:33:01.950
I'm like, how are you doing this?

2186
01:33:01.950 --> 01:33:04.863
He got sober at like 17.

2187
01:33:04.863 --> 01:33:08.240
And he was 26 or something and still sober.

2188
01:33:08.240 --> 01:33:09.830
And I'm thinking, man, you know.

2189
01:33:09.830 --> 01:33:12.300
And he was more in touch with who he was.

2190
01:33:12.300 --> 01:33:14.320
He had coping skills.

2191
01:33:14.320 --> 01:33:16.430
I mean, he was a young adult with coping skills.

2192
01:33:16.430 --> 01:33:18.305
It was just so cool to see.

2193
01:33:18.305 --> 01:33:19.650
It was so cool to see.
Yeah, that's cool.

2194
01:33:19.650 --> 01:33:22.564
I know exactly the meeting that you're talking about.

2195
01:33:22.564 --> 01:33:23.397
(laughs)

2196
01:33:23.397 --> 01:33:24.393
I probably know that person.

2197
01:33:25.460 --> 01:33:26.757
And that's one thing--

2198
01:33:26.757 --> 01:33:27.800
He was visiting from out of town actually,

2199
01:33:27.800 --> 01:33:30.140
which was why we were both so lost.

2200
01:33:30.140 --> 01:33:33.970
Well, one weird thing about meetings in Malibu is that

2201
01:33:33.970 --> 01:33:36.100
they call it like Rehab Riviera.

2202
01:33:36.100 --> 01:33:40.750
There's so many sober living houses and rehabs in that area.

2203
01:33:40.750 --> 01:33:42.590
So that means there's a lot of young people

2204
01:33:42.590 --> 01:33:44.190
who are grappling with sobriety.

2205
01:33:44.190 --> 01:33:47.570
But it's also, it can be very sort of transitory

2206
01:33:47.570 --> 01:33:50.220
'cause people are kind of coming in and coming out.

2207
01:33:50.220 --> 01:33:51.990
This was like an old-timers.

2208
01:33:51.990 --> 01:33:52.823
This was at a church

2209
01:33:52.823 --> 01:33:54.440
and it was right down the street from the high school

2210
01:33:54.440 --> 01:33:56.230
and there were just a lot of old timers there.

2211
01:33:56.230 --> 01:33:59.110
And it wasn't a young meeting at all.

2212
01:33:59.110 --> 01:34:00.350
It was actually a pretty good meeting.

2213
01:34:00.350 --> 01:34:02.231
I was speaking up there somewhere

2214
01:34:02.231 --> 01:34:04.950
and found my way to that meeting.

2215
01:34:04.950 --> 01:34:06.040
Yeah.

2216
01:34:06.040 --> 01:34:11.040
In general, recovery at LA is remarkable for many reasons,

2217
01:34:11.500 --> 01:34:14.040
not the least of which is how many young people

2218
01:34:14.040 --> 01:34:15.780
are kind of in the program.

2219
01:34:15.780 --> 01:34:17.800
Because so many young people come to Los Angeles

2220
01:34:17.800 --> 01:34:20.280
to chase their dream and they get into trouble

2221
01:34:20.280 --> 01:34:24.370
and they, like the cops don't mess around in LA,

2222
01:34:24.370 --> 01:34:28.270
like people are forced to meet their maker kinda right away,

2223
01:34:28.270 --> 01:34:29.950
whereas if they were somewhere else,

2224
01:34:29.950 --> 01:34:32.060
maybe they coulda gotten away with it a little bit longer.

2225
01:34:32.060 --> 01:34:36.300
And so there's this incredibly dynamic, robust community

2226
01:34:36.300 --> 01:34:38.920
of young people who are thriving in sobriety

2227
01:34:38.920 --> 01:34:41.280
and I just find that so inspirational.

2228
01:34:41.280 --> 01:34:44.780
But I think, when I think about Georgia

2229
01:34:44.780 --> 01:34:47.430
and I think about Brian in your book,

2230
01:34:47.430 --> 01:34:50.770
what's instructive perhaps for parents

2231
01:34:50.770 --> 01:34:53.570
that are trying to wrap their heads around this issue

2232
01:34:53.570 --> 01:34:55.720
is the nonlinear nature of all of this.

2233
01:34:55.720 --> 01:34:58.990
Like, these people, there's this idea that,

2234
01:34:58.990 --> 01:35:00.160
look, you got a problem

2235
01:35:00.160 --> 01:35:02.050
and now I'm gonna send you to a treatment center

2236
01:35:02.050 --> 01:35:05.160
or you're gonna go see this person or go to this program

2237
01:35:05.160 --> 01:35:06.550
and all will be well.

2238
01:35:06.550 --> 01:35:08.720
And that works occasionally.

2239
01:35:08.720 --> 01:35:13.280
But for the vast majority of sober people,

2240
01:35:13.280 --> 01:35:15.680
there's a rubber banding effect that takes place

2241
01:35:15.680 --> 01:35:18.960
that could go on for years in some cases

2242
01:35:18.960 --> 01:35:21.510
before recovery really locks in.

2243
01:35:21.510 --> 01:35:22.950
And I think it's instructive,

2244
01:35:22.950 --> 01:35:26.330
and hopefully you can talk a little bit about this,

2245
01:35:26.330 --> 01:35:29.830
for parents out there to kind of understand

2246
01:35:29.830 --> 01:35:33.700
that if somebody's got 30 days or 90 days

2247
01:35:33.700 --> 01:35:37.900
or even a year to not get overly attached to that,

2248
01:35:37.900 --> 01:35:40.350
in the same way you talk about in "The Gift of Failure,"

2249
01:35:40.350 --> 01:35:43.740
like not being attached to grades,

2250
01:35:43.740 --> 01:35:46.530
being more attached to the process of sobriety,

2251
01:35:46.530 --> 01:35:50.730
and there's gonna be some backward steps most likely

2252
01:35:50.730 --> 01:35:53.520
before something really connects

2253
01:35:53.520 --> 01:35:57.960
and you create a stable foundation of sobriety.

2254
01:35:57.960 --> 01:35:58.900
It's funny you say that

2255
01:35:58.900 --> 01:36:01.690
'cause I just realized that right behind me

2256
01:36:01.690 --> 01:36:04.471
I don't, I have, I actually--

2257
01:36:04.471 --> 01:36:06.150
I see a triangle.

2258
01:36:06.150 --> 01:36:07.834
That's my 24-hour chip.

2259
01:36:07.834 --> 01:36:08.800
Oh, cool.

2260
01:36:08.800 --> 01:36:09.633
That's the most important one.

2261
01:36:09.633 --> 01:36:11.240
I don't keep my seven-year chip up.

2262
01:36:11.240 --> 01:36:12.380
It's in a drawer somewhere.

2263
01:36:12.380 --> 01:36:15.540
But the 24-hour one is the one that means the most to me.

2264
01:36:15.540 --> 01:36:17.370
So that's the one that's up on my wall.

2265
01:36:17.370 --> 01:36:19.560
And I think, especially for kids,

2266
01:36:19.560 --> 01:36:21.000
I don't know if you had a chance to see this,

2267
01:36:21.000 --> 01:36:24.577
but there was a show that was made by MTV called

2268
01:36:24.577 --> 01:36:26.450
"16 and in Recovery."

2269
01:36:26.450 --> 01:36:30.672
And it was based at the Northshore Recovery School.

2270
01:36:30.672 --> 01:36:34.810
There is a consortium of an association of recovery schools

2271
01:36:34.810 --> 01:36:35.920
in the United States.

2272
01:36:35.920 --> 01:36:39.730
And this is sort of, I think, what I'm supposed to do next.

2273
01:36:39.730 --> 01:36:42.380
I think I'm supposed to start a recovery high school.

2274
01:36:43.720 --> 01:36:47.720
So anyway, this was based on the Northshore Recovery High

2275
01:36:47.720 --> 01:36:50.180
and it really does an incredible job

2276
01:36:50.180 --> 01:36:53.250
of showing what kids are up against in recovery.

2277
01:36:53.250 --> 01:36:57.570
Because adults can say, cool, okay?

2278
01:36:57.570 --> 01:36:58.950
So in recovery, they say,

2279
01:36:58.950 --> 01:37:00.440
I'm gonna have to get new friends

2280
01:37:00.440 --> 01:37:03.290
and I'm gonna have to sort of change a lot of what I do.

2281
01:37:03.290 --> 01:37:05.700
But then you send a kid back to their family

2282
01:37:05.700 --> 01:37:07.370
and maybe their parents are using

2283
01:37:07.370 --> 01:37:08.790
and their friends are all using

2284
01:37:08.790 --> 01:37:10.180
and they can't pull a geographic

2285
01:37:10.180 --> 01:37:12.140
and they can't do any of these other things

2286
01:37:12.140 --> 01:37:13.610
that adults can do.

2287
01:37:13.610 --> 01:37:16.800
Relapse is very much a part of kids' picture.

2288
01:37:16.800 --> 01:37:19.830
We would see the same kids over and over again at the rehab.

2289
01:37:19.830 --> 01:37:23.390
And it was always such a weird thing.

2290
01:37:23.390 --> 01:37:25.630
Like, someone would come in, walk into the classroom

2291
01:37:25.630 --> 01:37:27.040
and I didn't know they were back.

2292
01:37:27.040 --> 01:37:30.170
And I was so happy to see them and so sad to see them

2293
01:37:30.170 --> 01:37:31.070
all at the same time.

2294
01:37:31.070 --> 01:37:34.190
Because I love them, and I wanna hug them,

2295
01:37:34.190 --> 01:37:35.340
but at the same time they're there

2296
01:37:35.340 --> 01:37:37.630
because they're using again.

2297
01:37:37.630 --> 01:37:42.630
And so I think what parents need to understand

2298
01:37:42.760 --> 01:37:47.760
is that the journey in recovery is definitely,

2299
01:37:48.140 --> 01:37:50.000
definitely not linear with kids.

2300
01:37:50.000 --> 01:37:50.833
It's not.

2301
01:37:51.987 --> 01:37:53.709
Wouldn't that be great if it was?

2302
01:37:53.709 --> 01:37:54.542
I know.

2303
01:37:54.542 --> 01:37:55.870
In "The Gift of Failure," I talk about the development,

2304
01:37:55.870 --> 01:37:57.750
let alone recovery child development's

2305
01:37:57.750 --> 01:37:59.480
not a nice linear slope either.

2306
01:37:59.480 --> 01:38:01.380
It's all over the place like the stock market.

2307
01:38:01.380 --> 01:38:04.130
And what you have to just hope is that you're gonna end up

2308
01:38:04.130 --> 01:38:05.730
up here and not lose your shirt

2309
01:38:05.730 --> 01:38:09.110
and that eventually all of that learning will happen

2310
01:38:09.110 --> 01:38:11.453
and will end up in a better place.

2311
01:38:13.052 --> 01:38:15.593
What are some like simple things

2312
01:38:17.590 --> 01:38:21.633
that you can share, like sort of parenting tips,

2313
01:38:22.530 --> 01:38:24.250
if they start to see their kids

2314
01:38:24.250 --> 01:38:26.127
sliding in the wrong direction?

2315
01:38:27.780 --> 01:38:32.780
So a lot of the tips that I give in the book

2316
01:38:32.900 --> 01:38:34.910
are before that happens.

2317
01:38:34.910 --> 01:38:37.310
Like, how do we not get to the place, hopefully,

2318
01:38:37.310 --> 01:38:40.190
where kids are sliding in the wrong direction?

2319
01:38:40.190 --> 01:38:43.140
But if you do notice that your kid,

2320
01:38:43.140 --> 01:38:44.250
or you're worried that your kid

2321
01:38:44.250 --> 01:38:45.920
is sliding in the wrong direction,

2322
01:38:45.920 --> 01:38:50.160
then you really need to come at those conversations about,

2323
01:38:50.160 --> 01:38:54.330
I'm concerned that something's happening with you.

2324
01:38:54.330 --> 01:38:56.010
There need to be a lot of those I statements.

2325
01:38:56.010 --> 01:38:57.530
In fact, I teach kids

2326
01:38:57.530 --> 01:39:00.010
that are worried that their parents are gonna be defensive

2327
01:39:00.010 --> 01:39:01.940
if they come to them with a question.

2328
01:39:01.940 --> 01:39:06.010
To say, it makes me uncomfortable when you,

2329
01:39:06.010 --> 01:39:09.070
when you do this, I feel this way.

2330
01:39:09.070 --> 01:39:12.010
When this happens, this is how it makes me feel.

2331
01:39:12.010 --> 01:39:14.840
Because it's a lot harder to get defensive

2332
01:39:14.840 --> 01:39:16.260
about how someone feels.

2333
01:39:16.260 --> 01:39:18.570
And so if you were to go to your kid and say,

2334
01:39:18.570 --> 01:39:22.330
look, sweetie, I'm scared, I'm worried.

2335
01:39:22.330 --> 01:39:23.870
I see you looking sad.

2336
01:39:23.870 --> 01:39:25.040
And I don't want you to look sad.

2337
01:39:25.040 --> 01:39:28.850
I want you to feel better about yourself to, you know.

2338
01:39:28.850 --> 01:39:32.630
Whenever we go to kids from that place of confrontation,

2339
01:39:32.630 --> 01:39:33.930
that's just never gonna go well,

2340
01:39:33.930 --> 01:39:36.350
and lecturing really doesn't go well either.

2341
01:39:36.350 --> 01:39:39.350
So going to them from a place of concern as a parent

2342
01:39:39.350 --> 01:39:41.783
is the obvious starting place for that.

2343
01:39:42.690 --> 01:39:44.203
And how do you--
I think--

2344
01:39:44.203 --> 01:39:45.579
Sorry to interject here.

2345
01:39:45.579 --> 01:39:47.073
Yeah, no, no, no.

2346
01:39:47.073 --> 01:39:47.906
But how do you do that

2347
01:39:47.906 --> 01:39:51.550
and avoid the pitfalls of enmeshment?

2348
01:39:51.550 --> 01:39:54.350
Because you don't wanna bring your emotional baggage

2349
01:39:54.350 --> 01:39:57.550
to your kid and like place them in a position

2350
01:39:57.550 --> 01:39:59.200
where they feel like they have to shoulder

2351
01:39:59.200 --> 01:40:01.740
your emotional trauma.

2352
01:40:01.740 --> 01:40:02.853
Yeah, yeah.

2353
01:40:04.816 --> 01:40:08.380
Honestly, I say one of the things you can do is say that.

2354
01:40:08.380 --> 01:40:11.640
I'm trying to untangle my fear because I see you

2355
01:40:11.640 --> 01:40:13.557
possibly going down the same road I went down

2356
01:40:13.557 --> 01:40:16.700
and that makes me scared for myself.

2357
01:40:16.700 --> 01:40:18.810
There is that problem that we have, or we,

2358
01:40:18.810 --> 01:40:22.100
number one, we tend to view their mistakes as our failures

2359
01:40:22.100 --> 01:40:23.940
and that being defensive about that

2360
01:40:23.940 --> 01:40:25.230
just doesn't make any sense either.

2361
01:40:25.230 --> 01:40:26.550
They're not our report card.

2362
01:40:26.550 --> 01:40:30.370
Like, our kids are just not a report card for our parenting.

2363
01:40:30.370 --> 01:40:34.950
Because, frankly, when you were in your lowest place,

2364
01:40:34.950 --> 01:40:36.620
if your parents were taking that part,

2365
01:40:36.620 --> 01:40:40.050
do they get to take, do they need to take the blame

2366
01:40:40.050 --> 01:40:42.870
for you as an F when you were really using it?

2367
01:40:42.870 --> 01:40:45.900
Now do they get an A 'cause you're successful now,

2368
01:40:45.900 --> 01:40:47.150
has something changed?

2369
01:40:47.150 --> 01:40:50.020
I don't think that we can take our kids

2370
01:40:50.020 --> 01:40:53.190
as any sort of an accounting of our parenting

2371
01:40:53.190 --> 01:40:54.760
or a report card for our parenting.

2372
01:40:54.760 --> 01:40:57.410
So we need to disentangle ourselves from that

2373
01:40:57.410 --> 01:40:58.260
for just a little bit.

2374
01:40:58.260 --> 01:41:01.430
And we do that in little ways.

2375
01:41:01.430 --> 01:41:05.580
Like, I told my son when he started looking at colleges,

2376
01:41:05.580 --> 01:41:06.860
that the one thing I wouldn't do

2377
01:41:06.860 --> 01:41:08.440
is put a sticker on the back of my car

2378
01:41:08.440 --> 01:41:11.840
because I didn't wanna turn his very complicated,

2379
01:41:11.840 --> 01:41:14.190
very personal decision about where he went to school.

2380
01:41:14.190 --> 01:41:16.640
I didn't wanna turn that into some bragging point

2381
01:41:16.640 --> 01:41:19.770
that I get when I pull into the school parking lot.

2382
01:41:19.770 --> 01:41:21.280
Just making sure they believe us

2383
01:41:21.280 --> 01:41:23.760
when we say what we care about is them

2384
01:41:23.760 --> 01:41:24.830
and being supportive of them.

2385
01:41:24.830 --> 01:41:26.880
The other thing that really works well

2386
01:41:26.880 --> 01:41:30.600
is to be more focused on the process than the product.

2387
01:41:30.600 --> 01:41:33.640
Because when we tell our kids that what we care about

2388
01:41:33.640 --> 01:41:35.690
is that you're learning or becoming

2389
01:41:35.690 --> 01:41:39.210
or advancing forward, maturing,

2390
01:41:39.210 --> 01:41:42.320
but then what we show them is what we care most about

2391
01:41:42.320 --> 01:41:45.820
is the grade or the honors or the trophy or the whatever,

2392
01:41:45.820 --> 01:41:46.780
then they don't believe us.

2393
01:41:46.780 --> 01:41:49.410
So having more of an emphasis on process over product.

2394
01:41:49.410 --> 01:41:53.210
And who talks about this beautifully actually

2395
01:41:53.210 --> 01:41:56.950
is Lisa Damour in her second book, "Under Pressure."

2396
01:41:56.950 --> 01:41:59.650
She talks about the fact that one of the things we can do

2397
01:41:59.650 --> 01:42:03.590
is help kids, number one, helps kids reframe their stress,

2398
01:42:03.590 --> 01:42:07.140
help them understand, like find a way

2399
01:42:07.140 --> 01:42:10.530
to not fall into that whole, I'm so stressed

2400
01:42:10.530 --> 01:42:13.140
and therefore I'm falling apart kind of trap,

2401
01:42:13.140 --> 01:42:17.140
but helping kids understand that that process

2402
01:42:18.430 --> 01:42:20.020
is the important part.

2403
01:42:20.020 --> 01:42:23.200
And nice thing about what Lisa talks a lot about is that

2404
01:42:24.080 --> 01:42:26.080
when you focus on the process and less on the product,

2405
01:42:26.080 --> 01:42:27.930
it helps diffuse their anxiety

2406
01:42:27.930 --> 01:42:30.430
and it helps diffuse their feelings of,

2407
01:42:30.430 --> 01:42:31.990
yeah, but I didn't get that grade,

2408
01:42:31.990 --> 01:42:34.050
or yeah, but I didn't get those points.

2409
01:42:34.050 --> 01:42:38.030
And that anxiety sort of circle, cycle can go down.

2410
01:42:38.030 --> 01:42:40.730
Well, the parental self-identification piece

2411
01:42:40.730 --> 01:42:44.150
is so important to that, and in general as well.

2412
01:42:44.150 --> 01:42:48.000
Because it's almost hardwired into us as parents

2413
01:42:48.000 --> 01:42:53.000
to be overly emotionally involved

2414
01:42:53.210 --> 01:42:54.790
in the successes and failures

2415
01:42:54.790 --> 01:42:58.340
and pains and high highs and low lows of our children

2416
01:42:58.340 --> 01:43:01.010
and to create distance is what's required

2417
01:43:01.010 --> 01:43:03.092
in order to parent effectively.

2418
01:43:03.092 --> 01:43:06.740
But it's so difficult to do.

2419
01:43:06.740 --> 01:43:09.590
Remember when I said I give kids my email

2420
01:43:09.590 --> 01:43:11.440
so they can email me with their things

2421
01:43:11.440 --> 01:43:12.750
they want their parents to know.

2422
01:43:12.750 --> 01:43:16.640
And I said, the number one thing is some iteration of,

2423
01:43:16.640 --> 01:43:19.270
I don't know who you think your parenting, but it's not me.

2424
01:43:19.270 --> 01:43:21.170
And so the advice I give to parents,

2425
01:43:21.170 --> 01:43:23.210
I think the hardest thing I have to say to parents,

2426
01:43:23.210 --> 01:43:25.247
is we have to love the kids we have

2427
01:43:25.247 --> 01:43:27.890
and not the kids we wish we had.

2428
01:43:27.890 --> 01:43:29.750
That has to be our starting place.

2429
01:43:29.750 --> 01:43:32.890
And we can't just love them based on their performance.

2430
01:43:32.890 --> 01:43:36.470
Because outcome love or performance-based love,

2431
01:43:36.470 --> 01:43:38.970
that is incredibly emotionally damaging.

2432
01:43:38.970 --> 01:43:41.690
And it gets them to the place with us

2433
01:43:41.690 --> 01:43:43.080
where they don't trust us

2434
01:43:43.080 --> 01:43:45.240
when we say that we love you no matter what.

2435
01:43:45.240 --> 01:43:46.360
Yeah, 'cause that's the thing.

2436
01:43:46.360 --> 01:43:51.110
We say that, but then our behavior is pretty demonstrative

2437
01:43:51.110 --> 01:43:54.410
in how we react to the good grade versus the bad grade.

2438
01:43:54.410 --> 01:43:55.675
Yeah, we put it on the refrigerator.

2439
01:43:55.675 --> 01:43:56.567
Right.

2440
01:43:56.567 --> 01:43:58.620
And we are just kinda silent when we don't talk about the,

2441
01:43:58.620 --> 01:44:00.250
and the way to get away from that

2442
01:44:00.250 --> 01:44:02.980
is by focusing more on the process and less on the product.

2443
01:44:02.980 --> 01:44:03.920
Oh, that's interesting

2444
01:44:03.920 --> 01:44:06.060
that you got an A and your friend got an F.

2445
01:44:06.060 --> 01:44:08.130
Well, what did your friend do that you didn't do?

2446
01:44:08.130 --> 01:44:09.770
What did you do that your friend didn't do?

2447
01:44:09.770 --> 01:44:12.600
And what are you gonna do differently next time?

2448
01:44:12.600 --> 01:44:14.850
I got to work on this really cool show with Amazon

2449
01:44:14.850 --> 01:44:16.210
called The Stinky and Dirty Show

2450
01:44:16.210 --> 01:44:17.660
and it's for little, little kids,

2451
01:44:17.660 --> 01:44:20.370
and that whole entire show is these two,

2452
01:44:20.370 --> 01:44:22.730
a whole bunch of different machines

2453
01:44:22.730 --> 01:44:25.280
trying to get a task done and screwing it up

2454
01:44:25.280 --> 01:44:27.040
and not beating themselves up about it

2455
01:44:27.040 --> 01:44:28.660
and being supportive of each other

2456
01:44:28.660 --> 01:44:30.780
and figuring out what to take forward with them

2457
01:44:30.780 --> 01:44:32.230
and what to leave behind,

2458
01:44:32.230 --> 01:44:33.810
and over and over and over again.

2459
01:44:33.810 --> 01:44:34.720
What didn't work?

2460
01:44:34.720 --> 01:44:35.553
What did?

2461
01:44:35.553 --> 01:44:36.386
What do we leave behind?

2462
01:44:36.386 --> 01:44:37.427
What do we take forward with us?

2463
01:44:37.427 --> 01:44:39.770
And we're not gonna just blame someone else

2464
01:44:39.770 --> 01:44:40.690
for making a mistake.

2465
01:44:40.690 --> 01:44:42.640
We're not gonna pretend like the mistake never happened.

2466
01:44:42.640 --> 01:44:44.370
We're gonna figure it out

2467
01:44:44.370 --> 01:44:46.080
and learn something from this iteration.

2468
01:44:46.080 --> 01:44:49.320
And that's that focus on the process and less on the product

2469
01:44:49.320 --> 01:44:53.240
is really what's gonna help us have our kid's trust

2470
01:44:53.240 --> 01:44:54.860
and have them know that we support them

2471
01:44:54.860 --> 01:44:55.693
and that we love them.

2472
01:44:55.693 --> 01:44:58.240
And yes, even if they screw up, we're still gonna love you.

2473
01:44:58.240 --> 01:45:01.590
And so if, for example, I find out that you're doing drugs,

2474
01:45:01.590 --> 01:45:03.520
I'm gonna be here for you,

2475
01:45:03.520 --> 01:45:05.840
but I'm gonna support you in getting better.

2476
01:45:05.840 --> 01:45:08.960
And that my love is not changed

2477
01:45:08.960 --> 01:45:11.190
just because you're screwing up.

2478
01:45:11.190 --> 01:45:13.110
And I'm not gonna take it personally

2479
01:45:13.110 --> 01:45:14.260
just 'cause you're screwing up.

2480
01:45:14.260 --> 01:45:15.770
Right, yeah, that's the trick.

2481
01:45:15.770 --> 01:45:17.310
Like, not taking it personally.

2482
01:45:17.310 --> 01:45:21.490
Like, trying to make sure that the love is unconditional

2483
01:45:21.490 --> 01:45:24.210
and yet the boundary is firm.

2484
01:45:24.210 --> 01:45:28.080
Not indulging in judgment,

2485
01:45:28.080 --> 01:45:29.850
which just makes everything worse.

2486
01:45:29.850 --> 01:45:31.812
Like, it's a sticky wicket.

2487
01:45:31.812 --> 01:45:32.927
It's like a--

2488
01:45:32.927 --> 01:45:34.236
Try writing a book about it.

2489
01:45:34.236 --> 01:45:35.599
Calculus equation.

2490
01:45:35.599 --> 01:45:36.432
Yeah, I can.

2491
01:45:36.432 --> 01:45:37.265
(laughs)

2492
01:45:37.265 --> 01:45:38.098
Try writing a book about it.

2493
01:45:38.098 --> 01:45:41.010
And now, if one of my kids gets addicted,

2494
01:45:41.010 --> 01:45:43.450
basically, I get an F, not just for my parenting

2495
01:45:43.450 --> 01:45:44.483
but for my work.

2496
01:45:47.580 --> 01:45:48.413
So stakes are lower for you.

2497
01:45:48.413 --> 01:45:49.993
What are your kids think about the book?

2498
01:45:51.650 --> 01:45:53.860
Neither one has finished the whole thing yet.

2499
01:45:53.860 --> 01:45:54.693
One--
Really?

2500
01:45:55.890 --> 01:45:58.793
Yeah, they've read bits of it.

2501
01:45:59.840 --> 01:46:01.860
Like I passed the bits of it past them

2502
01:46:01.860 --> 01:46:04.013
that I needed for them to sign off on.

2503
01:46:04.960 --> 01:46:08.340
Ben, my older son, has read the story about Brian,

2504
01:46:08.340 --> 01:46:10.767
because Brian and Ben were really close.

2505
01:46:10.767 --> 01:46:11.600
He was his friend, yeah.
Yep.

2506
01:46:11.600 --> 01:46:16.600
And Finn, I've past it by him all of the parts

2507
01:46:16.600 --> 01:46:18.720
that had anything to do with him.

2508
01:46:18.720 --> 01:46:21.255
But at some point, I guess, they're gonna read it.

2509
01:46:21.255 --> 01:46:22.088
I don't know.

2510
01:46:22.088 --> 01:46:24.450
I thought Ben had read "Gift of Failure."

2511
01:46:24.450 --> 01:46:26.267
He said he only read parts of it.

2512
01:46:26.267 --> 01:46:28.620
And that Finn still hasn't read it.

2513
01:46:28.620 --> 01:46:29.453
They just don't care.

2514
01:46:29.453 --> 01:46:30.530
I'm not very interesting.

2515
01:46:30.530 --> 01:46:31.580
I'm just not that interesting.

2516
01:46:31.580 --> 01:46:32.413
They know they're in it.

2517
01:46:32.413 --> 01:46:34.330
They also know that I ran anything--

2518
01:46:34.330 --> 01:46:35.920
We don't need to read it, we live it.

2519
01:46:35.920 --> 01:46:37.000
We know you.

2520
01:46:37.000 --> 01:46:37.833
Yeah.

2521
01:46:37.833 --> 01:46:40.586
And I also think that they know that they've read the parts

2522
01:46:40.586 --> 01:46:43.190
that are about them.

2523
01:46:43.190 --> 01:46:45.573
And the rest is just embarrassing mom.

2524
01:46:47.530 --> 01:46:48.960
We gotta wind this down in a little bit.

2525
01:46:48.960 --> 01:46:52.120
But I do wanna spend a minute or two

2526
01:46:52.120 --> 01:46:55.263
talking about marijuana specifically.

2527
01:46:56.570 --> 01:46:59.860
How has like the legalization of pot

2528
01:46:59.860 --> 01:47:04.403
like impacted how all this operates with young people?

2529
01:47:05.330 --> 01:47:06.330
Yeah, it's harder.

2530
01:47:07.920 --> 01:47:10.460
The problem is, is we have these stupid hierarchies

2531
01:47:10.460 --> 01:47:12.010
about drugs, like, okay, this one.

2532
01:47:12.010 --> 01:47:15.000
I mean, if everything, all other things being equal,

2533
01:47:15.000 --> 01:47:17.240
alcohol should be illegal, right?

2534
01:47:17.240 --> 01:47:19.730
'Cause in terms of like deaths and illness

2535
01:47:19.730 --> 01:47:21.840
and all that other stuff.
Horrific.

2536
01:47:21.840 --> 01:47:23.340
Yeah.

2537
01:47:23.340 --> 01:47:25.740
But pot is so easy to get now.

2538
01:47:25.740 --> 01:47:28.385
I mean, perhaps delivered to your door.

2539
01:47:28.385 --> 01:47:31.630
Both of my kids told me even before legalization

2540
01:47:31.630 --> 01:47:33.700
that it would've been, it's always easier

2541
01:47:33.700 --> 01:47:34.840
for them to get ahold of pot

2542
01:47:34.840 --> 01:47:36.440
than to get ahold of cigarettes.

2543
01:47:38.010 --> 01:47:39.430
Which was fascinating to me.

2544
01:47:39.430 --> 01:47:41.730
That hadn't occurred to me.

2545
01:47:41.730 --> 01:47:45.068
Anyway, so I live in Vermont, I talked about this,

2546
01:47:45.068 --> 01:47:46.580
I talk about this in the book,

2547
01:47:46.580 --> 01:47:49.280
that not only did I move my kid

2548
01:47:49.280 --> 01:47:51.870
at a really delicate time during his adolescence,

2549
01:47:51.870 --> 01:47:53.553
I moved him to Vermont.

2550
01:47:55.470 --> 01:47:57.970
The only other place where I've smelled quite this much pot

2551
01:47:57.970 --> 01:48:01.163
was last time I was in Portland, I think, or Seattle.

2552
01:48:02.866 --> 01:48:05.440
So that made things a little more complicated.

2553
01:48:05.440 --> 01:48:07.724
There's also the novelty issue.

2554
01:48:07.724 --> 01:48:09.370
I think it was really interesting to see actually

2555
01:48:09.370 --> 01:48:11.183
how much adult use went up.

2556
01:48:12.170 --> 01:48:15.080
In general, over the last decade,

2557
01:48:15.080 --> 01:48:17.820
drug use across the board has been, except for vaping,

2558
01:48:17.820 --> 01:48:20.160
has been going down among adolescents.

2559
01:48:20.160 --> 01:48:22.020
And we're a little worried

2560
01:48:22.020 --> 01:48:24.380
about sort of the downward slope

2561
01:48:24.380 --> 01:48:26.470
has leveled out a little bit.

2562
01:48:26.470 --> 01:48:29.070
I don't know that we quite know what that means yet.

2563
01:48:30.352 --> 01:48:35.010
And drug use is down, in all categories, in adults too,

2564
01:48:35.010 --> 01:48:37.993
except pot and psychedelics.
Right.

2565
01:48:39.150 --> 01:48:40.720
Number one, we have legalization.

2566
01:48:40.720 --> 01:48:43.210
Number two, we have really interesting books

2567
01:48:43.210 --> 01:48:46.130
by Michael Pollan and a bunch of other people talking.

2568
01:48:46.130 --> 01:48:50.410
And we have what looks like some really valid use

2569
01:48:50.410 --> 01:48:53.570
for some drugs for PTSD and end-of-life.

2570
01:48:53.570 --> 01:48:55.170
I think there's some really interesting research

2571
01:48:55.170 --> 01:48:56.013
going on there.

2572
01:48:56.900 --> 01:48:59.360
But across the board, I think it's been interesting

2573
01:48:59.360 --> 01:49:01.180
that it's actually mostly been adults

2574
01:49:01.180 --> 01:49:04.820
who have had a major uptick in there, in the pot use.

2575
01:49:04.820 --> 01:49:07.710
Well, I don't know what it's like in Vermont.

2576
01:49:07.710 --> 01:49:09.570
There's no billboards in Vermont, right?

2577
01:49:09.570 --> 01:49:11.180
But in Los Angeles--
No.

2578
01:49:11.180 --> 01:49:12.618
Or is that changed?

2579
01:49:12.618 --> 01:49:15.490
No, Vermont's not allowed to have billboards.

2580
01:49:15.490 --> 01:49:17.690
We have no billboards here.

2581
01:49:17.690 --> 01:49:20.650
Last time I took my now 17-year-old to New York,

2582
01:49:20.650 --> 01:49:23.210
I had to be in Studio City for something.

2583
01:49:23.210 --> 01:49:24.670
And he was looking around, he said,

2584
01:49:24.670 --> 01:49:27.150
so this is the sushi and pot part of town, huh?

2585
01:49:27.150 --> 01:49:30.098
Right, that's all of Los Angeles, I think.

2586
01:49:30.098 --> 01:49:31.179
(laughs)

2587
01:49:31.179 --> 01:49:32.120
That's the whole part of the town.

2588
01:49:32.120 --> 01:49:37.120
I mean, it's wild to drive around Los Angeles

2589
01:49:37.310 --> 01:49:40.550
and see dispensaries on every corner.

2590
01:49:40.550 --> 01:49:43.030
Some of which look like the Apple store.

2591
01:49:43.030 --> 01:49:47.710
Like, they have sort of created these environments

2592
01:49:47.710 --> 01:49:50.800
and experiences that are,

2593
01:49:50.800 --> 01:49:54.390
it's no longer like a flashing green light

2594
01:49:54.390 --> 01:49:55.750
down a dark alley.

2595
01:49:55.750 --> 01:49:58.150
This is like on the main promenade.

2596
01:49:58.150 --> 01:50:02.030
And billboards that would have you believe

2597
01:50:02.030 --> 01:50:07.030
that pot is just sort of an important part

2598
01:50:07.350 --> 01:50:09.540
of your daily wellness routine.

2599
01:50:09.540 --> 01:50:12.060
And CBD is in everything and all of this stuff.

2600
01:50:12.060 --> 01:50:17.060
And so how does that impact the psyche of a young person?

2601
01:50:17.630 --> 01:50:21.720
Well, the problem is also that, among adolescents,

2602
01:50:21.720 --> 01:50:24.270
so our own sort of, we do have receptors

2603
01:50:24.270 --> 01:50:29.270
for the chemicals that are in pot in our brain

2604
01:50:30.240 --> 01:50:33.450
because it seems like we have these endogenous chemicals

2605
01:50:33.450 --> 01:50:34.800
that do sort of a similar thing,

2606
01:50:34.800 --> 01:50:37.340
although not in the same concentrations obviously.

2607
01:50:37.340 --> 01:50:39.660
And most of those are right around the hippocampus,

2608
01:50:39.660 --> 01:50:43.760
which is where we process and store,

2609
01:50:43.760 --> 01:50:46.280
our memories get formed and stored there

2610
01:50:46.280 --> 01:50:48.570
and especially emotional memory.

2611
01:50:48.570 --> 01:50:51.400
And so some of the,

2612
01:50:51.400 --> 01:50:53.780
there's some research I'm a little skeptical of

2613
01:50:53.780 --> 01:50:58.120
that shows that in heavy pot users who are younger

2614
01:50:58.120 --> 01:51:00.700
that their hippocampi are smaller

2615
01:51:00.700 --> 01:51:03.630
and that could be correlation that could,

2616
01:51:03.630 --> 01:51:05.490
we don't know what that is.

2617
01:51:05.490 --> 01:51:08.190
But there is really good research

2618
01:51:08.190 --> 01:51:10.120
looking at sort of how the brain functions,

2619
01:51:10.120 --> 01:51:12.880
and because now that we have fMRIs and things like that

2620
01:51:12.880 --> 01:51:14.310
we can really sort of actually see

2621
01:51:14.310 --> 01:51:16.810
what's lighting up in the brain as it's happening.

2622
01:51:17.660 --> 01:51:20.380
Memory formation is just not happening in the same way

2623
01:51:20.380 --> 01:51:24.300
in kids who smoke pot.

2624
01:51:24.300 --> 01:51:28.880
And some of that appears to be permanent.

2625
01:51:28.880 --> 01:51:33.350
The stunting, the hippocampus appears

2626
01:51:33.350 --> 01:51:36.327
to possibly have long-term ramifications.

2627
01:51:36.327 --> 01:51:38.033
And that really scares me.

2628
01:51:38.970 --> 01:51:41.090
So pot, interestingly enough,

2629
01:51:41.090 --> 01:51:43.970
pot, like for adults, have at it, go at it.

2630
01:51:43.970 --> 01:51:45.590
But for kids, because in adult,

2631
01:51:45.590 --> 01:51:48.834
what's so interesting is after the brain is done growing,

2632
01:51:48.834 --> 01:51:53.330
those problems don't seem to be as much of an issue.

2633
01:51:53.330 --> 01:51:55.490
It's just when the brain is in this acute,

2634
01:51:55.490 --> 01:51:59.040
incredibly plastic place that the environment affects it

2635
01:51:59.040 --> 01:52:01.010
in ways that it wouldn't normally.

2636
01:52:01.010 --> 01:52:04.210
And so that's why this message of delay, delay, delay.

2637
01:52:04.210 --> 01:52:05.760
With each passing year,

2638
01:52:05.760 --> 01:52:07.900
our kids risk of having substance use disorder

2639
01:52:07.900 --> 01:52:09.350
during their lifetime goes down.

2640
01:52:09.350 --> 01:52:11.150
And with each passing year,

2641
01:52:11.150 --> 01:52:14.640
the risks that these things do to their brain goes down.

2642
01:52:14.640 --> 01:52:17.300
So delay, delay, delay is sort of the message

2643
01:52:17.300 --> 01:52:18.480
of this whole entire book.

2644
01:52:18.480 --> 01:52:19.810
And here's the information

2645
01:52:19.810 --> 01:52:22.263
about why exactly it's important to delay.

2646
01:52:23.159 --> 01:52:26.780
And that rubicon is sort of around like 25, right?

2647
01:52:26.780 --> 01:52:30.102
Like, it's not 16.
Yeah, it depends.

2648
01:52:30.102 --> 01:52:31.120
It is not 16, no.

2649
01:52:31.120 --> 01:52:33.574
Early mid to mid 20s actually.

2650
01:52:33.574 --> 01:52:34.407
24, 25.

2651
01:52:34.407 --> 01:52:37.410
But that also depends on the kid.

2652
01:52:37.410 --> 01:52:38.900
And you can't force that stuff.

2653
01:52:38.900 --> 01:52:41.400
I mean, these are things that,

2654
01:52:41.400 --> 01:52:43.600
myelination and synaptogenesis,

2655
01:52:43.600 --> 01:52:45.520
and hooking up, wiring up the frontal lobe

2656
01:52:45.520 --> 01:52:47.890
to the rest of the brain, that's not stuff you can rush.

2657
01:52:47.890 --> 01:52:51.020
And the thing that most people are worried about is

2658
01:52:51.870 --> 01:52:53.840
if you don't have your full,

2659
01:52:53.840 --> 01:52:55.597
the full ability for everything to hook up

2660
01:52:55.597 --> 01:52:56.777
and everything to get myelinated

2661
01:52:56.777 --> 01:52:59.170
and the synapses to talk to each other in the brain

2662
01:52:59.170 --> 01:53:02.420
during this period, you don't get to go back.

2663
01:53:02.420 --> 01:53:05.330
It shuts that door, closes in the early 20s,

2664
01:53:05.330 --> 01:53:06.960
early to mid 20s.

2665
01:53:06.960 --> 01:53:08.410
So the damage is done.

2666
01:53:08.410 --> 01:53:11.280
As the damage done and there's no going back to fix that.

2667
01:53:11.280 --> 01:53:13.950
And do you find that when you communicate that

2668
01:53:13.950 --> 01:53:15.780
to a young person that that lands,

2669
01:53:15.780 --> 01:53:18.640
like they're able to hear that or is that just dismissed?

2670
01:53:18.640 --> 01:53:22.380
Like, my experience is gonna be different or I don't care.

2671
01:53:22.380 --> 01:53:25.086
There's an article I wrote back when I had a column,

2672
01:53:25.086 --> 01:53:26.690
I had a column in The New York Times

2673
01:53:26.690 --> 01:53:28.020
called the Parent-Teacher Conference

2674
01:53:28.020 --> 01:53:31.760
and I wrote, one column was I was sitting around

2675
01:53:31.760 --> 01:53:34.420
at the rehab one time and we had a full group

2676
01:53:34.420 --> 01:53:36.810
of really honest kids, really forthright kids.

2677
01:53:36.810 --> 01:53:41.010
And so I asked them, what in your most open

2678
01:53:41.010 --> 01:53:45.600
and receptive moment might you have heard from adults

2679
01:53:45.600 --> 01:53:47.790
that might've made you think differently?

2680
01:53:47.790 --> 01:53:49.270
And I said, I don't want you to tell me

2681
01:53:49.270 --> 01:53:52.800
what you think I wanna hear, or whatever.

2682
01:53:52.800 --> 01:53:55.180
And these were kids that pretty, they trusted me.

2683
01:53:55.180 --> 01:53:57.276
We'd worked together for awhile.

2684
01:53:57.276 --> 01:54:00.830
And for all of them, it was honest information.

2685
01:54:00.830 --> 01:54:03.300
Give us the information we need to make a smart decision

2686
01:54:03.300 --> 01:54:05.290
and then trust us to make good decisions

2687
01:54:05.290 --> 01:54:07.780
given that we have, we're still growing

2688
01:54:07.780 --> 01:54:09.750
and we're not always gonna make the right decisions,

2689
01:54:09.750 --> 01:54:12.960
but at least have some faith that when you tell us stuff

2690
01:54:12.960 --> 01:54:16.240
we can process that and make some decisions for ourselves.

2691
01:54:16.240 --> 01:54:19.650
So no, they might not have made different decisions,

2692
01:54:19.650 --> 01:54:23.190
but they all said, respect me enough

2693
01:54:23.190 --> 01:54:24.690
to give me the information,

2694
01:54:24.690 --> 01:54:28.370
respect me enough to to know that given some information

2695
01:54:28.370 --> 01:54:31.690
I can weigh, and here's the other problem

2696
01:54:31.690 --> 01:54:33.980
I'm gonna mention, is that when you say,

2697
01:54:33.980 --> 01:54:36.220
oh my gosh, how could you have done this?

2698
01:54:36.220 --> 01:54:38.650
How could you not have known that if you

2699
01:54:38.650 --> 01:54:39.750
got in a car with a drunk driver,

2700
01:54:39.750 --> 01:54:41.100
they were gonna hit a tree?

2701
01:54:41.970 --> 01:54:43.823
And the understanding seems to be

2702
01:54:43.823 --> 01:54:46.380
that kids can't weigh risk correctly

2703
01:54:46.380 --> 01:54:48.210
or that they don't understand consequences

2704
01:54:48.210 --> 01:54:51.030
and that is absolutely 100% not true.

2705
01:54:51.030 --> 01:54:53.210
Kids really do see consequences.

2706
01:54:53.210 --> 01:54:56.230
The problem is, is they weigh the potential benefits

2707
01:54:56.230 --> 01:54:58.100
to their actions more heavily

2708
01:54:58.100 --> 01:54:59.910
than they weigh the possible risks.

2709
01:54:59.910 --> 01:55:02.808
And when you get kids in groups,

2710
01:55:02.808 --> 01:55:07.060
they are more likely to take risk, which explains a lot.

2711
01:55:07.060 --> 01:55:09.080
And sometimes it's even if they're in groups

2712
01:55:09.080 --> 01:55:10.950
but they're not in the same room together.

2713
01:55:10.950 --> 01:55:13.160
If a kid just believes other kids are watching them,

2714
01:55:13.160 --> 01:55:15.770
they'll take way more risks, which explains a lot

2715
01:55:15.770 --> 01:55:18.820
about like TikTok videos, I suppose,

2716
01:55:18.820 --> 01:55:20.720
and people jumping off of high things.

2717
01:55:22.470 --> 01:55:24.480
And that doesn't happen in adults.

2718
01:55:24.480 --> 01:55:26.820
If I put you in a room with some of your friends

2719
01:55:26.820 --> 01:55:28.750
and have you play a video game

2720
01:55:28.750 --> 01:55:30.960
that asks you whether or not to make these decisions

2721
01:55:30.960 --> 01:55:32.370
that sort of gauge your risk,

2722
01:55:32.370 --> 01:55:34.490
you won't take any more chances

2723
01:55:34.490 --> 01:55:35.810
based on the fact that your friends are there,

2724
01:55:35.810 --> 01:55:37.505
but kids will.

2725
01:55:37.505 --> 01:55:39.000
They have a different calculus.

2726
01:55:39.000 --> 01:55:41.180
Sure, the calibration is different

2727
01:55:41.180 --> 01:55:44.520
because they're gonna over-index on social approval.

2728
01:55:44.520 --> 01:55:47.063
Because that feels like life or death to them.

2729
01:55:48.124 --> 01:55:48.957
And what feels good, right, yeah.

2730
01:55:48.957 --> 01:55:53.170
Right, to be ostracized or to be included is survival.

2731
01:55:53.170 --> 01:55:56.730
And so that's gonna weigh disproportionately

2732
01:55:56.730 --> 01:55:59.460
into the decision to incur a risk.

2733
01:55:59.460 --> 01:56:02.730
And that's why the chapter is called Wired for Risk

2734
01:56:02.730 --> 01:56:05.573
because they are wired to, for novelty,

2735
01:56:06.830 --> 01:56:09.230
all the things that they're naturally supposed to be doing

2736
01:56:09.230 --> 01:56:12.070
as they pull away from us and become their own adults.

2737
01:56:12.070 --> 01:56:14.610
We want them to wanna take some positive risks.

2738
01:56:14.610 --> 01:56:17.380
We want them to wanna go out there and seek novelty

2739
01:56:17.380 --> 01:56:20.400
because that's what's gonna make them be successful adults.

2740
01:56:20.400 --> 01:56:22.280
But the question is, how we moderate that

2741
01:56:22.280 --> 01:56:25.490
and how we present, if you have a kid who really loves risk,

2742
01:56:25.490 --> 01:56:27.260
you better be looking for as many ways

2743
01:56:27.260 --> 01:56:30.740
to introduce positive risk into that kid's life as possible.

2744
01:56:30.740 --> 01:56:32.810
Get them to do things that, yes, are risky

2745
01:56:32.810 --> 01:56:34.890
but risky in a direction that isn't

2746
01:56:34.890 --> 01:56:38.023
like them leaping off of the garage into a swimming pool.

2747
01:56:39.359 --> 01:56:43.100
And how does that risk calculus,

2748
01:56:43.100 --> 01:56:45.413
does that differentiate based on gender?

2749
01:56:46.960 --> 01:56:51.200
Yeah, I haven't read a really good study on this,

2750
01:56:51.200 --> 01:56:52.930
and that may just be my oversight.

2751
01:56:52.930 --> 01:56:56.350
But all of the stuff I've read

2752
01:56:56.350 --> 01:57:01.350
has been equally female/male,

2753
01:57:01.530 --> 01:57:03.910
like all of that stuff I was just talking about with risk,

2754
01:57:03.910 --> 01:57:06.590
all of his research, this is Laurence Steinberg

2755
01:57:06.590 --> 01:57:07.700
at Temple University.

2756
01:57:07.700 --> 01:57:10.770
I think all of his studies are evenly female,

2757
01:57:10.770 --> 01:57:14.190
male/female, 50/50.

2758
01:57:15.370 --> 01:57:18.070
I do know that in, from an education perspective,

2759
01:57:18.070 --> 01:57:20.680
I've done a lot of work researching boys in education,

2760
01:57:20.680 --> 01:57:22.620
and I will tell you that I did,

2761
01:57:22.620 --> 01:57:24.170
I wrote an article for The Atlantic

2762
01:57:24.170 --> 01:57:28.650
on the importance of touch, of normal social touch.

2763
01:57:28.650 --> 01:57:30.290
And one of the things we do know

2764
01:57:30.290 --> 01:57:33.920
is that boys tend to get their fuller,

2765
01:57:33.920 --> 01:57:37.080
they get more social touch opportunities

2766
01:57:37.080 --> 01:57:39.630
to experience social touch at all boys schools

2767
01:57:39.630 --> 01:57:40.580
because the teachers,

2768
01:57:40.580 --> 01:57:42.810
and especially when they're taught by men,

2769
01:57:42.810 --> 01:57:45.340
because men understand the need

2770
01:57:45.340 --> 01:57:47.100
for the roughhousing sort of thing,

2771
01:57:47.100 --> 01:57:50.650
whereas when they're in a co-ed environment

2772
01:57:50.650 --> 01:57:51.840
or they're being taught by women.

2773
01:57:51.840 --> 01:57:54.190
The women are like, oh, don't hit each other,

2774
01:57:54.190 --> 01:57:56.730
don't punch each other on the shoulder that kinda thing.

2775
01:57:56.730 --> 01:57:58.584
But yeah, I haven't read a lot of stuff--

2776
01:57:58.584 --> 01:57:59.704
That's interesting.

2777
01:57:59.704 --> 01:58:00.650
My instinct--
But there is stuff--

2778
01:58:00.650 --> 01:58:03.350
My instinct says that probably boys

2779
01:58:03.350 --> 01:58:04.570
are wired to take more risk,

2780
01:58:04.570 --> 01:58:05.807
but I'm gonna have to go look it up.

2781
01:58:05.807 --> 01:58:08.300
And there's actually a brand new book out

2782
01:58:08.300 --> 01:58:10.940
that's over on my shelf over there somewhere

2783
01:58:10.940 --> 01:58:14.870
called "To Raise A Boy" by Emma Brown

2784
01:58:14.870 --> 01:58:17.453
that I am just itching to open up,

2785
01:58:17.453 --> 01:58:18.740
because we need some good books on boys

2786
01:58:18.740 --> 01:58:20.117
and that kinda stuff.

2787
01:58:20.117 --> 01:58:22.040
But there is lots of stuff in your book

2788
01:58:22.040 --> 01:58:24.530
about gender differentials

2789
01:58:24.530 --> 01:58:27.560
with respect to substance use and abuse, right?

2790
01:58:27.560 --> 01:58:31.410
Like, girls relationships with alcohol versus boys

2791
01:58:31.410 --> 01:58:34.430
and how that changes depending upon age, et cetera.

2792
01:58:34.430 --> 01:58:36.407
So there are some--

2793
01:58:36.407 --> 01:58:37.492
Not just attitudes either.

2794
01:58:37.492 --> 01:58:39.790
I mean, girls have less of a particular enzyme

2795
01:58:39.790 --> 01:58:41.070
that breaks alcohol down.

2796
01:58:41.070 --> 01:58:43.050
And so given the fact that,

2797
01:58:43.050 --> 01:58:45.450
we know boys' bodies have more water in them

2798
01:58:45.450 --> 01:58:46.980
and girls' bodies have more fat in them

2799
01:58:46.980 --> 01:58:48.880
and therefore a girl can have fewer drinks

2800
01:58:48.880 --> 01:58:52.070
and be as drunk as a boy that has, whatever.

2801
01:58:52.070 --> 01:58:55.170
But also, girls are just now metabolizing the alcohol

2802
01:58:55.170 --> 01:58:56.270
the same way the boys are.

2803
01:58:56.270 --> 01:59:00.340
And so girls are getting a lot more of that effect going on.

2804
01:59:00.340 --> 01:59:03.403
And girls tend to drink for different reasons.

2805
01:59:04.450 --> 01:59:07.373
Girls tend to drink to deal with their anxiety.

2806
01:59:09.273 --> 01:59:12.460
There are patterns that will show up.

2807
01:59:12.460 --> 01:59:14.840
And what I hoped to do with this book

2808
01:59:14.840 --> 01:59:17.610
was show you all the patterns that have been out there

2809
01:59:17.610 --> 01:59:18.443
and proven themselves.

2810
01:59:18.443 --> 01:59:21.920
So you see a pattern that you recognize in your kid,

2811
01:59:21.920 --> 01:59:23.430
you can say, ah, okay,

2812
01:59:23.430 --> 01:59:25.970
so that's a conversation I could have.

2813
01:59:25.970 --> 01:59:28.540
All of these bits and pieces of information

2814
01:59:28.540 --> 01:59:31.250
about risk factors and about how a kid might react

2815
01:59:31.250 --> 01:59:33.270
those are all just so you can say,

2816
01:59:33.270 --> 01:59:34.710
okay, I recognize that in my kid,

2817
01:59:34.710 --> 01:59:36.280
or I don't recognize that in my kid.

2818
01:59:36.280 --> 01:59:38.137
And if so, that's information for me.

2819
01:59:38.137 --> 01:59:39.590
And that information is power

2820
01:59:39.590 --> 01:59:42.750
because now I have a starting place for our conversation.

2821
01:59:42.750 --> 01:59:45.410
I wanna end this with just some thoughts

2822
01:59:45.410 --> 01:59:50.260
on the non-discriminatory nature

2823
01:59:50.260 --> 01:59:53.080
of substance abuse and addiction.

2824
01:59:53.080 --> 01:59:54.830
If anybody is under the impression

2825
01:59:54.830 --> 01:59:57.360
that their parenting skills

2826
01:59:57.360 --> 02:00:02.170
are going to completely inoculate their child from any risk,

2827
02:00:02.170 --> 02:00:04.837
let me disabuse you of that notion right now.

2828
02:00:06.160 --> 02:00:07.650
Addiction does not discriminate.

2829
02:00:07.650 --> 02:00:09.900
It doesn't matter your ethnicity

2830
02:00:09.900 --> 02:00:11.930
or your socioeconomic class.

2831
02:00:11.930 --> 02:00:16.010
So maybe let's end this with not a cautionary tale,

2832
02:00:16.010 --> 02:00:17.760
but maybe a reality check

2833
02:00:17.760 --> 02:00:20.530
because we are dealing with a situation

2834
02:00:20.530 --> 02:00:23.430
in which addiction rates in teens,

2835
02:00:23.430 --> 02:00:27.300
I'm sure in the pandemic, are going through the roof.

2836
02:00:27.300 --> 02:00:29.820
And you quote the statistic that nine out of 10 adults

2837
02:00:29.820 --> 02:00:32.470
with substance use disorder report

2838
02:00:32.470 --> 02:00:35.930
they began drinking and taking drugs before age 18.

2839
02:00:35.930 --> 02:00:38.870
So this is a very real situation

2840
02:00:38.870 --> 02:00:42.310
with incredibly profound consequences

2841
02:00:42.310 --> 02:00:45.250
for young people and parents,

2842
02:00:45.250 --> 02:00:47.930
regardless of who you think you are.

2843
02:00:47.930 --> 02:00:52.930
So maybe round us out with just a takeaway on that,

2844
02:00:52.980 --> 02:00:54.240
on that subject.

2845
02:00:54.240 --> 02:00:55.980
I think there's so much potential

2846
02:00:55.980 --> 02:00:58.080
that's just out there waiting for us to grab it.

2847
02:00:58.080 --> 02:01:01.880
And a big part of that is these school programs

2848
02:01:01.880 --> 02:01:04.950
that work really well with parents and schools,

2849
02:01:04.950 --> 02:01:06.310
especially when they're working together.

2850
02:01:06.310 --> 02:01:08.210
A lot of the really, really good

2851
02:01:09.160 --> 02:01:14.160
school-based substance abuse prevention programs have,

2852
02:01:14.300 --> 02:01:16.330
the full curriculum is not just for the school,

2853
02:01:16.330 --> 02:01:18.130
but for the parents as well.

2854
02:01:18.130 --> 02:01:20.280
And there's such an opportunity

2855
02:01:20.280 --> 02:01:23.510
because so few schools are using evidence-based programs

2856
02:01:23.510 --> 02:01:26.370
and there are a bunch out there that are really good

2857
02:01:26.370 --> 02:01:28.180
and have been shown to work.

2858
02:01:28.180 --> 02:01:31.210
If we could get those, like if I could take any,

2859
02:01:31.210 --> 02:01:32.780
make any one thing happen,

2860
02:01:32.780 --> 02:01:35.580
it would be that we get those programs into schools

2861
02:01:35.580 --> 02:01:40.290
so that at the very least we're starting there

2862
02:01:40.290 --> 02:01:41.880
where we have a huge deficit

2863
02:01:41.880 --> 02:01:44.960
and that parents can share in those materials

2864
02:01:44.960 --> 02:01:47.680
because so much of the stuff that works in schools

2865
02:01:47.680 --> 02:01:49.370
there are components that work at home

2866
02:01:49.370 --> 02:01:51.971
and we could all sort of be on the same page about this.

2867
02:01:51.971 --> 02:01:55.290
But I guess if I get to be queen of the world,

2868
02:01:55.290 --> 02:01:57.940
it's that everybody gets early intervention

2869
02:01:57.940 --> 02:02:02.940
for their ACEs, their trauma, their learning deficits

2870
02:02:03.850 --> 02:02:04.910
all of that stuff.

2871
02:02:04.910 --> 02:02:06.773
That's my queen of the world wish.

2872
02:02:07.810 --> 02:02:12.350
Yeah, and I think I would also offer to any parent

2873
02:02:12.350 --> 02:02:15.360
who's contending with this or has a child

2874
02:02:15.360 --> 02:02:19.530
who's dabbling or dealing with an issue right now,

2875
02:02:19.530 --> 02:02:21.620
to the extent that you can get on top of it,

2876
02:02:21.620 --> 02:02:23.580
it's such an unbelievable opportunity

2877
02:02:23.580 --> 02:02:25.430
for a young person to start grappling

2878
02:02:25.430 --> 02:02:26.730
with their interior life,

2879
02:02:26.730 --> 02:02:29.930
to confront their trauma and their anxiety

2880
02:02:29.930 --> 02:02:33.240
and develop like a fluency and a language

2881
02:02:33.240 --> 02:02:35.960
for how to deal with challenging emotions.

2882
02:02:35.960 --> 02:02:38.740
Like, this is something that I knew nothing about

2883
02:02:38.740 --> 02:02:41.540
until I was a middle-aged man.

2884
02:02:41.540 --> 02:02:44.450
And the idea that a teenager

2885
02:02:44.450 --> 02:02:46.900
could start contending with this

2886
02:02:46.900 --> 02:02:51.040
is such a healthy thing and such a beautiful opportunity

2887
02:02:51.040 --> 02:02:55.270
for how they're gonna kind of mature into the world.

2888
02:02:55.270 --> 02:02:56.730
There are aspects of recovery.

2889
02:02:56.730 --> 02:02:58.110
I look at some of the,

2890
02:02:58.110 --> 02:03:00.263
there's one meeting in particular that,

2891
02:03:01.570 --> 02:03:04.150
it's a dual speaker meeting, two speakers in one night,

2892
02:03:04.150 --> 02:03:08.840
and inevitably there's some like biker guy in his 60s

2893
02:03:08.840 --> 02:03:12.110
who gets up there and tells this story and reveals,

2894
02:03:12.110 --> 02:03:14.950
says things like, I just didn't have any humility

2895
02:03:14.950 --> 02:03:18.130
or I didn't know how to reach out and ask for help

2896
02:03:18.130 --> 02:03:20.750
and no one had ever told me that that was okay.

2897
02:03:20.750 --> 02:03:22.380
And those are the tools

2898
02:03:22.380 --> 02:03:24.680
that I wish we could start giving kids younger

2899
02:03:24.680 --> 02:03:27.030
so that we don't have to have these people

2900
02:03:27.030 --> 02:03:29.330
come up to a podium and say,

2901
02:03:29.330 --> 02:03:32.420
I just never, ever knew how to turn to someone and say,

2902
02:03:32.420 --> 02:03:33.253
I need help.

2903
02:03:33.253 --> 02:03:36.140
Imagine having to get to 60 before you realize

2904
02:03:36.140 --> 02:03:39.420
that you're allowed to be sad about things and cry

2905
02:03:39.420 --> 02:03:44.070
and ask people for help or realize even what humility is.

2906
02:03:44.070 --> 02:03:46.770
I think there are aspects to going through recovery

2907
02:03:46.770 --> 02:03:49.010
that some of the kids I've seen

2908
02:03:49.010 --> 02:03:51.570
who have successfully come out the other side, other side

2909
02:03:51.570 --> 02:03:52.740
have incredible skills.

2910
02:03:52.740 --> 02:03:54.067
And there's no--

2911
02:03:55.290 --> 02:03:56.778
And they become incredible human beings

2912
02:03:56.778 --> 02:03:58.370
with so much to offer.
It's no coincidence.

2913
02:03:58.370 --> 02:04:00.590
Well, it's no coincidence, spoiler alert,

2914
02:04:00.590 --> 02:04:03.020
that really good substance abuse prevention programs

2915
02:04:03.020 --> 02:04:06.447
are good social, emotional learning programs.

2916
02:04:06.447 --> 02:04:08.060
That's at the heart of them.

2917
02:04:08.060 --> 02:04:08.893
That's what they are.

2918
02:04:08.893 --> 02:04:11.660
And they give us the tools to know how to deal,

2919
02:04:11.660 --> 02:04:14.160
to name and tame our emotions and that kinda thing.

2920
02:04:14.160 --> 02:04:15.170
Right.

2921
02:04:15.170 --> 02:04:18.108
Well, so good to talk to you, Jessica.

2922
02:04:18.108 --> 02:04:20.370
I love talking to you.
I really appreciate it.

2923
02:04:20.370 --> 02:04:22.230
This book, as I said at the outset,

2924
02:04:22.230 --> 02:04:25.090
I think is going to help a lot of people.

2925
02:04:25.090 --> 02:04:26.540
It's a gift to the planet.
I hope so.

2926
